eadless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\nScope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:01:08\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\nTesting Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"6d29b129-7c62-43af-ac82-b1e517da0404"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a large game-building task.\n\nSTAGE.md instructs building the game described in PLAN.md. TASK.md specifies the file names and deliverables. The implementer must finish all local work in PLAN.md and place all required items from TASK.md into this folder.\n\nPROGRESS.md will track progress for resumption after context compaction. TASK.md is the brief and PLAN.md is the detailed plan. The goal is a code-change task building an original Wii Fit-inspired game. The implementer must specify outcomes rather than architecture, module layouts, or exact signatures. OBJECTIVE's explicit words take precedence over any conflicting guidance.\n\nThe acceptance criteria must be outcome-based. They should cover the defining deliverables from TASK.md and PLAN.md without prescribing module layout or implementation details unless the brief explicitly requires them. TASK.md mandates a subfolder named `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108]` for all development files.\n\nThe deliverables also include a single HTML file in the root folder, PROGRESS.md, and exit-interview.json. Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback must appear in the chat reply and the in-game About/Credits screen. The game title is not yet fixed; the implementer will randomly select one from fifty brainstormed options.\n\nThe implementer will select the game title. The HTML filename uses bracketed placeholders for the title and source name.\n\nThe HTML filename will follow the exact placeholder pattern from TASK.md, substituting the chosen title and \"Wii Fit\" for the bracketed tokens. PLAN.md specifies a comprehensive test harness at `node tests/run.mjs` that performs scripted playthroughs, browser tests, and hub tests.\n\nVerification should gate on the test harness at `node tests/run.mjs` because it is required by the objective and capturable. Visual or interactive outcomes cannot be criteria; instead, checks must anchor on artifact existence, exercised logic units, and provably loading browser scripts via plain file:// references.\n\nThe entry-point launch check probes a headless browser, serves the page, asserts zero errors, confirms the render surface is substantially filled, validates that driven input produces visible change, and captures a screenshot. The acceptance criteria should be small—aiming for three to five grouped outcomes that reflect the game's completion rather than its architecture.\n\nThe game is an original inspired by Wii Fit, not a recreation. PLAN.md already defines the core mechanics, so the plan itself serves as the specification. I will not research Wii Fit canon or add extra defining mechanics beyond what PLAN.md requires.\n\nThe first acceptance criterion requires the single HTML file, a matching subfolder for development files, exit-interview.json with the exact expected shape, PROGRESS.md, and the About/Credits screen to display the same source, testing, engine, and prompt fields as the JSON. The game title must be chosen by the RNG method and shown on the title screen.\n\nThe second criterion requires the core game loop to be fully playable. The Lean mechanic must drive all activities. The five-day story must progress through Body Check, the island, three sessions per day, all twelve activities, Gentle Games, Grand Stillness, the ending, and free play. Spine Age, minutes, and saves must function. Scripted playthroughs must reach the ending. Original content must avoid Wii, Mii, or Balance Board names.\n\nThe third criterion covers the presentation and UX contract. The interface must be controller-first with native keyboard and mouse support, prompt placement must follow the last device, and PS glyphs should default while offering an Xbox option. Touch-only devices must display a controller-required message and settings screen instead of touch controls. After ten seconds of idle time, an attract mode loops through activities and exits on any input. All audio must be generated by code using animalese voices and music at ten percent default. Engine-native pixel art must use new source-feeling assets. A dev mode must be reachable from pause with live sliders and a reset option.\n\nThe fourth criterion requires multiplayer support: local classes for two to four players and an AI Hub ghost session that passes the hub harness at the five specified scenarios. The game must also run standalone to the title screen.\n\nThe fifth criterion requires the test harness at `node tests/run.mjs` to print only failures and a count. It must cover lean bands, activity star bands, full playthrough, saves, attract mode, dev mode, class logic, hub logic, and data lint. Browser and hub groups must pass green. No TODOs, placeholders, or stubs may remain.\n\nThe plan must avoid prescribing module or file layout. TASK.md and PLAN.md already define the structure, so the implementer reads them directly. The criteria should describe observable outcomes that PLAN.md requires, not exact filenames or directory trees. This prevents the frozen plan from contradicting a working alternative.\n\nThe brief explicitly names deliverables such as the HTML filename, development subfolder, exit-interview.json, and PROGRESS.md. These are outcomes to verify, not architecture to dictate. Internal file paths like game/balance.js remain PLAN.md's implementation details.\n\nFile layout and internal module organization belong in the implementation approach, not in acceptance criteria. The plan must group only player-visible and test-visible outcomes from PLAN.md. Anything that cannot be done on this computer is excluded from the done condition.\n\nAll hardware requirements from PLAN.md can be satisfied with stand-in emulators. Physical controllers, touch devices, second players, real iPhones, TVs, and displays are replaced by Playwright profiles, browser pages, AnalyserNode, WebKit iPhone emulation, 1920×1080 screenshots, and xvfb. PLAN.md section 16 already uses these same stand-ins. Therefore every deliverable in the plan is testable on this computer.\n\nThe chat reply is not a verifiable criterion because file-based verifiers cannot audit it. The four Source Information fields must instead be checked in the exit-interview.json and the in-game About screen. The implementer's terminal response is treated as the hand-in record.\n\nThe title must be a playful riff on Wii Fit that never uses the words \"Wii Fit\" itself. The implementer will silently run a real random number generator to pick one from fifty brainstormed options, and the verifier cannot audit that process. The title screen and the HTML filename must both reflect the chosen title.\n\nThe implementer must select a concrete Game ID to replace the PLAN.md placeholder. FPS targets, optimization, real Wii Fit recreation, BMI mechanics, body-size jokes, copied names, WebGPU, audio files, hub chat or emotes, physical controllers, real iOS, real TV, touch controls, World3D, and external libraries are explicitly out of scope. The objective allows cuts if the plan exceeds available room.\n\nThe full game is the target. Cuts are an implementer escape for extreme time or space limits, not a passable outcome. The acceptance criteria must require the complete feature set.\n\nThe filename template uses \"Wii Fit\" as the SOURCE segment, so the HTML file will literally contain a space. The in-game About and Credits screens must display the four Source Information fields in the brief's exact order, with \"unknown\" only where unconfirmed. The game must open from a local `file://` URL without a silent black screen.\n\nThe goal is a recognisable wellness-retreat demo. The story path runs from title through member card, arrival, instructor pick, and the five-day loop: bungalow, body check in ducks instead of weight or BMI, island hub, three sessions, evening, bed. It ends with Gentle Games, Grand Stillness, credits, and free play.\n\nAll twelve activities must be playable with card, demo, countdown, play, stars, and again/done flows. Tier 2 unlocks after two stars on tier 1 and from day four onward. The full set is Stork Stand, Sun Flow, Quiet Candle, Squat Rail, Push-Up, Side Lunge, Step Class, Hoop Hips, Coastal Jog, Mount Posture Slalom, Tray Service, and Laundry Line. Spine Age, Gerald minutes, and three save slots that round-trip must all function. Each activity needs its specified camera perspective.\n\nThe tone must be short, polite, and sincere. The script must exceed 150 lines in that voice. A scripted full playthrough from New Member to Ending must succeed. A passive run must also reach the ending. The day 3 plateau must hold Spine Age.\n\nDev mode must be reachable from the pause menu without pausing the game. It exposes live sliders for balance and scoring variables, plus a reset-to-defaults button and jump tools. All audio must be generated in code: Animalese-style gibberish voices and music at ten percent default with no track compensation. Visuals use the engine's native pixel-puppet look in bright daylight, with new art for signature subjects like scales, plates, and mats. The canvas renders at 1920×1080 on an expanded low-res surface. No dense data screens are allowed and body text must remain readable.\n\nThe local class supports two to four players on one screen with scores and a podium. In an AI Hub session the game skips titles and menus, connects ghost seats one through four, shows the card, plays Morning Class with lockstep events and ghosts, and passes the hub harness for players one, two, three, and four under bad-network conditions plus a host-leave scenario. Outside any session the game remains unaffected and reaches its title screen. Head meta tags must match the brief and plan, specifying hub type Game, player range one to four, source Wii Fit, and session ghost.\n\nThe test suite runs via `node tests/run.mjs` and prints only failures plus a one-line pass count. Every group must pass.\n\nTests validate the shipped game directly, not a reimplementation. Lean bands must stay within ±10 of the plan's table across twelve seeds. Star thresholds are strict: tier 1 passive <1★, average ≥1★, good ≥2★; tier 2 passive <1★, good ≥1★, expert ≥2★. The suite also checks full playthrough, save round-trips, determinism, attract timing, dev sliders, local class, fake-hub, and data lint with no TODO or placeholder strings.\n\nThe browser verification plan now includes five concrete checks: engine check.mjs clean, gamepad stub, keyboard and mouse input, touch notice, and music and voice generation. The first gating step is running `node tests/run.mjs all` from the dev folder.\n\nIf the full `all` test run is too heavy, the implementer may split it into rules, browser, and hub groups. Each group must still pass with zero failures. The next gating step is launching the built HTML from a local `file://` URL with Playwright Chromium.\n\nThe Playwright launch must assert zero page errors, a canvas sized to the intended expanded PS1 surface, substantial painted content, visible title text, responsive UI changes after input, and a music setting of 0.1. A screenshot is saved to `{SCRATCH}/title-1080.png` at 1920×1080. A second launch confirms consistent success. If Playwright fails to start, the implementer captures that failure and falls back to static checks.\n\nWebGPU is not required because canvas lighting is handled by the engine. A blank canvas would be an application bug, not a fallback.\n\nThe next gating steps are:\n\n- Run `node engine/tools/check.mjs` on the built file and capture its log.\n- Execute the hub harness from PLAN section 16 across all five URLs, verify `__hubCheck.pass`, confirm the game alone reaches the title screen, and capture the logs; the server binds to 127.0.0.1 port 0 and is killed by PID.\n- Parse `exit-interview.json` and assert its shape and that the About content in the bundle matches the injected strings.\n\nThe plan must be implemented in PLAN.md milestone order, sections 15 and 17. The codebase splits into a balance model, an activity registry with data, scenes, and audio. Scripts stay plain, global G is used, and the game loads from file://. Verification also requires 1920×1080 screenshots of the Plate, Card, results, and island views.\n\nThe plan must follow PLAN.md's exact file names and milestones. Diverging from that layout is not permitted.\n\nThe file layout is design guidance, not an acceptance criterion. Verifiers may not reject correct code for diverging from it. The DONE condition is game outcomes, not the file tree. The plan contains ten milestones, M0 through M9.\n\nThe ten milestones collapse into six ordered checkpoints. M0–M1 covers engine handoff, build, tests, and playable Lean and Stork bands. M2 covers the island day loop, saves, member card, and body check. M3–M5 cover the remaining activities and bot passes. M6 covers the five-day story, ending, lines, and music. M7 covers class and hub harness. M8–M9 cover attract, dev mode, about, bundle, exit-interview, full test command green, and PROGRESS.md current.\n\nThe scope is limited to the work folder, a new dev subfolder, the engine clone from the GitHub handoff, hub session.js, mocap clips, and Playwright. Sibling folders are not read.\n\nThe title filename must contain a space, so \"Wii Fit\" is required. Headless WebGL can be slow, and tests must wait on game state rather than wall-clock time. Memory pressure from earlyoom may kill browsers; the hub four-player session is especially heavy. A dialog shadow engine patch is mandatory. The custom input map must keep confirm and start bindings. Attract mode and touch notice interact. Source information visible in chat replies is not an auditable copy for file verifiers; JSON and the About screen are.\n\nThe chat reply text must be written into the files as an auditable copy, since verifiers may not see it. TASK.md requires this content in the final reply, so the implementer must produce it and store it in the files.\n\nThe chat reply is a session deliverable, not a file. Verifiers grade the stored file and the in-game About screen. The chat text must also be printed at the end of the run. Scripted tests advance the game state directly via game.advance, so they do not need real time.\n\nThe two-page ghost visual is evidence, while the multiplayer harness remains gating. Headless Playwright works without a DISPLAY variable; xvfb is only for headed sessions. Further Wii Fit recreation research is out of scope; the implementer may read engine documentation inside the fetched handoff.\n\nGenre and perspective must be preserved verbatim from the plan. The criteria should include Sports and per-activity cameras. PROGRESS.md is a required hand-in from STAGE.md. The bundled HTML must live in the work folder root, not inside the dev subfolder.\n\nThe brief names the exact build output path, so the HTML must land there. Tests live in the dev folder and run from it. The engine handoff is a depth-1 clone of main, which may differ from the plan’s tested v0.88.0; this is a noted risk.\n\nThe title must be generated by a real RNG over a silent list of playful riffs. Verifiers can confirm it is not \"Wii Fit\" and appears on screen and in aiHub.name. The brainstorm itself is not auditable.\n\nThe exit interview must contain an aiHub object with the required shape. The brief mandates this structure and one genre from the list. The plan suggests specific values such as Sports, Game, Wii Fit, 1-4, empty series, and tags. These are guidance unless the brief explicitly requires them.\n\nThe exit-interview aiHub must contain type Game, genre Sports, source Wii Fit, players 1–4, and an empty series. Head tags in the bundled HTML are observable and will be checked as a criterion. No remote engine inspection is needed because the plan already validates recipes.\n\nThe five acceptance criteria are independently checkable and will not be split further. Each criterion maps to a specific verification step: package metadata, game content, UX and audio, multiplayer harness, and the test command.\n\nThe browser launch check is extra gating for the real entry point. The primary observable is the title screen showing the game title and a drawn studio with substantial pixels, not merely a canvas element. Launches must run more than once to confirm consistency. Synthetic stand-ins are forbidden. Tests must drive shipped functions via game.advance and debug.snapshot, not reimplementation; the lean formula can be verified by feeding bot intents.\n\nThe lean band table contains hardcoded expected percentages. Asserting the simulation lands in a band is the point. The verifier should not be required to re-simulate this formula independently.\n\nThe \"no hardcoded expected values\" rule forbids stubbing the balance model under test. Tests must assert against the spec's bands by simulating through the shipped balance model. The plan file will be written with the write tool.\n\nThe write tool creates parent directories automatically, so the target path is safe to use. I am now refining the acceptance criteria wording for clarity on small models.\n\nThe work-folder root must contain exactly one playable HTML file named `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108.html`, where `{Title}` is a real RNG choice and never \"Wii Fit\". A dev subfolder with that same basename holds the source. `exit-interview.json` sits beside the HTML with TASK.md's exact keys, non-empty plain text values, `usage` set to `added on import`, `plan` pointing to `PLAN.md`, and `source` set to `Wii Fit`. `PROGRESS.md` is also required.\n\nThe HTML About screen must reproduce the JSON's Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback verbatim, including Usage \"added on import\". The page runs from `file://` with inlined scripts to avoid CORS failures. Criterion 2 will list every activity explicitly rather than omitting one.\n\nStar bands are testable and defining, so they will be grouped into the test criterion rather than criterion 2. The criteria must also capture defining game mechanics: stillness as sport, talking scale, and ducks. Grand Stillness is a win-or-lose condition that continues the story.\n\nGentle Mode is a brief UX criterion. Boot messages are polish and fold into tone lines rather than a separate criterion. The Gerald unlock table is included in criterion 2 as one phrase: \"minutes unlock the listed cosmetic rewards.\" The class activities list is Stork, Candle, Squat, Step (two lanes), Hoop, 35s, or Surprise me.\n\nCriterion 4 will include the hub round: three events drawn from deal pools, roughly 150 seconds, AIHub.time(), ghosts, and a single result. The risks section will note that the upstream GitHub main handoff may not match PLAN.md's tested v0.88.0 or hub 1.4.0; if a recipe fails, the implementer must fix it within the game or engine and record the issue in Engine Feedback rather than silently skipping the harness.\n\nA blank screenshot from a software-rendered WebGL readback is a failure to fix. A browser crash from memory exhaustion is environmental; rerun with one browser and capture the crash rather than treating it as a pass.\n\nThe filename must keep the space because the brief substitutes \"Wii Fit\" into the title token.\n\nStory length is 40–50 real minutes. Scripted tests advance the simulation, not the wall clock.\n\nThe four feedback blocks must appear in the chat reply. Graders will audit `exit-interview.json` and the About screen as the single source of truth. The filename must contain the exact string `Wii Fit` as the SOURCE token.\n\nThe plan will follow PLAN.md's build order. Balance and scoring logic must stay pure so tests can run in a VM with window stand-ins. PROGRESS.md should be updated at every milestone. The task checklist ends with a testing and evidence step. Verification steps will be clearly tagged.\n\nThe plan will cite PLAN.md sections 15–17 as guidance for global scripts, the activity registry, data files, one test command, and one build. Verifiers judge outcomes rather than file layout. TASK.md fixes deliverable names.\n\nThe plan will require that new activity tiers, poses, charts, courses, trays, lines, or days can be added by editing data files rather than activity code. This captures the intended designer-tooling outcome without prescribing a specific file name.\n\nDeep links such as #hub and #act/stork/1 are in PLAN.md. Tests can reach scenes directly without relying on them. Autosave belongs to the saves criterion. The plan is ready to write.\n\nThe Risks section is mandatory because several genuine contradictions exist. Non-goals are explicitly listed: frame-rate targets, rendering optimization, a faithful Wii Fit recreation, physical controllers or hardware, WebGPU, network features beyond the AI Hub ghost session, Gerald’s minutes and chat, and extra libraries beyond the handoff engine.\n\nThe work root must contain the playable HTML, exit-interview.json, and PROGRESS.md. The dev subfolder receives the engine handoff from the GitHub dist/handoff clone, plus vendor session.js and mocap clips from PLAN.md §15. Game scripts, tests/run.mjs, the hub harness, and build.mjs also live there. Playwright 1.63.0 is already present and must not be reinstalled. Agents may not read sibling agent folders or kill processes they did not start.\n\nThe test criterion will require zero failures and a one-line count, not the specific number 213. Browser verification will cover touch on phone profile, WebKit iPhone smoke, and Firefox load as a single gating step within `tests/run.mjs`. `check.mjs` is included in that browser group.\n\nThe plan will require a separate launch step for entry points, not just unit tests. The file:// load must run twice to prove consistent success. Launch assertions are gating criteria; screenshots are captured evidence during that gating step.\n\nEvery gating step must capture a log. The criteria must include every never-cut feature and the full activity list, because the implementer will read this plan together with PLAN.md and the objective. Omitting any feature would weaken the contract below the objective.\n\nEyes Closed tier 2 is explicitly never cut. All tier 2 activities must be implemented because the computer can do them. The risks section will note that PLAN.md's cut order is not authorized here unless a feature is impossible; none are. The full attract playlist and all activities will be kept in the contract without shrinking it.\n\nThe filename must substitute the chosen title and the source text `Wii Fit` into the TASK.md pattern, keeping spaces that are part of those tokens. Both the `.html` in the work-folder root and the dev subfolder must use this same string. STAGE.md instructs keeping progress in PROGRESS.md, but TASK.md requires everything to be in this folder; since PROGRESS.md comes from STAGE, its location is left to the implementer.\n\nPROGRESS.md must be placed in the work-folder root, next to the HTML file. This resolves the ambiguity between STAGE.md and TASK.md.\n\nThe verification plan will run `node tests/run.mjs all` once from the dev subfolder at the end. Combined stdout and stderr must be captured to `{SCRATCH}/tests-all.log`. The script groups tests into rules, browser, and hub.\n\nThe rules-group gating step must prove the shipped game drives real input and snapshots. The log must show coverage of lean-table bands, every activity tier star band, a full New Member to Ending playthrough with a passive run, save reload, attract at 10 seconds, dev slider reset, 4-player class, and a fake AIHub report-once. A suite that omits any of these assertions fails this step.\n\nThe test runner prints only failures and a one-line count, so the log cannot reveal coverage details. Verification must therefore require reading `tests/run.mjs` and its test modules to confirm the required assertions exist and actually drive the shipped game. A stub that merely prints \"0 failed\" would then be rejected.\n\nThe Playwright Chromium gating step loads the built HTML twice via `file://` in one browser. Each run must show zero console or page errors, a canvas that is not the default 300×150 and whose bitmap is substantially painted, the chosen title string visible, and a keyboard input that changes a visible focus or menu state.\n\nThe launch step must capture `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`, and `{SCRATCH}/title-1920x1080.png` on at least one run. Both runs must succeed identically. If Playwright fails to start, the error goes to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log`. A fallback script-load check evaluates page scripts with a `window` global and no `module` or `require` to verify no throw and a game global. No screenshot may be invented.\n\nThe browser-group launch may be the same `tests/run.mjs browser` run that step 1 already executes. Step 2 must still capture the two file:// loads and the title screenshot. Running both would risk an out-of-memory crash.\n\nStep 2 must still run even if browser tests already passed. It should execute after the browsers from step 1 are closed, using one Chromium instance and a small script. This entry-point check carries a memory risk.\n\nThe hub gating step requires evidence that all five harness URLs ran with players 1 through 4 and host-leave all with `__hubCheck.pass`. If the runner swallows details, the test code must assert all five URLs and the log must show zero failed for hub. A standalone file:// load reaching the title must also be verified as part of step 2.\n\nThe hub server will run on 127.0.0.1 with an OS-selected port and be killed by PID. Its gating observations merge into step 1 to avoid duplicating the server run.\n\nA static hand-in check runs without a browser. It verifies files match the name pattern, exit-interview JSON parses correctly, the built HTML contains G.ABOUT or feedback strings and hub meta tags, game sources lack TODOs or forbidden audio extensions, and the built script includes the music default of .1. This check captures `{SCRATCH}/handoff-static.log`.\n\nThe browser group must also run `node engine/tools/check.mjs` on the built file and pass with no errors. Rules tests load via vm with window stand-ins rather than Node-only module.exports so the file:// page and tests share the same scripts. The plan is now complete.\n\nThe headline is refined to \"Build the original Wii Fit–inspired wellness-retreat balance game specified in PLAN.md and hand in TASK.md's package.\" The goal kind is code-change. A contradiction between TASK and PLAN is resolved: TASK permits omitting downloadable dependencies, but PLAN prefers inline assets. The brief wins, so the criterion will require a single HTML file that works from file://.\n\nA contradiction between TASK and PLAN.md is now clear. TASK allows omitting downloadable dependencies, but PLAN's browser test forbids all network requests and requires inline assets. Because the objective demands everything in PLAN.md, the no-network rule wins. The build must be fully self-contained with no external CDN or network calls.\n\nThe brief's allowance to skip inlining is optional, so the stricter inline requirement satisfies both documents. Criterion 4 must also require that `session.js` be the first script in the HTML head and remain unmodified.\n\nSession behavior is implementation-defined: autoPause, live, and fullscreen must produce a running round with no title screen, settings overlay over the active round, and no attract, dev, or quit menus. The 50-title RNG selection will be recorded in PROGRESS.md as verification evidence rather than a gating requirement.\n\nCriterion 1 will require PROGRESS.md to record the chosen title and the RNG method, not the full list of 50 titles. The JSON criterion must include the exact source date 2026-11-04 22:01:08 from TASK. The model is xAI, grok-4.7, with reasoning effort set to xhigh.\n\nThe environment is Grok CLI on Linux. The testing parameters field must be populated but need not contain exact prose. The About screen must list every required metadata field, including the date, source, usage, model, and environment. TASK specifies the source as Wii Fit and the plan file name as PLAN.md.\n\nThe JSON criterion will require fixed fields to match TASK.md verbatim: Tech, Art, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, and Usage. Environment and Model must be non-empty and consistent between the JSON and the About screen. The exit-interview arrays testingFeedback, engineFeedback, and promptFeedback must contain exactly 4, 6, and 4 strings in the order of the TASK.md questions. aiHub.tags should be 3 to 6 lowercase tags from the suggested set.\n\nThe JSON criterion will require genre Sports, type Game, source Wii Fit, players 1–4, and an empty series field. The tag list is not frozen to the suggested themes; only those themes are acceptable. The criteria will be tightened so small models can verify them directly.\n\nThe launch step will include the Playwright fallback script as required by the instructions. The primary browser observable is that the title screen shows the game title and a substantially filled studio frame, twice. Input changes must be visibly driven: pressing Down moves the pill focus or Enter advances the title menu.\n\nThe launch script must wait for the title menu to become interactive rather than using a fixed sleep. It can poll game state or evaluate `G` and the debug snapshot in the page context to confirm the boot sequence finished.\n\nThe launch check will use `page.evaluate` to read the debug snapshot and verify the title scene is active with high pixel fill. It will also confirm that pressing a key visibly changes the focus or snapshot state. The attract screen must not start before ten seconds of idle time. Two separate browser launches are required. xvfb is unnecessary for headless Playwright.\n\nA risk is added: earlyoom may kill the page when the hub harness and four browsers run together. The runner should retry once with the harness’s own pages only, one server, and no extra browsers. If the environment still cannot launch, the failure is recorded as a blocking environment issue rather than a skipped hub test. The hub gating criterion remains mandatory.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The brief is a full original wellness-retreat game from PLAN.md, plus TASK.md’s hand-in files. I’ll write the outcome contract from those, not a second architecture."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3a993251-5da7-4d6c-ae53-f74c48f5cffa-5","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e%2Fwork/01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c/goal/plan-01a1aa15-0e39-7353-9681-a62f8eca0135.md","content":"# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original Wii Fit–inspired wellness-retreat game and hand in TASK.md’s package\n\n## Goal kind\ncode-change\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. The work-folder root holds the hand-in: a single HTML file and a dev subfolder both named `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108` (HTML adds `.html`), where `{Title}` is a playful riff on Wii Fit chosen with a real RNG and is not the product name “Wii Fit”; `PROGRESS.md` recording that title and that an RNG was used (not the silent list of 50); and `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML. The JSON parses as one object in TASK.md’s exact shape, with feedback arrays in question order (4 testing, 6 engine, 4 prompt), `usage` exactly `added on import`, `date` `2026-11-04 22:01:08`, `source` `Wii Fit`, `plan` `PLAN.md`, `environment` `Grok CLI (headless); Linux`, `model` `xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh`, catalogue type `Game`, genre `Sports`, players `1–4`, series empty, and every other value non-empty plain text. The in-game About/Credits pages show the same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback wording, in that field order. The page runs from `file://` (no module/CORS black screen, no runtime network).\n2. The demo is a finished original sports/fitness minigame collection (catalogue Sports) about a five-day stay at a wellness retreat, coached in a short, sincere, polite voice by a talking scale and wooden instructors. There is no real weight, BMI, or body-size joke, and no Wii, Mii, or Balance Board names, copied characters, music, menus, or levels except “Wii Fit” inside Source Information. One Lean — a centre of balance chasing the player’s intent, stumbling when it leaves the unit disc — is the only skill, and the body follows that input rather than a canned clip that ignores it. A New Member playthrough can reach the ending: member card, arrival, instructor choice, then each day bungalow → body check (weighed in ducks) → island → three sessions → evening → bed; day 3 holds Spine Age; day 5 runs the Gentle Games and a Grand Stillness duel and continues whether you win or lose; credits then Free Play. All twelve activities are playable with card, short demo, countdown, play, stars, Again/Done: Stork Stand (including Eyes Closed), Sun Flow, Quiet Candle, Squat Rail, Push-Up, Side Lunge, Step Class, Hoop Hips, Coastal Jog, Mount Posture Slalom, Tray Service, and Laundry Line, with tier 2 after 2★ on tier 1 and from day 4. Views match the activity (fixed 2.5D studio, three-quarter island, behind-the-skier slalom, behind-the-runner jog, down at the tray, side-on laundry). Spine Age, Gerald’s unsinkable minutes and the listed cosmetic unlocks, and three separate save slots that reload persist. A passive player can still finish the story.\n3. The same screens are built for a TV pad first and also feel native on keyboard and mouse: lean is stick, key-nudge, or pointer; prompts follow the last used device; glyphs default to PlayStation and a setting switches to Xbox; hover moves focus and click activates in both the game pills and the HTML menus. Touch-only devices get no touch controls, the text “Controller or keyboard required”, and a Settings button, and attract still runs. After 10 seconds without input on the title or top-level menus, a looping AI/replay demo plays the activities and does not write saves or unlocks; any input returns to the title and swallows that press; it does not start at 9.9 seconds. Dev mode opens from the pause menu in every scene, keeps the game running, exposes the important Lean, scoring, cardio, game, and time variables as live sliders with a reset to defaults, and can jump scene, activity, tier, and day. All music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are Animalese-style gibberish; music defaults to 10% volume and is not made louder to compensate. Art is the engine’s bright pixel-puppet look plus new source-specific pieces (scale, plate, mats, and the activity props). Text stays large, one idea per screen, no dense data grids.\n4. On its own, local Class joins 2–4 players on one screen (pads or split keyboard) for Stork Stand, Quiet Candle, Squat Rail, Step Class, and Hoop Hips, scores them, and shows a podium; empty mats are non-scoring bots. In an AI Hub session the game skips title, attract, dev, quit, restart, and saves, connects ghost mode for 1–4 seats, shows the ready card, and plays Morning Class: three shared events dealt from the plan’s pools, timed from hub time, with translucent ghosts, progress, and a single result. The unmodified hub session script is the first script in the built page, and the hub meta tags identify a Game, source Wii Fit, 1–4 players, ghost session, about 3 minutes. The five harness cases pass (1, 2, 3 and 4 with bad network, 4 with host-leave). Outside a session, connect does not take over, and the page reaches its own title.\n5. `node tests/run.mjs` with `rules`, `browser`, `hub`, and `all` is the only test command, prints failures plus one count line, and `all` finishes with 0 failed. Those tests drive the shipped game from its real start (not a reimplementation or a mocked Lean): section 3’s bot bands land within ±10 on 12 seeds; every activity and tier meets the star gates (tier 1 passive under 1★, average at least 1★, good at least 2★; tier 2 passive under 1★, good at least 1★, expert at least 2★); a scripted playthrough and a passive run reach the ending with the day-3 plateau and unlocks; saves round-trip and a damaged save does not crash; the same seed and input hash the same twice; attract, dev sliders/reset/jumps, class, and a fake hub (one report on time-up, end stops input) behave as above; data references resolve and game sources contain no TODO, placeholder, or stub. The browser group loads the built file from `file://` with no page errors, a gamepad stub, keyboard and mouse, the touch notice, non-silent music at volume 0.1 and non-silent gibberish, and no network or audio-file requests, and the engine page check reports no errors. Nothing required above is left stubbed.\n\n## Verification plan\n1. gating: From the dev subfolder, run `node tests/run.mjs all` once after browsers are free. Capture stdout and stderr to `{SCRATCH}/tests-all.log`. Pass only if the log’s final line is a count with 0 failed and no failure block. Read the test sources and confirm they load the shipped scripts, advance the real game, and assert every outcome named in criterion 5 (bands, both playthroughs, saves, determinism, attract at 10s not 9.9s, dev, class, fake hub, data lint, file:// browser checks, engine check, and the five hub harness URLs including `__hubCheck.pass`). A runner that only prints a count, or tests that never call the shipped game, does not pass.\n2. gating: With no other browser left open, launch the built HTML from `file://` in headless Chromium twice, sequentially. Each run must wait on game state (not the wall clock) until the title scene is up, then show zero page errors, a drawing surface that is not the default 300×150 and is substantially painted (a successful blank or tiny readback fails), the chosen title visible, and a real key or pad-stub press that moves menu focus or leaves the title in the expected way. Capture `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`, and `{SCRATCH}/title-1920x1080.png`. Both runs must agree. If Playwright cannot start or cannot read pixels at all, save that error as `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log` and instead evaluate the same built scripts with a `window` global and no Node `module`/`require`, asserting they throw nothing and install the game; a readback that works and is blank is a game failure, not this fallback.\n3. gating: Static hand-in, no browser. Confirm the HTML, matching dev subfolder, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json` names and JSON shape in criterion 1; confirm the built page inlines its scripts, injects the same About text as the JSON, lists the hub meta tags, keeps session.js first and unmodified, and contains no TODO/placeholder/stub and no audio-file loads. Capture `{SCRATCH}/handoff-static.log`.\n4. evidence: If the browser group saved 1920×1080 shots of the plate, card, results, and island, copy them under `{SCRATCH}/`. Their absence alone does not fail when steps 1–3 passed. Do not start a second human, a physical pad, or a real phone.\n\n## Non-goals\n- Frame-rate targets, GPU lighting, WebGPU, and rendering optimization (explicitly later).\n- A Wii Fit recreation: Balance Board hardware, Mii, BMI, or copied minigames, menus, music, or levels.\n- A second human, a physical DualSense, real iOS Safari, a television, or a listener. Stand-ins in TASK.md are enough.\n- Hub chat, emotes, custom names, or any network other than the ghost session. Gerald’s minutes are never spent.\n- Extra engines or libraries beyond the handoff engine, its mocap clips, and the hub client.\n\n## Assumed scope\n- Work-folder root: the named HTML, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\n- New dev subfolder only: engine from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge` `dist/handoff`, hub client and harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub` `lib/hub`, mocap clip build inputs from the engine repo, game scripts, `tests/run.mjs`, and the one build script. Playwright 1.63.0 is already installed; do not run `playwright install`. Do not read or write sibling agent folders, `/tmp`, or processes you did not start.\n\n## Implementation approach\nFollow PLAN.md sections 15–17: plain scripts on one global (so `file://` works), Lean/intent/scoring kept separate from drawing, activities as data plus a registry so tests can call each one, scenes generated from that registry, and `game.debug.snapshot()` for headless checks. Build in the milestone order M0–M9 so the one test command stays green. The Dialog shadow hook, menu hover focus, touch notice, input map retaining confirm/start, and music default 0.1 are part of making those outcomes true. File names inside the dev folder may differ if the outcomes and the single test command still hold. Update `PROGRESS.md` at each milestone. Inline the bundle; do not leave CDN loads. Use the plan’s cut list only if a feature is impossible on this computer — none of the activities are.\n\n## Task checklist\n- [ ] Fetch the handoff, hub client, and clip set; add the build and `tests/run.mjs`; boot a file:// page with the session wrapper, themed shell, and music at 0.1.\n- [ ] Implement Lean, device intent, bots, the plate, rigs, the scale, and Stork Stand until the section 3 bands hold on stick, keys, and mouse.\n- [ ] Add title, member, arrival, island day loop, body check, saves, Spine Age, Gerald, and a dev panel with live Lean sliders.\n- [ ] Add the other eleven activities and tier 2, including Eyes Closed, until every tier’s star gates pass.\n- [ ] Write the five-day script, Gentle Games, duel, ending, free play, line bank, and all synthesized music, effects, and voices; pass the scripted playthrough.\n- [ ] Add local Class and the hub Morning Class round; pass the five harness cases and a standalone title launch.\n- [ ] Finish attract, full dev mode, About, the single HTML, and `exit-interview.json`; run `node tests/run.mjs all` and keep the two launch logs.\n\n## Risks / Contradictions\n- PLAN.md was written against engine v0.88.0 and hub client 1.4.0, but the fetch is current `main`. If a tested recipe no longer matches, adapt and record it in Engine Feedback; do not drop the harness.\n- The required filename contains a space because SOURCE is `Wii Fit`. Keep that token.\n- Software WebGL and earlyoom can kill a browser (“Target closed”, “page crashed”). Close browsers between steps and keep one Chromium. One memory retry is allowed; a second kill is captured and is not a pass. Hub stays required.\n- A blank canvas from a working screenshot is a failed launch, not an environment excuse. Only a launcher that cannot start or cannot read pixels uses the script-eval fallback.\n- TASK.md also asks for the four feedback blocks in the chat reply. Graders should treat `exit-interview.json` and the About screen as the auditable copy; they must match, and the finishing reply must print the same text.\n"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":23297,"output_tokens":16066,"cache_read_input_tokens":15232,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"bd7f8c36-bc1e-4eca-b9a2-e6efb66bcb49"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-3a993251-5da7-4d6c-ae53-f74c48f5cffa-5","content":"{\"type\":\"SearchReplace\",\"EditsApplied\":{\"old_string\":\"\",\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original Wii Fit–inspired wellness-retreat game and hand in TASK.md’s package\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work-folder root holds the hand-in: a single HTML file and a dev subfolder both named `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108` (HTML adds `.html`), where `{Title}` is a playful riff on Wii Fit chosen with a real RNG and is not the product name “Wii Fit”; `PROGRESS.md` recording that title and that an RNG was used (not the silent list of 50); and `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML. The JSON parses as one object in TASK.md’s exact shape, with feedback arrays in question order (4 testing, 6 engine, 4 prompt), `usage` exactly `added on import`, `date` `2026-11-04 22:01:08`, `source` `Wii Fit`, `plan` `PLAN.md`, `environment` `Grok CLI (headless); Linux`, `model` `xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh`, catalogue type `Game`, genre `Sports`, players `1–4`, series empty, and every other value non-empty plain text. The in-game About/Credits pages show the same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback wording, in that field order. The page runs from `file://` (no module/CORS black screen, no runtime network).\\n2. The demo is a finished original sports/fitness minigame collection (catalogue Sports) about a five-day stay at a wellness retreat, coached in a short, sincere, polite voice by a talking scale and wooden instructors. There is no real weight, BMI, or body-size joke, and no Wii, Mii, or Balance Board names, copied characters, music, menus, or levels except “Wii Fit” inside Source Information. One Lean — a centre of balance chasing the player’s intent, stumbling when it leaves the unit disc — is the only skill, and the body follows that input rather than a canned clip that ignores it. A New Member playthrough can reach the ending: member card, arrival, instructor choice, then each day bungalow → body check (weighed in ducks) → island → three sessions → evening → bed; day 3 holds Spine Age; day 5 runs the Gentle Games and a Grand Stillness duel and continues whether you win or lose; credits then Free Play. All twelve activities are playable with card, short demo, countdown, play, stars, Again/Done: Stork Stand (including Eyes Closed), Sun Flow, Quiet Candle, Squat Rail, Push-Up, Side Lunge, Step Class, Hoop Hips, Coastal Jog, Mount Posture Slalom, Tray Service, and Laundry Line, with tier 2 after 2★ on tier 1 and from day 4. Views match the activity (fixed 2.5D studio, three-quarter island, behind-the-skier slalom, behind-the-runner jog, down at the tray, side-on laundry). Spine Age, Gerald’s unsinkable minutes and the listed cosmetic unlocks, and three separate save slots that reload persist. A passive player can still finish the story.\\n3. The same screens are built for a TV pad first and also feel native on keyboard and mouse: lean is stick, key-nudge, or pointer; prompts follow the last used device; glyphs default to PlayStation and a setting switches to Xbox; hover moves focus and click activates in both the game pills and the HTML menus. Touch-only devices get no touch controls, the text “Controller or keyboard required”, and a Settings button, and attract still runs. After 10 seconds without input on the title or top-level menus, a looping AI/replay demo plays the activities and does not write saves or unlocks; any input returns to the title and swallows that press; it does not start at 9.9 seconds. Dev mode opens from the pause menu in every scene, keeps the game running, exposes the important Lean, scoring, cardio, game, and time variables as live sliders with a reset to defaults, and can jump scene, activity, tier, and day. All music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are Animalese-style gibberish; music defaults to 10% volume and is not made louder to compensate. Art is the engine’s bright pixel-puppet look plus new source-specific pieces (scale, plate, mats, and the activity props). Text stays large, one idea per screen, no dense data grids.\\n4. On its own, local Class joins 2–4 players on one screen (pads or split keyboard) for Stork Stand, Quiet Candle, Squat Rail, Step Class, and Hoop Hips, scores them, and shows a podium; empty mats are non-scoring bots. In an AI Hub session the game skips title, attract, dev, quit, restart, and saves, connects ghost mode for 1–4 seats, shows the ready card, and plays Morning Class: three shared events dealt from the plan’s pools, timed from hub time, with translucent ghosts, progress, and a single result. The unmodified hub session script is the first script in the built page, and the hub meta tags identify a Game, source Wii Fit, 1–4 players, ghost session, about 3 minutes. The five harness cases pass (1, 2, 3 and 4 with bad network, 4 with host-leave). Outside a session, connect does not take over, and the page reaches its own title.\\n5. `node tests/run.mjs` with `rules`, `browser`, `hub`, and `all` is the only test command, prints failures plus one count line, and `all` finishes with 0 failed. Those tests drive the shipped game from its real start (not a reimplementation or a mocked Lean): section 3’s bot bands land within ±10 on 12 seeds; every activity and tier meets the star gates (tier 1 passive under 1★, average at least 1★, good at least 2★; tier 2 passive under 1★, good at least 1★, expert at least 2★); a scripted playthrough and a passive run reach the ending with the day-3 plateau and unlocks; saves round-trip and a damaged save does not crash; the same seed and input hash the same twice; attract, dev sliders/reset/jumps, class, and a fake hub (one report on time-up, end stops input) behave as above; data references resolve and game sources contain no TODO, placeholder, or stub. The browser group loads the built file from `file://` with no page errors, a gamepad stub, keyboard and mouse, the touch notice, non-silent music at volume 0.1 and non-silent gibberish, and no network or audio-file requests, and the engine page check reports no errors. Nothing required above is left stubbed.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev subfolder, run `node tests/run.mjs all` once after browsers are free. Capture stdout and stderr to `{SCRATCH}/tests-all.log`. Pass only if the log’s final line is a count with 0 failed and no failure block. Read the test sources and confirm they load the shipped scripts, advance the real game, and assert every outcome named in criterion 5 (bands, both playthroughs, saves, determinism, attract at 10s not 9.9s, dev, class, fake hub, data lint, file:// browser checks, engine check, and the five hub harness URLs including `__hubCheck.pass`). A runner that only prints a count, or tests that never call the shipped game, does not pass.\\n2. gating: With no other browser left open, launch the built HTML from `file://` in headless Chromium twice, sequentially. Each run must wait on game state (not the wall clock) until the title scene is up, then show zero page errors, a drawing surface that is not the default 300×150 and is substantially painted (a successful blank or tiny readback fails), the chosen title visible, and a real key or pad-stub press that moves menu focus or leaves the title in the expected way. Capture `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`, and `{SCRATCH}/title-1920x1080.png`. Both runs must agree. If Playwright cannot start or cannot read pixels at all, save that error as `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log` and instead evaluate the same built scripts with a `window` global and no Node `module`/`require`, asserting they throw nothing and install the game; a readback that works and is blank is a game failure, not this fallback.\\n3. gating: Static hand-in, no browser. Confirm the HTML, matching dev subfolder, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json` names and JSON shape in criterion 1; confirm the built page inlines its scripts, injects the same About text as the JSON, lists the hub meta tags, keeps session.js first and unmodified, and contains no TODO/placeholder/stub and no audio-file loads. Capture `{SCRATCH}/handoff-static.log`.\\n4. evidence: If the browser group saved 1920×1080 shots of the plate, card, results, and island, copy them under `{SCRATCH}/`. Their absence alone does not fail when steps 1–3 passed. Do not start a second human, a physical pad, or a real phone.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- Frame-rate targets, GPU lighting, WebGPU, and rendering optimization (explicitly later).\\n- A Wii Fit recreation: Balance Board hardware, Mii, BMI, or copied minigames, menus, music, or levels.\\n- A second human, a physical DualSense, real iOS Safari, a television, or a listener. Stand-ins in TASK.md are enough.\\n- Hub chat, emotes, custom names, or any network other than the ghost session. Gerald’s minutes are never spent.\\n- Extra engines or libraries beyond the handoff engine, its mocap clips, and the hub client.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work-folder root: the named HTML, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- New dev subfolder only: engine from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge` `dist/handoff`, hub client and harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub` `lib/hub`, mocap clip build inputs from the engine repo, game scripts, `tests/run.mjs`, and the one build script. Playwright 1.63.0 is already installed; do not run `playwright install`. Do not read or write sibling agent folders, `/tmp`, or processes you did not start.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md sections 15–17: plain scripts on one global (so `file://` works), Lean/intent/scoring kept separate from drawing, activities as data plus a registry so tests can call each one, scenes generated from that registry, and `game.debug.snapshot()` for headless checks. Build in the milestone order M0–M9 so the one test command stays green. The Dialog shadow hook, menu hover focus, touch notice, input map retaining confirm/start, and music default 0.1 are part of making those outcomes true. File names inside the dev folder may differ if the outcomes and the single test command still hold. Update `PROGRESS.md` at each milestone. Inline the bundle; do not leave CDN loads. Use the plan’s cut list only if a feature is impossible on this computer — none of the activities are.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Fetch the handoff, hub client, and clip set; add the build and `tests/run.mjs`; boot a file:// page with the session wrapper, themed shell, and music at 0.1.\\n- [ ] Implement Lean, device intent, bots, the plate, rigs, the scale, and Stork Stand until the section 3 bands hold on stick, keys, and mouse.\\n- [ ] Add title, member, arrival, island day loop, body check, saves, Spine Age, Gerald, and a dev panel with live Lean sliders.\\n- [ ] Add the other eleven activities and tier 2, including Eyes Closed, until every tier’s star gates pass.\\n- [ ] Write the five-day script, Gentle Games, duel, ending, free play, line bank, and all synthesized music, effects, and voices; pass the scripted playthrough.\\n- [ ] Add local Class and the hub Morning Class round; pass the five harness cases and a standalone title launch.\\n- [ ] Finish attract, full dev mode, About, the single HTML, and `exit-interview.json`; run `node tests/run.mjs all` and keep the two launch logs.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md was written against engine v0.88.0 and hub client 1.4.0, but the fetch is current `main`. If a tested recipe no longer matches, adapt and record it in Engine Feedback; do not drop the harness.\\n- The required filename contains a space because SOURCE is `Wii Fit`. Keep that token.\\n- Software WebGL and earlyoom can kill a browser (“Target closed”, “page crashed”). Close browsers between steps and keep one Chromium. One memory retry is allowed; a second kill is captured and is not a pass. Hub stays required.\\n- A blank canvas from a working screenshot is a failed launch, not an environment excuse. Only a launcher that cannot start or cannot read pixels uses the script-eval fallback.\\n- TASK.md also asks for the four feedback blocks in the chat reply. Graders should treat `exit-interview.json` and the About screen as the auditable copy; they must match, and the finishing reply must print the same text.\\n\",\"tool_output_for_prompt\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e%2Fwork/01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c/goal/plan-01a1aa15-0e39-7353-9681-a62f8eca0135.md has been created.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e%2Fwork/01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c/goal/plan-01a1aa15-0e39-7353-9681-a62f8eca0135.md has been created.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e%2Fwork/01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c/goal/plan-01a1aa15-0e39-7353-9681-a62f8eca0135.md\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"\",\"old_line\":1,\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original Wii Fit–inspired wellness-retreat game and hand in TASK.md’s package\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work-folder root holds the hand-in: a single HTML file and a dev subfolder both named `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108` (HTML adds `.html`), where `{Title}` is a playful riff on Wii Fit chosen with a real RNG and is not the product name “Wii Fit”; `PROGRESS.md` recording that title and that an RNG was used (not the silent list of 50); and `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML. The JSON parses as one object in TASK.md’s exact shape, with feedback arrays in question order (4 testing, 6 engine, 4 prompt), `usage` exactly `added on import`, `date` `2026-11-04 22:01:08`, `source` `Wii Fit`, `plan` `PLAN.md`, `environment` `Grok CLI (headless); Linux`, `model` `xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh`, catalogue type `Game`, genre `Sports`, players `1–4`, series empty, and every other value non-empty plain text. The in-game About/Credits pages show the same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback wording, in that field order. The page runs from `file://` (no module/CORS black screen, no runtime network).\\n2. The demo is a finished original sports/fitness minigame collection (catalogue Sports) about a five-day stay at a wellness retreat, coached in a short, sincere, polite voice by a talking scale and wooden instructors. There is no real weight, BMI, or body-size joke, and no Wii, Mii, or Balance Board names, copied characters, music, menus, or levels except “Wii Fit” inside Source Information. One Lean — a centre of balance chasing the player’s intent, stumbling when it leaves the unit disc — is the only skill, and the body follows that input rather than a canned clip that ignores it. A New Member playthrough can reach the ending: member card, arrival, instructor choice, then each day bungalow → body check (weighed in ducks) → island → three sessions → evening → bed; day 3 holds Spine Age; day 5 runs the Gentle Games and a Grand Stillness duel and continues whether you win or lose; credits then Free Play. All twelve activities are playable with card, short demo, countdown, play, stars, Again/Done: Stork Stand (including Eyes Closed), Sun Flow, Quiet Candle, Squat Rail, Push-Up, Side Lunge, Step Class, Hoop Hips, Coastal Jog, Mount Posture Slalom, Tray Service, and Laundry Line, with tier 2 after 2★ on tier 1 and from day 4. Views match the activity (fixed 2.5D studio, three-quarter island, behind-the-skier slalom, behind-the-runner jog, down at the tray, side-on laundry). Spine Age, Gerald’s unsinkable minutes and the listed cosmetic unlocks, and three separate save slots that reload persist. A passive player can still finish the story.\\n3. The same screens are built for a TV pad first and also feel native on keyboard and mouse: lean is stick, key-nudge, or pointer; prompts follow the last used device; glyphs default to PlayStation and a setting switches to Xbox; hover moves focus and click activates in both the game pills and the HTML menus. Touch-only devices get no touch controls, the text “Controller or keyboard required”, and a Settings button, and attract still runs. After 10 seconds without input on the title or top-level menus, a looping AI/replay demo plays the activities and does not write saves or unlocks; any input returns to the title and swallows that press; it does not start at 9.9 seconds. Dev mode opens from the pause menu in every scene, keeps the game running, exposes the important Lean, scoring, cardio, game, and time variables as live sliders with a reset to defaults, and can jump scene, activity, tier, and day. All music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are Animalese-style gibberish; music defaults to 10% volume and is not made louder to compensate. Art is the engine’s bright pixel-puppet look plus new source-specific pieces (scale, plate, mats, and the activity props). Text stays large, one idea per screen, no dense data grids.\\n4. On its own, local Class joins 2–4 players on one screen (pads or split keyboard) for Stork Stand, Quiet Candle, Squat Rail, Step Class, and Hoop Hips, scores them, and shows a podium; empty mats are non-scoring bots. In an AI Hub session the game skips title, attract, dev, quit, restart, and saves, connects ghost mode for 1–4 seats, shows the ready card, and plays Morning Class: three shared events dealt from the plan’s pools, timed from hub time, with translucent ghosts, progress, and a single result. The unmodified hub session script is the first script in the built page, and the hub meta tags identify a Game, source Wii Fit, 1–4 players, ghost session, about 3 minutes. The five harness cases pass (1, 2, 3 and 4 with bad network, 4 with host-leave). Outside a session, connect does not take over, and the page reaches its own title.\\n5. `node tests/run.mjs` with `rules`, `browser`, `hub`, and `all` is the only test command, prints failures plus one count line, and `all` finishes with 0 failed. Those tests drive the shipped game from its real start (not a reimplementation or a mocked Lean): section 3’s bot bands land within ±10 on 12 seeds; every activity and tier meets the star gates (tier 1 passive under 1★, average at least 1★, good at least 2★; tier 2 passive under 1★, good at least 1★, expert at least 2★); a scripted playthrough and a passive run reach the ending with the day-3 plateau and unlocks; saves round-trip and a damaged save does not crash; the same seed and input hash the same twice; attract, dev sliders/reset/jumps, class, and a fake hub (one report on time-up, end stops input) behave as above; data references resolve and game sources contain no TODO, placeholder, or stub. The browser group loads the built file from `file://` with no page errors, a gamepad stub, keyboard and mouse, the touch notice, non-silent music at volume 0.1 and non-silent gibberish, and no network or audio-file requests, and the engine page check reports no errors. Nothing required above is left stubbed.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev subfolder, run `node tests/run.mjs all` once after browsers are free. Capture stdout and stderr to `{SCRATCH}/tests-all.log`. Pass only if the log’s final line is a count with 0 failed and no failure block. Read the test sources and confirm they load the shipped scripts, advance the real game, and assert every outcome named in criterion 5 (bands, both playthroughs, saves, determinism, attract at 10s not 9.9s, dev, class, fake hub, data lint, file:// browser checks, engine check, and the five hub harness URLs including `__hubCheck.pass`). A runner that only prints a count, or tests that never call the shipped game, does not pass.\\n2. gating: With no other browser left open, launch the built HTML from `file://` in headless Chromium twice, sequentially. Each run must wait on game state (not the wall clock) until the title scene is up, then show zero page errors, a drawing surface that is not the default 300×150 and is substantially painted (a successful blank or tiny readback fails), the chosen title visible, and a real key or pad-stub press that moves menu focus or leaves the title in the expected way. Capture `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`, and `{SCRATCH}/title-1920x1080.png`. Both runs must agree. If Playwright cannot start or cannot read pixels at all, save that error as `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log` and instead evaluate the same built scripts with a `window` global and no Node `module`/`require`, asserting they throw nothing and install the game; a readback that works and is blank is a game failure, not this fallback.\\n3. gating: Static hand-in, no browser. Confirm the HTML, matching dev subfolder, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json` names and JSON shape in criterion 1; confirm the built page inlines its scripts, injects the same About text as the JSON, lists the hub meta tags, keeps session.js first and unmodified, and contains no TODO/placeholder/stub and no audio-file loads. Capture `{SCRATCH}/handoff-static.log`.\\n4. evidence: If the browser group saved 1920×1080 shots of the plate, card, results, and island, copy them under `{SCRATCH}/`. Their absence alone does not fail when steps 1–3 passed. Do not start a second human, a physical pad, or a real phone.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- Frame-rate targets, GPU lighting, WebGPU, and rendering optimization (explicitly later).\\n- A Wii Fit recreation: Balance Board hardware, Mii, BMI, or copied minigames, menus, music, or levels.\\n- A second human, a physical DualSense, real iOS Safari, a television, or a listener. Stand-ins in TASK.md are enough.\\n- Hub chat, emotes, custom names, or any network other than the ghost session. Gerald’s minutes are never spent.\\n- Extra engines or libraries beyond the handoff engine, its mocap clips, and the hub client.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work-folder root: the named HTML, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- New dev subfolder only: engine from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge` `dist/handoff`, hub client and harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub` `lib/hub`, mocap clip build inputs from the engine repo, game scripts, `tests/run.mjs`, and the one build script. Playwright 1.63.0 is already installed; do not run `playwright install`. Do not read or write sibling agent folders, `/tmp`, or processes you did not start.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md sections 15–17: plain scripts on one global (so `file://` works), Lean/intent/scoring kept separate from drawing, activities as data plus a registry so tests can call each one, scenes generated from that registry, and `game.debug.snapshot()` for headless checks. Build in the milestone order M0–M9 so the one test command stays green. The Dialog shadow hook, menu hover focus, touch notice, input map retaining confirm/start, and music default 0.1 are part of making those outcomes true. File names inside the dev folder may differ if the outcomes and the single test command still hold. Update `PROGRESS.md` at each milestone. Inline the bundle; do not leave CDN loads. Use the plan’s cut list only if a feature is impossible on this computer — none of the activities are.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Fetch the handoff, hub client, and clip set; add the build and `tests/run.mjs`; boot a file:// page with the session wrapper, themed shell, and music at 0.1.\\n- [ ] Implement Lean, device intent, bots, the plate, rigs, the scale, and Stork Stand until the section 3 bands hold on stick, keys, and mouse.\\n- [ ] Add title, member, arrival, island day loop, body check, saves, Spine Age, Gerald, and a dev panel with live Lean sliders.\\n- [ ] Add the other eleven activities and tier 2, including Eyes Closed, until every tier’s star gates pass.\\n- [ ] Write the five-day script, Gentle Games, duel, ending, free play, line bank, and all synthesized music, effects, and voices; pass the scripted playthrough.\\n- [ ] Add local Class and the hub Morning Class round; pass the five harness cases and a standalone title launch.\\n- [ ] Finish attract, full dev mode, About, the single HTML, and `exit-interview.json`; run `node tests/run.mjs all` and keep the two launch logs.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md was written against engine v0.88.0 and hub client 1.4.0, but the fetch is current `main`. If a tested recipe no longer matches, adapt and record it in Engine Feedback; do not drop the harness.\\n- The required filename contains a space because SOURCE is `Wii Fit`. Keep that token.\\n- Software WebGL and earlyoom can kill a browser (“Target closed”, “page crashed”). Close browsers between steps and keep one Chromium. One memory retry is allowed; a second kill is captured and is not a pass. Hub stays required.\\n- A blank canvas from a working screenshot is a failed launch, not an environment excuse. Only a launcher that cannot start or cannot read pixels uses the script-eval fallback.\\n- TASK.md also asks for the four feedback blocks in the chat reply. Graders should treat `exit-interview.json` and the About screen as the auditable copy; they must match, and the finishing reply must print the same text.\\n\",\"new_line\":1,\"context_before\":\"\",\"context_after\":\"\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"789d7f60-76e3-48d8-8084-d34656402210"}
