record it in Engine Feedback; do not drop the harness.\\n- The required filename contains a space because SOURCE is `Wii Fit`. Keep that token.\\n- Software WebGL and earlyoom can kill a browser (“Target closed”, “page crashed”). Close browsers between steps and keep one Chromium. One memory retry is allowed; a second kill is captured and is not a pass. Hub stays required.\\n- A blank canvas from a working screenshot is a failed launch, not an environment excuse. Only a launcher that cannot start or cannot read pixels uses the script-eval fallback.\\n- TASK.md also asks for the four feedback blocks in the chat reply. Graders should treat `exit-interview.json` and the About screen as the auditable copy; they must match, and the finishing reply must print the same text.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e%2Fwork/01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c/goal/plan.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original Wii Fit–inspired wellness-retreat game and hand in TASK.md’s package\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work-folder root holds the hand-in: a single HTML file and a dev subfolder both named `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108` (HTML adds `.html`), where `{Title}` is a playful riff on Wii Fit chosen with a real RNG and is not the product name “Wii Fit”; `PROGRESS.md` recording that title and that an RNG was used (not the silent list of 50); and `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML. The JSON parses as one object in TASK.md’s exact shape, with feedback arrays in question order (4 testing, 6 engine, 4 prompt), `usage` exactly `added on import`, `date` `2026-11-04 22:01:08`, `source` `Wii Fit`, `plan` `PLAN.md`, `environment` `Grok CLI (headless); Linux`, `model` `xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh`, catalogue type `Game`, genre `Sports`, players `1–4`, series empty, and every other value non-empty plain text. The in-game About/Credits pages show the same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback wording, in that field order. The page runs from `file://` (no module/CORS black screen, no runtime network).\\n2. The demo is a finished original sports/fitness minigame collection (catalogue Sports) about a five-day stay at a wellness retreat, coached in a short, sincere, polite voice by a talking scale and wooden instructors. There is no real weight, BMI, or body-size joke, and no Wii, Mii, or Balance Board names, copied characters, music, menus, or levels except “Wii Fit” inside Source Information. One Lean — a centre of balance chasing the player’s intent, stumbling when it leaves the unit disc — is the only skill, and the body follows that input rather than a canned clip that ignores it. A New Member playthrough can reach the ending: member card, arrival, instructor choice, then each day bungalow → body check (weighed in ducks) → island → three sessions → evening → bed; day 3 holds Spine Age; day 5 runs the Gentle Games and a Grand Stillness duel and continues whether you win or lose; credits then Free Play. All twelve activities are playable with card, short demo, countdown, play, stars, Again/Done: Stork Stand (including Eyes Closed), Sun Flow, Quiet Candle, Squat Rail, Push-Up, Side Lunge, Step Class, Hoop Hips, Coastal Jog, Mount Posture Slalom, Tray Service, and Laundry Line, with tier 2 after 2★ on tier 1 and from day 4. Views match the activity (fixed 2.5D studio, three-quarter island, behind-the-skier slalom, behind-the-runner jog, down at the tray, side-on laundry). Spine Age, Gerald’s unsinkable minutes and the listed cosmetic unlocks, and three separate save slots that reload persist. A passive player can still finish the story.\\n3. The same screens are built for a TV pad first and also feel native on keyboard and mouse: lean is stick, key-nudge, or pointer; prompts follow the last used device; glyphs default to PlayStation and a setting switches to Xbox; hover moves focus and click activates in both the game pills and the HTML menus. Touch-only devices get no touch controls, the text “Controller or keyboard required”, and a Settings button, and attract still runs. After 10 seconds without input on the title or top-level menus, a looping AI/replay demo plays the activities and does not write saves or unlocks; any input returns to the title and swallows that press; it does not start at 9.9 seconds. Dev mode opens from the pause menu in every scene, keeps the game running, exposes the important Lean, scoring, cardio, game, and time variables as live sliders with a reset to defaults, and can jump scene, activity, tier, and day. All music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are Animalese-style gibberish; music defaults to 10% volume and is not made louder to compensate. Art is the engine’s bright pixel-puppet look plus new source-specific pieces (scale, plate, mats, and the activity props). Text stays large, one idea per screen, no dense data grids.\\n4. On its own, local Class joins 2–4 players on one screen (pads or split keyboard) for Stork Stand, Quiet Candle, Squat Rail, Step Class, and Hoop Hips, scores them, and shows a podium; empty mats are non-scoring bots. In an AI Hub session the game skips title, attract, dev, quit, restart, and saves, connects ghost mode for 1–4 seats, shows the ready card, and plays Morning Class: three shared events dealt from the plan’s pools, timed from hub time, with translucent ghosts, progress, and a single result. The unmodified hub session script is the first script in the built page, and the hub meta tags identify a Game, source Wii Fit, 1–4 players, ghost session, about 3 minutes. The five harness cases pass (1, 2, 3 and 4 with bad network, 4 with host-leave). Outside a session, connect does not take over, and the page reaches its own title.\\n5. `node tests/run.mjs` with `rules`, `browser`, `hub`, and `all` is the only test command, prints failures plus one count line, and `all` finishes with 0 failed. Those tests drive the shipped game from its real start (not a reimplementation or a mocked Lean): section 3’s bot bands land within ±10 on 12 seeds; every activity and tier meets the star gates (tier 1 passive under 1★, average at least 1★, good at least 2★; tier 2 passive under 1★, good at least 1★, expert at least 2★); a scripted playthrough and a passive run reach the ending with the day-3 plateau and unlocks; saves round-trip and a damaged save does not crash; the same seed and input hash the same twice; attract, dev sliders/reset/jumps, class, and a fake hub (one report on time-up, end stops input) behave as above; data references resolve and game sources contain no TODO, placeholder, or stub. The browser group loads the built file from `file://` with no page errors, a gamepad stub, keyboard and mouse, the touch notice, non-silent music at volume 0.1 and non-silent gibberish, and no network or audio-file requests, and the engine page check reports no errors. Nothing required above is left stubbed.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: From the dev subfolder, run `node tests/run.mjs all` once after browsers are free. Capture stdout and stderr to `{SCRATCH}/tests-all.log`. Pass only if the log’s final line is a count with 0 failed and no failure block. Read the test sources and confirm they load the shipped scripts, advance the real game, and assert every outcome named in criterion 5 (bands, both playthroughs, saves, determinism, attract at 10s not 9.9s, dev, class, fake hub, data lint, file:// browser checks, engine check, and the five hub harness URLs including `__hubCheck.pass`). A runner that only prints a count, or tests that never call the shipped game, does not pass.\\n2. gating: With no other browser left open, launch the built HTML from `file://` in headless Chromium twice, sequentially. Each run must wait on game state (not the wall clock) until the title scene is up, then show zero page errors, a drawing surface that is not the default 300×150 and is substantially painted (a successful blank or tiny readback fails), the chosen title visible, and a real key or pad-stub press that moves menu focus or leaves the title in the expected way. Capture `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.log`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.log`, and `{SCRATCH}/title-1920x1080.png`. Both runs must agree. If Playwright cannot start or cannot read pixels at all, save that error as `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.log` and instead evaluate the same built scripts with a `window` global and no Node `module`/`require`, asserting they throw nothing and install the game; a readback that works and is blank is a game failure, not this fallback.\\n3. gating: Static hand-in, no browser. Confirm the HTML, matching dev subfolder, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json` names and JSON shape in criterion 1; confirm the built page inlines its scripts, injects the same About text as the JSON, lists the hub meta tags, keeps session.js first and unmodified, and contains no TODO/placeholder/stub and no audio-file loads. Capture `{SCRATCH}/handoff-static.log`.\\n4. evidence: If the browser group saved 1920×1080 shots of the plate, card, results, and island, copy them under `{SCRATCH}/`. Their absence alone does not fail when steps 1–3 passed. Do not start a second human, a physical pad, or a real phone.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- Frame-rate targets, GPU lighting, WebGPU, and rendering optimization (explicitly later).\\n- A Wii Fit recreation: Balance Board hardware, Mii, BMI, or copied minigames, menus, music, or levels.\\n- A second human, a physical DualSense, real iOS Safari, a television, or a listener. Stand-ins in TASK.md are enough.\\n- Hub chat, emotes, custom names, or any network other than the ghost session. Gerald’s minutes are never spent.\\n- Extra engines or libraries beyond the handoff engine, its mocap clips, and the hub client.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work-folder root: the named HTML, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- New dev subfolder only: engine from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge` `dist/handoff`, hub client and harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub` `lib/hub`, mocap clip build inputs from the engine repo, game scripts, `tests/run.mjs`, and the one build script. Playwright 1.63.0 is already installed; do not run `playwright install`. Do not read or write sibling agent folders, `/tmp`, or processes you did not start.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md sections 15–17: plain scripts on one global (so `file://` works), Lean/intent/scoring kept separate from drawing, activities as data plus a registry so tests can call each one, scenes generated from that registry, and `game.debug.snapshot()` for headless checks. Build in the milestone order M0–M9 so the one test command stays green. The Dialog shadow hook, menu hover focus, touch notice, input map retaining confirm/start, and music default 0.1 are part of making those outcomes true. File names inside the dev folder may differ if the outcomes and the single test command still hold. Update `PROGRESS.md` at each milestone. Inline the bundle; do not leave CDN loads. Use the plan’s cut list only if a feature is impossible on this computer — none of the activities are.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Fetch the handoff, hub client, and clip set; add the build and `tests/run.mjs`; boot a file:// page with the session wrapper, themed shell, and music at 0.1.\\n- [ ] Implement Lean, device intent, bots, the plate, rigs, the scale, and Stork Stand until the section 3 bands hold on stick, keys, and mouse.\\n- [ ] Add title, member, arrival, island day loop, body check, saves, Spine Age, Gerald, and a dev panel with live Lean sliders.\\n- [ ] Add the other eleven activities and tier 2, including Eyes Closed, until every tier’s star gates pass.\\n- [ ] Write the five-day script, Gentle Games, duel, ending, free play, line bank, and all synthesized music, effects, and voices; pass the scripted playthrough.\\n- [ ] Add local Class and the hub Morning Class round; pass the five harness cases and a standalone title launch.\\n- [ ] Finish attract, full dev mode, About, the single HTML, and `exit-interview.json`; run `node tests/run.mjs all` and keep the two launch logs.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md was written against engine v0.88.0 and hub client 1.4.0, but the fetch is current `main`. If a tested recipe no longer matches, adapt and record it in Engine Feedback; do not drop the harness.\\n- The required filename contains a space because SOURCE is `Wii Fit`. Keep that token.\\n- Software WebGL and earlyoom can kill a browser (“Target closed”, “page crashed”). Close browsers between steps and keep one Chromium. One memory retry is allowed; a second kill is captured and is not a pass. Hub stays required.\\n- A blank canvas from a working screenshot is a failed launch, not an environment excuse. Only a launcher that cannot start or cannot read pixels uses the script-eval fallback.\\n- TASK.md also asks for the four feedback blocks in the chat reply. Graders should treat `exit-interview.json` and the About screen as the auditable copy; they must match, and the finishing reply must print the same text.\\n\",\"total_lines\":47}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-18f5743b-007f-4ad1-a268-3d0ef21e0bf1-1","content":"{\"type\":\"ListDir\",\"Content\":{\"content\":\"- /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/\\n  - PLAN.md\\n  - STAGE.md\\n  - TASK.md\",\"absolute_root_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work\"}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"b52c438f-0d5a-4ce9-98b4-2da05071a94c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_5","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a large game development task.","signature":"UZsgXWwlXdfP4vQuHRN76bmeqPJamH+So3jLBZe7wtWhMw2lf8nbXV/uO4I2XJUxL9aG1l6ww7LyfkskYgy58jNetvSrwsWy0burN8hz1V5EsvP+fx//YSgXxszwj7zfcEaDQJ/6ry+9Th3jQ2QFd8DjdB9vg60P3Impp4xCC/psPTaDAPfAbBoMUYMXlyS/p8mLtRf0j2KxHCb+lXJlo4nbMI5nTd34zs/o0Ly/MekBYalQj6pni6SWpXoc"},{"type":"text","text":"The plan is a full Wii Fit–style retreat game with a specific hand-in. Next I’ll read STAGE, PLAN, and TASK so the build matches them."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a16aaf4d-0401-4565-b25b-cef0055bf62c-2","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/STAGE.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a16aaf4d-0401-4565-b25b-cef0055bf62c-3","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/TASK.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a16aaf4d-0401-4565-b25b-cef0055bf62c-4","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/PLAN.md","limit":400}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3334,"output_tokens":231,"cache_read_input_tokens":21376,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"aa0c47ff-a9c0-41f8-acca-66b87d993471"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-a16aaf4d-0401-4565-b25b-cef0055bf62c-2","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/STAGE.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"total_lines\":1}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-a16aaf4d-0401-4565-b25b-cef0055bf62c-3","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→SOURCE: Wii Fit\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:01:08\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→SOURCE: Wii Fit\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to de