,114,47,102,105,116,99,108,105,112,115,46,106,115,10,40,110,111,100,101,58,51,56,48,48,56,49,53,41,32,87,97,114,110,105,110,103,58,32,84,104,101,32,39,78,79,95,67,79,76,79,82,39,32,101,110,118,32,105,115,32,105,103,110,111,114,101,100,32,100,117,101,32,116,111,32,116,104,101,32,39,70,79,82,67,69,95,67,79,76,79,82,39,32,101,110,118,32,98,101,105,110,103,32,115,101,116,46,10,40,85,115,101,32,96,110,111,100,101,32,45,45,116,114,97,99,101,45,119,97,114,110,105,110,103,115,32,46,46,46,96,32,116,111,32,115,104,111,119,32,119,104,101,114,101,32,116,104,101,32,119,97,114,110,105,110,103,32,119,97,115,32,99,114,101,97,116,101,100,41,10,10,97,100,100,101,100,32,50,32,112,97,99,107,97,103,101,115,32,105,110,32,56,51,49,109,115,10,78,80,77,95,79,75,10],\"output_for_prompt\":\"exit: 0\\nvendor/clipsrc/src/mocap:\\ntotal 40\\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 micha micha  4096 Nov  4 22:06 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 micha micha  4096 Nov  4 22:06 ..\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 28843 Nov  4 22:06 readable.js\\n\\nvendor/clipsrc/tools:\\ntotal 16\\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 micha micha 4096 Nov  4 22:06 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 micha micha 4096 Nov  4 22:06 ..\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 3121 Nov  4 22:06 anim-set.mjs\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 2161 Nov  4 22:06 mocap-lib.mjs\\nwrote vendor/fitclips.js: 23 clips from MESH2MOTION and CMU and CMU_111 and CMU_22, 560 key poses, 173 KB\\n176912 vendor/fitclips.js\\n(node:3800815) Warning: The 'NO_COLOR' env is ignored due to the 'FORCE_COLOR' env being set.\\n(Use `node --trace-warnings ...` to show where the warning was created)\\n\\nadded 2 packages in 831ms\\nNPM_OK\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"cd \\\"Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108\\\" && ls -la vendor/clipsrc/tools vendor/clipsrc/src/mocap && node vendor/clipsrc/tools/anim-set.mjs vendor/clipsrc/mesh2motion.js vendor/clipsrc/cmu.js vendor/clipsrc/CMU_111.js vendor/clipsrc/CMU_22.js \\\\\\n  --clips \\\"Pushup,Meditate,Jumping Jacks,Sleeping,Victory,Cheering_Two_Hands,Dizzy,Idle_Subtle,Greeting,CMU:Run_Loop,CMU:Walk_Loop,Stretch_Yawn,Get_Up_From_Back,Waiting_Loop,Hop_One_Foot_Loop,Wave_Hello,111_38_part1,111_38_part2,111_39_part1,111_39_part2,111_39_part3,111_41_part3,22_14\\\" \\\\\\n  --name FITCLIPS --out vendor/fitclips.js --credit \\\"Mesh2Motion (CC0), Quaternius (CC0), CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses)\\\" && wc -c vendor/fitclips.js && npm install playwright@1.63.0 --no-fund --no-audit --loglevel=error && echo NPM_OK\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Build fitclips set and install Playwright\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e%2Fwork/01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c/terminal/call-550cb569-a7b2-4201-97aa-ae0d1c605e20-13.log\",\"total_bytes\":752}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-550cb569-a7b2-4201-97aa-ae0d1c605e20-14","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\\n\\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\\n\\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\\n10→\\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\\n\\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\n\\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\\n\\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\\n20→> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Modes\\n\\nA game declares one mode.\\n\\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\\n30→|---|---|---|\\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\\n\\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\\n40→- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\\n  world the host sends them.\\n\\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## A round\\n50→\\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\\n   (*The card*).\\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\\n4. The game reports how it goes.\\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\\n60→   over the game, then removes it.\\n\\n### Tags\\n\\nIn the game's `<head>`:\\n\\n```html\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"MIN-MAX\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"MODE\\\">\\n70→<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"GOAL\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"CONTROLS\\\">\\n```\\n\\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\\n  is in that range.\\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\\n80→\\nOptional:\\n\\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\\n\\n### The client\\n\\n90→[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\\n\\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\\n100→  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\\n\\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\\nedited copy is not replaced.\\n\\n### Joining\\n\\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\\n110→  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\\n\\n120→`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\\n130→\\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\\n\\n### The card\\n\\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\\n\\n140→```js\\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\\n```\\n\\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\\nit deserves.\\n\\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\\n150→\\n- the game's name;\\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\\n- how to win the round;\\n- the controls this player will use;\\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\\n\\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\\non the card:\\n\\n160→- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\\n\\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\\n\\n170→`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\\n180→\\n### The start\\n\\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\\n`start`.\\n\\n### Reporting\\n\\n190→- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\\n  by the host only:\\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\\n200→    placed last.\\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\\n\\n### Time-up and the end\\n\\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\\n  `finish()` by then is void.\\n210→- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\\n  game, then removes it.\\n\\n| `end` reason | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\\n220→| `session-ended` | the session closed |\\n\\n### Leaving\\n\\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\\n230→  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\\n  that player leaving.\\n\\n### Scoring\\n\\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\\n  progress.\\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\\n  result instead of medals.\\n240→- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\\n\\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\\nresults:\\n\\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\\n\\n250→Places and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\\n\\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\\n\\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\\n10→\\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\\n\\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\n\\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\\n\\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\\n20→> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Modes\\n\\nA game declares one mode.\\n\\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\\n30→|---|---|---|\\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\\n\\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\\n40→- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\\n  world the host sends them.\\n\\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## A round\\n50→\\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\\n   (*The card*).\\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\\n4. The game reports how it goes.\\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\\n60→   over the game, then removes it.\\n\\n### Tags\\n\\nIn the game's `<head>`:\\n\\n```html\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"MIN-MAX\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"MODE\\\">\\n70→<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"GOAL\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"CONTROLS\\\">\\n```\\n\\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\\n  is in that range.\\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\\n80→\\nOptional:\\n\\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\\n\\n### The client\\n\\n90→[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\\n\\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\\n100→  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\\n\\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\\nedited copy is not replaced.\\n\\n### Joining\\n\\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\\n110→  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\\n\\n120→`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\\n130→\\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\\n\\n### The card\\n\\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\\n\\n140→```js\\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\\n```\\n\\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\\nit deserves.\\n\\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\\n150→\\n- the game's name;\\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\\n- how to win the round;\\n- the controls this player will use;\\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\\n\\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\\non the card:\\n\\n160→- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\\n\\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\\n\\n170→`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\\n180→\\n### The start\\n\\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\\n`start`.\\n\\n### Reporting\\n\\n190→- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\\n  by the host only:\\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\\n200→    placed last.\\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\\n\\n### Time-up and the end\\n\\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\\n  `finish()` by then is void.\\n210→- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\\n  game, then removes it.\\n\\n| `end` reason | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\\n220→| `session-ended` | the session closed |\\n\\n### Leaving\\n\\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\\n230→  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\\n  that player leaving.\\n\\n### Scoring\\n\\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\\n  progress.\\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\\n  result instead of medals.\\n240→- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\\n\\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\\nresults:\\n\\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\\n\\n250→Places and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/tests/hub/README.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":250,\"raw_output\":\"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\\n\\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\\n\\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\\n\\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\\n\\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\n\\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\\n\\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Modes\\n\\nA game declares one mode.\\n\\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\\n\\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\\n  world the host sends them.\\n\\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## A round\\n\\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\\n   (*The card*).\\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\\n4. The game reports how it goes.\\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\\n   over the game, then removes it.\\n\\n### Tags\\n\\nIn the game's `<head>`:\\n\\n```html\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"MIN-MAX\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"MODE\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"GOAL\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"CONTROLS\\\">\\n```\\n\\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\\n  is in that range.\\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\\n\\nOptional:\\n\\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\\n\\n### The client\\n\\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\\n\\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\\n\\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\\nedited copy is not replaced.\\n\\n### Joining\\n\\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\\n\\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\\n\\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\\n\\n### The card\\n\\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\\n\\n```js\\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\\n```\\n\\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\\nit deserves.\\n\\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\\n\\n- the game's name;\\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\\n- how to win the round;\\n- the controls this player will use;\\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\\n\\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\\non the card:\\n\\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\\n\\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\\n\\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\\n\\n### The start\\n\\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\\n`start`.\\n\\n### Reporting\\n\\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\\n  by the host only:\\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\\n    placed last.\\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\\n\\n### Time-up and the end\\n\\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\\n  `finish()` by then is void.\\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\\n  game, then removes it.\\n\\n| `end` reason | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\\n\\n### Leaving\\n\\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\\n  that player leaving.\\n\\n### Scoring\\n\\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\\n  progress.\\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\\n  result instead of medals.\\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\\n\\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\\nresults:\\n\\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\\n\\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\\n\",\"total_lines\":410}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-550cb569-a7b2-4201-97aa-ae0d1c605e20-15","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"269→## 5. Input\\n270→\\n- `new E.Input(screen, map, signal) → input`: signal: an AbortSignal that removes every listener this adds (game.dispose()). A screen without a canvas (headless) gets no DOM listeners: input comes from press(code), release(code), tap(action) and hold(action, on)\\n- `E.Input.DEFAULT { attack, dash, skill, jump }`: top-down / isometric action (Zelda, Diablo): Space dashes, J or click attacks\\n- `E.Input.PLATFORMER { jump, attack, dash, skill }`: side-scrollers (Mario, Mega Man): Space / Z / K jump, X / J attack or shoot, Shift / C dash\\n- `E.Input.SHMUP { fire, bomb }`: shoot-'em-ups (1942, Gradius): Space / Z / J fire (hold for autofire), X / K bomb\\n- `E.Input.THIRDPERSON { sprint, jump, crouch, interact, attack, block, dodge, use, map }` †: third-person action and stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear): WASD / stick move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch (a toggle), E interact, J attack, K block, L dodge, Q use, M map. Pad: A jump, B crouch, X attack, Y interact, LB block, RB dodge, LT use, RT or L3 sprint, Back map\\n- `E.Input.halfKeys(preset, side)` †: a preset for half a keyboard ('left' or 'right'): its moves and menu keys, the preset's other actions on its buttons in order, and the preset's gamepad codes (Input.halfKeys(Input.SHMUP, 'right') -> fire on . and Numpad 1, bomb on /). A preset's own halves come first: preset.halves = { left: { pass: ['KeyR'] }, right: { pass: ['KeyL'] } } (an action a menu reads beside confirm would take confirm's F otherwise)\\n- `input.anyPressed()`: any action pressed this step (title screens: \\\"press any button\\\")\\n- `input.bindButtons(root)` †: wire every element with data-act=\\\"<action>\\\" inside root to the action\\n- `input.bindings` †: the player's bindings over the preset: { action: { keys: [codes], pad: [codes] } } (game.settings.bindings keeps them)\\n280→- `input.box()` †: the pointer's gestures, read each step in screen pixels (the HUD's space), from a mouse or one finger: box() -> { x0, y0, x1, y1, from, to, done } while a box is drawn (a mouse's left button dragged; on a touch screen a long press, then a drag), done the step it is let go; else null\\n- `input.buffered(a, win = .15)`: was it pressed within the last `win` seconds and not yet consumed? (input buffering)\\n- `input.clear()`: Release all devices and discard buffered input on focus loss or a modal transition.\\n- `input.clicked()` †: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space: r.W, r.H), during the step after it lifted (a press that drags away, like the touch stick's, is none); else null. Menus and dialogs answer to it; a HUD button of your own can: if (hit(input.clicked())) ...\\n- `input.codeLabel(code)`: a code as a person reads it: 'W' (or the keyboard's own letter there), 'Space', '↑', 'Left click', and a gamepad button by its symbol on the controller in use ('A', '✕', 'B'...; glyphs picks the family)\\n- `input.consume(a)`: mark a buffered press of action a as used, so buffered(a) is false until the next press\\n- `input.consumeAll()`: forget every pending press (after closing a menu or dialog, so the same press does not also attack)\\n- `input.doubleTap()` †: [x, y] the step a second tap or click lands within .35 s and 8 pixels of the first (a double tap), else null\\n- `input.down(a)`: is the action held right now?\\n- `input.drag()` †: how far the touch stick's finger moved since the last step, in screen pixels [dx, dy] (the HUD's space), or null while no finger holds it: to steer one to one (a shooter's ship follows the thumb) instead of by the stick's direction\\n290→- `input.feed(type, x, y, o = {})` †: a pointer event in screen pixels, as the browser's would give: feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch: false, button: 0, id: 0 (which finger, for zones) }) (a test's or a bot's mouse and fingers: clicks, boxes, long presses, double taps and pans follow; a finger in one of the zones holds its act), or feed('zoom', x, y, { k: 1.1 }): a wheel's notch or a pinch about that point\\n- `input.history` †: the last second of input, oldest first: [{ t (input.clock), dir (numpad 1-9 for a fighter facing right: 5 neutral), pressed: the actions pressed then, directions aside }], one entry each time the direction changes or a button is pressed: an input display draws it; sequence() and motion() read it (section 10h: a kit without it has none)\\n- `input.hold(a, on = true)`: hold an action down (on) or let it go (false), as its key would\\n- `input.label(a, device = this.lastDevice)`: the key or button to show in a prompt for action a, on the device the player used last (or the one named: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'), with their bindings: input.label('jump') -> 'Space', 'A' or '✕'\\n- `input.longPress()` †: [x, y] the step a press held still has been held .45 s (a long press), else null\\n- `input.motion(digits, button, o = {})` †: a fighter's move in numpad notation, mirrored by facing: input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing, window }) is a quarter circle forward and punch (down, down-forward, forward, attack: a fireball); '623' a dragon punch, '41236' a half circle. facing: 1 (right, the default) or -1. button: an action, 'attack+kick', or none (the motion alone)\\n- `input.mouseScreen()`: mouse position in projected screen pixels, or null\\n- `input.move()`: movement intent in screen directions: x right, y down, length <= 1\\n- `input.padStyle()`: the controller family whose symbols label() shows: glyphs, or with glyphs 'auto' the one its name says\\n- `input.pan()` †: [dx, dy] how far a drag moved this step: one finger on open ground (not a box), or a mouse's middle or right button; else null. A free camera pans by it (game.cam.free)\\n300→- `input.pointer()` †: the mouse pointer in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space), or null before it has moved over the canvas\\n- `input.press(code)`: press a key or button code ('KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:attack') as if the person did: scripted input, tests, bots\\n- `input.pressed(a)`: true only during the first update step after the press (menus, jumps, one-shot actions)\\n- `input.rebind(a, device, code)` †: bind action a to one code for a device ('keys' = keyboard and mouse, 'pad' = gamepad), in place of that device's codes: input.rebind('attack', 'keys', 'KeyF'). Returns null when done, the action that already uses the code (nothing changes: free it first), or 'reserved' for the codes the menus keep (Escape, Enter, F keys, Start, the d-pad). The arrow keys and the d-pad always move as well\\n- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false } } })\\n- `input.release(code)`: let go of a code pressed with press()\\n- `input.released(a)`: true only during the first update step after the action was let go\\n- `input.repeat(a, delay = .3, rate = .08)`: pressed, then repeats while held (after `delay` s, every `rate` s): menu cursors, puzzle pieces\\n- `input.resetBindings()` †: forget the player's bindings: every action back to its preset's codes\\n- `input.rumble(strength = .5, seconds = .15)` †: shake the gamepad: strength 0..1 for seconds (scaled by rumbleScale; nothing where the controller can't). Returns true when it rumbled\\n310→- `input.screenPoint(cx, cy)` †: browser client coords -> screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space, with no camera), or null without a canvas\\n- `input.sequence(steps, o = {})` †: true on the step a sequence of inputs ends, each one after the last within window seconds (.5) of the first: input.sequence(['down', 'down+right', 'right', 'attack']). A step names actions joined by +: its directions are held exactly as it says (up, down, left, right; 'neutral' for none) as they change to that, its buttons pressed. A cheat code: input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })\\n- `input.stopRumble()` †: stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged)\\n- `input.suppress(a)` †: Suppress a held action until the next tick (a HUD widget owns the pointer this step).\\n- `input.suppressCode(code)` †: A widget owns this button; other devices bound to the same action still work.\\n- `input.tap(a)`: press an action for one update step, as a quick tap of its key (scripted input, tests, an AI): pressed() and down() are true in the next step, released() in the one after\\n- `input.tick(dt)`: advance input by one step: read the gamepad and turn presses since the last step into this step's pressed() and released() (the engine calls it every step)\\n- `input.touchArea()` †: where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself)\\n- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { jump: 'A', attack: 'B' }, force }). The other half of the screen is a move stick (past halfway it presses up, down, left and right too, as a d-pad does: menus and ladders). START is the corner's menu button, top right: it opens the menu, or pauses a pausable scene into it (without the corner a START button there starts a title and pauses, as a gamepad's Start)\\n- `input.touchSet(a, label = null, show = true)` †: one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('confirm', near ? 'TALK' : null, near || talking)\\n320→- `input.unbind(a, device)` †: take away action a's codes for one device ('keys' or 'pad'); the arrow keys and d-pad stay on the moves\\n- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, THIRDPERSON, SHMUP) has the moves (up, down, left, right) and the menu's actions (start, pause, confirm, cancel). The keys held stay held (down() in the new layout, not pressed() again), and a press no step has seen counts in it\\n- `input.value(a)` †: how far action a is pressed, 0 to 1: a gamepad button by its travel (the triggers, even short of a press), a stick's direction (PadAxisUp ..., or up / down / left / right on the touch stick) by its push, any other code held 1\\n- `input.zoom()` †: the camera zoom the player asked for since the last step, as a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther; 1: none): the wheel, - and = and a pinch, where the scene lets them zoom. The game applies it (game.zoomBy)\\n- `input.zoomPoint()` †: [x, y] where the last zoom was asked for: the mouse pointer for the wheel, between the fingers for a pinch; null when the keys zoomed (about the screen's middle)\\n- `input.aimSource = 'move'` †: what aims right now: 'move' | 'mouse' | 'pad'\\n- `input.clock = 0`: seconds of input time (the steps' dt added up)\\n- `input.deadzone = .18`: how far a gamepad stick moves before it counts (0..0.8)\\n- `input.glyphs = 'auto'`: the gamepad's button symbols for label(): 'auto' (from the controller's name) | 'xbox' | 'playstation' | 'nintendo' | 'generic' (positions)\\n- `input.held`: the key and button codes down right now ('KeyD', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump')\\n330→- `input.invertMoveX`, `input.invertMoveY = false` †: the move stick turned round (the Controller settings)\\n- `input.keysTo = null` †: the other players' inputs the keyboard's codes go to as well (players sharing it, each with half its keys: game.players)\\n- `input.lastDevice = 'keyboard'`: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch', the last one used (show the right button prompts)\\n- `input.map`: each action's codes ({ jump: ['Space', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump'] }); every action also answers to 'Act:<name>' (DOM buttons with data-act, tap, hold)\\n- `input.mouse`: { cx, cy } its page position, active once it has moved over the canvas, t the input clock when it last moved\\n- `input.names = null` †: the actions' names on the Controls screen ({ attack: 'Sword' }; false hides one from rebinding: the game's menu.controls); onBindings(b): called with the player's new bindings (the game saves and applies them)\\n- `input.pad = 'any'` †: the gamepad this input reads: 'any' (the first connected one another player hasn't: player 1's), an index (a joined player's own), or null (none)\\n- `input.padAim = null` †: its right stick, or null when centered\\n- `input.padAxes = []` †: the gamepad's raw axes\\n- `input.padId = ''`, `input.padIndex = null`: the gamepad in use (null and '' without one)\\n340→- `input.padLost = false`: true for the step after the gamepad in use was unplugged (a pausable scene pauses)\\n- `input.padMove = [0, 0]`: the gamepad's left stick after the dead zone\\n- `input.padPrev = []`: which gamepad buttons were down at the last step\\n- `input.padValues = []` †: each gamepad button's travel, 0 to 1 (the triggers; input.value reads them)\\n- `input.preset` †: the layout last given to use() (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, SHMUP or your own)\\n- `input.rumbleScale = .6` †: vibration strength 0..1 (0 = off; game.settings.rumble sets it)\\n- `input.screen`: the game's screen (mouse positions are read through it)\\n- `input.steer = 'stick'` †: 'stick' (a stick drawn under the thumb) or 'drag' (a finger anywhere on the screen but the buttons steers by drag(), with no stick drawn: a shooter's ship that follows the thumb)\\n- `input.stick = [0, 0]` †: the touch stick's direction [x, y], length <= 1\\n- `input.stickDeadzone = .12` †: the part of that radius a thumb moves before the stick counts (0..0.5)\\n350→- `input.stickId` †: the pointer (finger) holding the touch stick\\n- `input.stickOn = false` †: a finger is on it\\n- `input.stickRadius = 44` †: how far (CSS pixels) a finger drags for full tilt\\n- `input.stickSide = 'left'` †: 'left' or 'right', the half of the screen the stick lives on\\n- `input.touchEnabled = true` †: false turns the touch stick off\\n- `input.zones = null` †: areas of the screen a finger holds an action down in, [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }] (fractions of the canvas, 0..1; the first that holds the finger wins), or null: a pinball's two halves, a rhythm game's lanes. A finger landing in one presses act until it lifts (several fingers, several zones), and starts no stick\\n\\n## 6. Pixel fonts\\n\\n- `E.font`: Pixel fonts. 'normal' (default) is a 5x7 proportional font with lower case; 'tiny' is the 3x5 upper-case font. text(g, str, x, y, color, opts) where opts is an outline color, false, or { font: 'normal' | 'tiny', outline: '#0b0814' | false, shadow: '#000' (1 px drop shadow), gradient: ['#fff', '#8ad'] (top to bottom), scale: 1, align: 'left' | 'center' | 'right', wrap: maxWidthPx, lineGap } '\\\\n' starts a new line. Returns { w, h } of the drawn block. title(...) draws big logo text.\\n360→- `E.font.default` †: the font used when none is named: 'normal' (5 x 7) or 'tiny' (3 x 5)\\n- `E.font.height(lines = 1, o, lineGap)`: the height in pixels of that many lines (with o's scale and font, and lineGap between them)\\n- `E.font.lineHeight(o)`: pixels from one line to the next\\n- `E.font.text(g, s, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o = '#0b0814')`: draw text at (x, y) (its top-left; align: 'center' or 'right' moves it) in color. o: an outline color, or { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font }; returns { w, h }\\n- `E.font.title(g, s, x, y, o = {})`: Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge. title(g, 'CASTLE OF NIGHT', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: ['#fff6c8', '#ffd36a', '#e07a2a'], outline: '#1a0c10', depth: 3, depthColor: '#6a2a1a', align: 'center' })\\n- `E.font.width(s, o)`: width in pixels of the widest line. opts: a scale number or { font, scale }\\n- `E.font.wrap(s, maxW, o)`: split text into lines no wider than maxW pixels\\n\\n## 7. Lighting\\n\\n370→- `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity = 1, o)` †: radius in world units; intensity ~0.5..1.5. opts: { color: '#ffaa55' (both lightings), shadow: true (GPU lighting: walls and casters block it) }\\n- `game.lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` †: something that blocks light (GPU shadows): a cylinder of radius r from the floor up to height top\\n- `game.lights.heat(x, y, z, width, strength = 1)` †: heat shimmer above a point (GPU only). width in world units, strength ~0.5..1.5\\n- `game.lights.on` †: true when the canvas lighting draws this frame: enabled, or standing in for GPU lighting the game turned on that can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view); gpu.fallback = false keeps such a frame unlit\\n- `game.lights.refresh()` †: rebuild the darkness bands after changing dark, levels or glow (lights.dark = [r, g, b]; lights.refresh())\\n- `game.lights.render(r)` †: darken the frame by the lights (the renderer calls it after the queue, when enabled)\\n- `game.lights.ambient = .15` †: how lit unlit places are, 0 (black) .. 1\\n- `game.lights.dark = [10, 6, 24]` †: the color of darkness, [r, g, b]\\n- `game.lights.darkness = .9` †: how dark the darkest places get, 0..1 (the smooth style, E.style.trans 'alpha')\\n- `game.lights.enabled = false` †: false (the default) turns the canvas lighting off\\n380→- `game.lights.glow = [255, 200, 140, 0]` †: the color, [r, g, b, a], where light is brightest\\n- `game.lights.glowAt = 1.7` †: the light level (1 = fully lit) above which it glows\\n- `game.lights.levels = [.9, .7, .48, .26, .08, 0]` †: the darkness of each lighting band, darkest first (dithered between them)\\n- `game.lights.list = []` †: this frame's lights from add()\\n- `game.lights.screen = false` †: true measures lights on the screen, not the ground (side views and PlatformMaps do it by themselves)\\n- `game.lights.tint = .32` †: how strongly lights add their own color, 0..1\\n\\n## 8. Particles\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"269→## 5. Input\\n270→\\n- `new E.Input(screen, map, signal) → input`: signal: an AbortSignal that removes every listener this adds (game.dispose()). A screen without a canvas (headless) gets no DOM listeners: input comes from press(code), release(code), tap(action) and hold(action, on)\\n- `E.Input.DEFAULT { attack, dash, skill, jump }`: top-down / isometric action (Zelda, Diablo): Space dashes, J or click attacks\\n- `E.Input.PLATFORMER { jump, attack, dash, skill }`: side-scrollers (Mario, Mega Man): Space / Z / K jump, X / J attack or shoot, Shift / C dash\\n- `E.Input.SHMUP { fire, bomb }`: shoot-'em-ups (1942, Gradius): Space / Z / J fire (hold for autofire), X / K bomb\\n- `E.Input.THIRDPERSON { sprint, jump, crouch, interact, attack, block, dodge, use, map }` †: third-person action and stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear): WASD / stick move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch (a toggle), E interact, J attack, K block, L dodge, Q use, M map. Pad: A jump, B crouch, X attack, Y interact, LB block, RB dodge, LT use, RT or L3 sprint, Back map\\n- `E.Input.halfKeys(preset, side)` †: a preset for half a keyboard ('left' or 'right'): its moves and menu keys, the preset's other actions on its buttons in order, and the preset's gamepad codes (Input.halfKeys(Input.SHMUP, 'right') -> fire on . and Numpad 1, bomb on /). A preset's own halves come first: preset.halves = { left: { pass: ['KeyR'] }, right: { pass: ['KeyL'] } } (an action a menu reads beside confirm would take confirm's F otherwise)\\n- `input.anyPressed()`: any action pressed this step (title screens: \\\"press any button\\\")\\n- `input.bindButtons(root)` †: wire every element with data-act=\\\"<action>\\\" inside root to the action\\n- `input.bindings` †: the player's bindings over the preset: { action: { keys: [codes], pad: [codes] } } (game.settings.bindings keeps them)\\n280→- `input.box()` †: the pointer's gestures, read each step in screen pixels (the HUD's space), from a mouse or one finger: box() -> { x0, y0, x1, y1, from, to, done } while a box is drawn (a mouse's left button dragged; on a touch screen a long press, then a drag), done the step it is let go; else null\\n- `input.buffered(a, win = .15)`: was it pressed within the last `win` seconds and not yet consumed? (input buffering)\\n- `input.clear()`: Release all devices and discard buffered input on focus loss or a modal transition.\\n- `input.clicked()` †: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space: r.W, r.H), during the step after it lifted (a press that drags away, like the touch stick's, is none); else null. Menus and dialogs answer to it; a HUD button of your own can: if (hit(input.clicked())) ...\\n- `input.codeLabel(code)`: a code as a person reads it: 'W' (or the keyboard's own letter there), 'Space', '↑', 'Left click', and a gamepad button by its symbol on the controller in use ('A', '✕', 'B'...; glyphs picks the family)\\n- `input.consume(a)`: mark a buffered press of action a as used, so buffered(a) is false until the next press\\n- `input.consumeAll()`: forget every pending press (after closing a menu or dialog, so the same press does not also attack)\\n- `input.doubleTap()` †: [x, y] the step a second tap or click lands within .35 s and 8 pixels of the first (a double tap), else null\\n- `input.down(a)`: is the action held right now?\\n- `input.drag()` †: how far the touch stick's finger moved since the last step, in screen pixels [dx, dy] (the HUD's space), or null while no finger holds it: to steer one to one (a shooter's ship follows the thumb) instead of by the stick's direction\\n290→- `input.feed(type, x, y, o = {})` †: a pointer event in screen pixels, as the browser's would give: feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch: false, button: 0, id: 0 (which finger, for zones) }) (a test's or a bot's mouse and fingers: clicks, boxes, long presses, double taps and pans follow; a finger in one of the zones holds its act), or feed('zoom', x, y, { k: 1.1 }): a wheel's notch or a pinch about that point\\n- `input.history` †: the last second of input, oldest first: [{ t (input.clock), dir (numpad 1-9 for a fighter facing right: 5 neutral), pressed: the actions pressed then, directions aside }], one entry each time the direction changes or a button is pressed: an input display draws it; sequence() and motion() read it (section 10h: a kit without it has none)\\n- `input.hold(a, on = true)`: hold an action down (on) or let it go (false), as its key would\\n- `input.label(a, device = this.lastDevice)`: the key or button to show in a prompt for action a, on the device the player used last (or the one named: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'), with their bindings: input.label('jump') -> 'Space', 'A' or '✕'\\n- `input.longPress()` †: [x, y] the step a press held still has been held .45 s (a long press), else null\\n- `input.motion(digits, button, o = {})` †: a fighter's move in numpad notation, mirrored by facing: input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing, window }) is a quarter circle forward and punch (down, down-forward, forward, attack: a fireball); '623' a dragon punch, '41236' a half circle. facing: 1 (right, the default) or -1. button: an action, 'attack+kick', or none (the motion alone)\\n- `input.mouseScreen()`: mouse position in projected screen pixels, or null\\n- `input.move()`: movement intent in screen directions: x right, y down, length <= 1\\n- `input.padStyle()`: the controller family whose symbols label() shows: glyphs, or with glyphs 'auto' the one its name says\\n- `input.pan()` †: [dx, dy] how far a drag moved this step: one finger on open ground (not a box), or a mouse's middle or right button; else null. A free camera pans by it (game.cam.free)\\n300→- `input.pointer()` †: the mouse pointer in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space), or null before it has moved over the canvas\\n- `input.press(code)`: press a key or button code ('KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:attack') as if the person did: scripted input, tests, bots\\n- `input.pressed(a)`: true only during the first update step after the press (menus, jumps, one-shot actions)\\n- `input.rebind(a, device, code)` †: bind action a to one code for a device ('keys' = keyboard and mouse, 'pad' = gamepad), in place of that device's codes: input.rebind('attack', 'keys', 'KeyF'). Returns null when done, the action that already uses the code (nothing changes: free it first), or 'reserved' for the codes the menus keep (Escape, Enter, F keys, Start, the d-pad). The arrow keys and the d-pad always move as well\\n- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false } } })\\n- `input.release(code)`: let go of a code pressed with press()\\n- `input.released(a)`: true only during the first update step after the action was let go\\n- `input.repeat(a, delay = .3, rate = .08)`: pressed, then repeats while held (after `delay` s, every `rate` s): menu cursors, puzzle pieces\\n- `input.resetBindings()` †: forget the player's bindings: every action back to its preset's codes\\n- `input.rumble(strength = .5, seconds = .15)` †: shake the gamepad: strength 0..1 for seconds (scaled by rumbleScale; nothing where the controller can't). Returns true when it rumbled\\n310→- `input.screenPoint(cx, cy)` †: browser client coords -> screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space, with no camera), or null without a canvas\\n- `input.sequence(steps, o = {})` †: true on the step a sequence of inputs ends, each one after the last within window seconds (.5) of the first: input.sequence(['down', 'down+right', 'right', 'attack']). A step names actions joined by +: its directions are held exactly as it says (up, down, left, right; 'neutral' for none) as they change to that, its buttons pressed. A cheat code: input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })\\n- `input.stopRumble()` †: stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged)\\n- `input.suppress(a)` †: Suppress a held action until the next tick (a HUD widget owns the pointer this step).\\n- `input.suppressCode(code)` †: A widget owns this button; other devices bound to the same action still work.\\n- `input.tap(a)`: press an action for one update step, as a quick tap of its key (scripted input, tests, an AI): pressed() and down() are true in the next step, released() in the one after\\n- `input.tick(dt)`: advance input by one step: read the gamepad and turn presses since the last step into this step's pressed() and released() (the engine calls it every step)\\n- `input.touchArea()` †: where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself)\\n- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { jump: 'A', attack: 'B' }, force }). The other half of the screen is a move stick (past halfway it presses up, down, left and right too, as a d-pad does: menus and ladders). START is the corner's menu button, top right: it opens the menu, or pauses a pausable scene into it (without the corner a START button there starts a title and pauses, as a gamepad's Start)\\n- `input.touchSet(a, label = null, show = true)` †: one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('confirm', near ? 'TALK' : null, near || talking)\\n320→- `input.unbind(a, device)` †: take away action a's codes for one device ('keys' or 'pad'); the arrow keys and d-pad stay on the moves\\n- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, THIRDPERSON, SHMUP) has the moves (up, down, left, right) and the menu's actions (start, pause, confirm, cancel). The keys held stay held (down() in the new layout, not pressed() again), and a press no step has seen counts in it\\n- `input.value(a)` †: how far action a is pressed, 0 to 1: a gamepad button by its travel (the triggers, even short of a press), a stick's direction (PadAxisUp ..., or up / down / left / right on the touch stick) by its push, any other code held 1\\n- `input.zoom()` †: the camera zoom the player asked for since the last step, as a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther; 1: none): the wheel, - and = and a pinch, where the scene lets them zoom. The game applies it (game.zoomBy)\\n- `input.zoomPoint()` †: [x, y] where the last zoom was asked for: the mouse pointer for the wheel, between the fingers for a pinch; null when the keys zoomed (about the screen's middle)\\n- `input.aimSource = 'move'` †: what aims right now: 'move' | 'mouse' | 'pad'\\n- `input.clock = 0`: seconds of input time (the steps' dt added up)\\n- `input.deadzone = .18`: how far a gamepad stick moves before it counts (0..0.8)\\n- `input.glyphs = 'auto'`: the gamepad's button symbols for label(): 'auto' (from the controller's name) | 'xbox' | 'playstation' | 'nintendo' | 'generic' (positions)\\n- `input.held`: the key and button codes down right now ('KeyD', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump')\\n330→- `input.invertMoveX`, `input.invertMoveY = false` †: the move stick turned round (the Controller settings)\\n- `input.keysTo = null` †: the other players' inputs the keyboard's codes go to as well (players sharing it, each with half its keys: game.players)\\n- `input.lastDevice = 'keyboard'`: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch', the last one used (show the right button prompts)\\n- `input.map`: each action's codes ({ jump: ['Space', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump'] }); every action also answers to 'Act:<name>' (DOM buttons with data-act, tap, hold)\\n- `input.mouse`: { cx, cy } its page position, active once it has moved over the canvas, t the input clock when it last moved\\n- `input.names = null` †: the actions' names on the Controls screen ({ attack: 'Sword' }; false hides one from rebinding: the game's menu.controls); onBindings(b): called with the player's new bindings (the game saves and applies them)\\n- `input.pad = 'any'` †: the gamepad this input reads: 'any' (the first connected one another player hasn't: player 1's), an index (a joined player's own), or null (none)\\n- `input.padAim = null` †: its right stick, or null when centered\\n- `input.padAxes = []` †: the gamepad's raw axes\\n- `input.padId = ''`, `input.padIndex = null`: the gamepad in use (null and '' without one)\\n340→- `input.padLost = false`: true for the step after the gamepad in use was unplugged (a pausable scene pauses)\\n- `input.padMove = [0, 0]`: the gamepad's left stick after the dead zone\\n- `input.padPrev = []`: which gamepad buttons were down at the last step\\n- `input.padValues = []` †: each gamepad button's travel, 0 to 1 (the triggers; input.value reads them)\\n- `input.preset` †: the layout last given to use() (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, SHMUP or your own)\\n- `input.rumbleScale = .6` †: vibration strength 0..1 (0 = off; game.settings.rumble sets it)\\n- `input.screen`: the game's screen (mouse positions are read through it)\\n- `input.steer = 'stick'` †: 'stick' (a stick drawn under the thumb) or 'drag' (a finger anywhere on the screen but the buttons steers by drag(), with no stick drawn: a shooter's ship that follows the thumb)\\n- `input.stick = [0, 0]` †: the touch stick's direction [x, y], length <= 1\\n- `input.stickDeadzone = .12` †: the part of that radius a thumb moves before the stick counts (0..0.5)\\n350→- `input.stickId` †: the pointer (finger) holding the touch stick\\n- `input.stickOn = false` †: a finger is on it\\n- `input.stickRadius = 44` †: how far (CSS pixels) a finger drags for full tilt\\n- `input.stickSide = 'left'` †: 'left' or 'right', the half of the screen the stick lives on\\n- `input.touchEnabled = true` †: false turns the touch stick off\\n- `input.zones = null` †: areas of the screen a finger holds an action down in, [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }] (fractions of the canvas, 0..1; the first that holds the finger wins), or null: a pinball's two halves, a rhythm game's lanes. A finger landing in one presses act until it lifts (several fingers, several zones), and starts no stick\\n\\n## 6. Pixel fonts\\n\\n- `E.font`: Pixel fonts. 'normal' (default) is a 5x7 proportional font with lower case; 'tiny' is the 3x5 upper-case font. text(g, str, x, y, color, opts) where opts is an outline color, false, or { font: 'normal' | 'tiny', outline: '#0b0814' | false, shadow: '#000' (1 px drop shadow), gradient: ['#fff', '#8ad'] (top to bottom), scale: 1, align: 'left' | 'center' | 'right', wrap: maxWidthPx, lineGap } '\\\\n' starts a new line. Returns { w, h } of the drawn block. title(...) draws big logo text.\\n360→- `E.font.default` †: the font used when none is named: 'normal' (5 x 7) or 'tiny' (3 x 5)\\n- `E.font.height(lines = 1, o, lineGap)`: the height in pixels of that many lines (with o's scale and font, and lineGap between them)\\n- `E.font.lineHeight(o)`: pixels from one line to the next\\n- `E.font.text(g, s, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o = '#0b0814')`: draw text at (x, y) (its top-left; align: 'center' or 'right' moves it) in color. o: an outline color, or { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font }; returns { w, h }\\n- `E.font.title(g, s, x, y, o = {})`: Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge. title(g, 'CASTLE OF NIGHT', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: ['#fff6c8', '#ffd36a', '#e07a2a'], outline: '#1a0c10', depth: 3, depthColor: '#6a2a1a', align: 'center' })\\n- `E.font.width(s, o)`: width in pixels of the widest line. opts: a scale number or { font, scale }\\n- `E.font.wrap(s, maxW, o)`: split text into lines no wider than maxW pixels\\n\\n## 7. Lighting\\n\\n370→- `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity = 1, o)` †: radius in world units; intensity ~0.5..1.5. opts: { color: '#ffaa55' (both lightings), shadow: true (GPU lighting: walls and casters block it) }\\n- `game.lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` †: something that blocks light (GPU shadows): a cylinder of radius r from the floor up to height top\\n- `game.lights.heat(x, y, z, width, strength = 1)` †: heat shimmer above a point (GPU only). width in world units, strength ~0.5..1.5\\n- `game.lights.on` †: true when the canvas lighting draws this frame: enabled, or standing in for GPU lighting the game turned on that can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view); gpu.fallback = false keeps such a frame unlit\\n- `game.lights.refresh()` †: rebuild the darkness bands after changing dark, levels or glow (lights.dark = [r, g, b]; lights.refresh())\\n- `game.lights.render(r)` †: darken the frame by the lights (the renderer calls it after the queue, when enabled)\\n- `game.lights.ambient = .15` †: how lit unlit places are, 0 (black) .. 1\\n- `game.lights.dark = [10, 6, 24]` †: the color of darkness, [r, g, b]\\n- `game.lights.darkness = .9` †: how dark the darkest places get, 0..1 (the smooth style, E.style.trans 'alpha')\\n- `game.lights.enabled = false` †: false (the default) turns the canvas lighting off\\n380→- `game.lights.glow = [255, 200, 140, 0]` †: the color, [r, g, b, a], where light is brightest\\n- `game.lights.glowAt = 1.7` †: the light level (1 = fully lit) above which it glows\\n- `game.lights.levels = [.9, .7, .48, .26, .08, 0]` †: the darkness of each lighting band, darkest first (dithered between them)\\n- `game.lights.list = []` †: this frame's lights from add()\\n- `game.lights.screen = false` †: true measures lights on the screen, not the ground (side views and PlatformMaps do it by themselves)\\n- `game.lights.tint = .32` †: how strongly lights add their own color, 0..1\\n\\n## 8. Particles\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/API-INDEX.md\",\"offset\":269,\"limit\":120,\"raw_output\":\"## 5. Input\\n\\n- `new E.Input(screen, map, signal) → input`: signal: an AbortSignal that removes every listener this adds (game.dispose()). A screen without a canvas (headless) gets no DOM listeners: input comes from press(code), release(code), tap(action) and hold(action, on)\\n- `E.Input.DEFAULT { attack, dash, skill, jump }`: top-down / isometric action (Zelda, Diablo): Space dashes, J or click attacks\\n- `E.Input.PLATFORMER { jump, attack, dash, skill }`: side-scrollers (Mario, Mega Man): Space / Z / K jump, X / J attack or shoot, Shift / C dash\\n- `E.Input.SHMUP { fire, bomb }`: shoot-'em-ups (1942, Gradius): Space / Z / J fire (hold for autofire), X / K bomb\\n- `E.Input.THIRDPERSON { sprint, jump, crouch, interact, attack, block, dodge, use, map }` †: third-person action and stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear): WASD / stick move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch (a toggle), E interact, J attack, K block, L dodge, Q use, M map. Pad: A jump, B crouch, X attack, Y interact, LB block, RB dodge, LT use, RT or L3 sprint, Back map\\n- `E.Input.halfKeys(preset, side)` †: a preset for half a keyboard ('left' or 'right'): its moves and menu keys, the preset's other actions on its buttons in order, and the preset's gamepad codes (Input.halfKeys(Input.SHMUP, 'right') -> fire on . and Numpad 1, bomb on /). A preset's own halves come first: preset.halves = { left: { pass: ['KeyR'] }, right: { pass: ['KeyL'] } } (an action a menu reads beside confirm would take confirm's F otherwise)\\n- `input.anyPressed()`: any action pressed this step (title screens: \\\"press any button\\\")\\n- `input.bindButtons(root)` †: wire every element with data-act=\\\"<action>\\\" inside root to the action\\n- `input.bindings` †: the player's bindings over the preset: { action: { keys: [codes], pad: [codes] } } (game.settings.bindings keeps them)\\n- `input.box()` †: the pointer's gestures, read each step in screen pixels (the HUD's space), from a mouse or one finger: box() -> { x0, y0, x1, y1, from, to, done } while a box is drawn (a mouse's left button dragged; on a touch screen a long press, then a drag), done the step it is let go; else null\\n- `input.buffered(a, win = .15)`: was it pressed within the last `win` seconds and not yet consumed? (input buffering)\\n- `input.clear()`: Release all devices and discard buffered input on focus loss or a modal transition.\\n- `input.clicked()` †: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space: r.W, r.H), during the step after it lifted (a press that drags away, like the touch stick's, is none); else null. Menus and dialogs answer to it; a HUD button of your own can: if (hit(input.clicked())) ...\\n- `input.codeLabel(code)`: a code as a person reads it: 'W' (or the keyboard's own letter there), 'Space', '↑', 'Left click', and a gamepad button by its symbol on the controller in use ('A', '✕', 'B'...; glyphs picks the family)\\n- `input.consume(a)`: mark a buffered press of action a as used, so buffered(a) is false until the next press\\n- `input.consumeAll()`: forget every pending press (after closing a menu or dialog, so the same press does not also attack)\\n- `input.doubleTap()` †: [x, y] the step a second tap or click lands within .35 s and 8 pixels of the first (a double tap), else null\\n- `input.down(a)`: is the action held right now?\\n- `input.drag()` †: how far the touch stick's finger moved since the last step, in screen pixels [dx, dy] (the HUD's space), or null while no finger holds it: to steer one to one (a shooter's ship follows the thumb) instead of by the stick's direction\\n- `input.feed(type, x, y, o = {})` †: a pointer event in screen pixels, as the browser's would give: feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch: false, button: 0, id: 0 (which finger, for zones) }) (a test's or a bot's mouse and fingers: clicks, boxes, long presses, double taps and pans follow; a finger in one of the zones holds its act), or feed('zoom', x, y, { k: 1.1 }): a wheel's notch or a pinch about that point\\n- `input.history` †: the last second of input, oldest first: [{ t (input.clock), dir (numpad 1-9 for a fighter facing right: 5 neutral), pressed: the actions pressed then, directions aside }], one entry each time the direction changes or a button is pressed: an input display draws it; sequence() and motion() read it (section 10h: a kit without it has none)\\n- `input.hold(a, on = true)`: hold an action down (on) or let it go (false), as its key would\\n- `input.label(a, device = this.lastDevice)`: the key or button to show in a prompt for action a, on the device the player used last (or the one named: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'), with their bindings: input.label('jump') -> 'Space', 'A' or '✕'\\n- `input.longPress()` †: [x, y] the step a press held still has been held .45 s (a long press), else null\\n- `input.motion(digits, button, o = {})` †: a fighter's move in numpad notation, mirrored by facing: input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing, window }) is a quarter circle forward and punch (down, down-forward, forward, attack: a fireball); '623' a dragon punch, '41236' a half circle. facing: 1 (right, the default) or -1. button: an action, 'attack+kick', or none (the motion alone)\\n- `input.mouseScreen()`: mouse position in projected screen pixels, or null\\n- `input.move()`: movement intent in screen directions: x right, y down, length <= 1\\n- `input.padStyle()`: the controller family whose symbols label() shows: glyphs, or with glyphs 'auto' the one its name says\\n- `input.pan()` †: [dx, dy] how far a drag moved this step: one finger on open ground (not a box), or a mouse's middle or right button; else null. A free camera pans by it (game.cam.free)\\n- `input.pointer()` †: the mouse pointer in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space), or null before it has moved over the canvas\\n- `input.press(code)`: press a key or button code ('KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:attack') as if the person did: scripted input, tests, bots\\n- `input.pressed(a)`: true only during the first update step after the press (menus, jumps, one-shot actions)\\n- `input.rebind(a, device, code)` †: bind action a to one code for a device ('keys' = keyboard and mouse, 'pad' = gamepad), in place of that device's codes: input.rebind('attack', 'keys', 'KeyF'). Returns null when done, the action that already uses the code (nothing changes: free it first), or 'reserved' for the codes the menus keep (Escape, Enter, F keys, Start, the d-pad). The arrow keys and the d-pad always move as well\\n- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false } } })\\n- `input.release(code)`: let go of a code pressed with press()\\n- `input.released(a)`: true only during the first update step after the action was let go\\n- `input.repeat(a, delay = .3, rate = .08)`: pressed, then repeats while held (after `delay` s, every `rate` s): menu cursors, puzzle pieces\\n- `input.resetBindings()` †: forget the player's bindings: every action back to its preset's codes\\n- `input.rumble(strength = .5, seconds = .15)` †: shake the gamepad: strength 0..1 for seconds (scaled by rumbleScale; nothing where the controller can't). Returns true when it rumbled\\n- `input.screenPoint(cx, cy)` †: browser client coords -> screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space, with no camera), or null without a canvas\\n- `input.sequence(steps, o = {})` †: true on the step a sequence of inputs ends, each one after the last within window seconds (.5) of the first: input.sequence(['down', 'down+right', 'right', 'attack']). A step names actions joined by +: its directions are held exactly as it says (up, down, left, right; 'neutral' for none) as they change to that, its buttons pressed. A cheat code: input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })\\n- `input.stopRumble()` †: stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged)\\n- `input.suppress(a)` †: Suppress a held action until the next tick (a HUD widget owns the pointer this step).\\n- `input.suppressCode(code)` †: A widget owns this button; other devices bound to the same action still work.\\n- `input.tap(a)`: press an action for one update step, as a quick tap of its key (scripted input, tests, an AI): pressed() and down() are true in the next step, released() in the one after\\n- `input.tick(dt)`: advance input by one step: read the gamepad and turn presses since the last step into this step's pressed() and released() (the engine calls it every step)\\n- `input.touchArea()` †: where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself)\\n- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { jump: 'A', attack: 'B' }, force }). The other half of the screen is a move stick (past halfway it presses up, down, left and right too, as a d-pad does: menus and ladders). START is the corner's menu button, top right: it opens the menu, or pauses a pausable scene into it (without the corner a START button there starts a title and pauses, as a gamepad's Start)\\n- `input.touchSet(a, label = null, show = true)` †: one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('confirm', near ? 'TALK' : null, near || talking)\\n- `input.unbind(a, device)` †: take away action a's codes for one device ('keys' or 'pad'); the arrow keys and d-pad stay on the moves\\n- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, THIRDPERSON, SHMUP) has the moves (up, down, left, right) and the menu's actions (start, pause, confirm, cancel). The keys held stay held (down() in the new layout, not pressed() again), and a press no step has seen counts in it\\n- `input.value(a)` †: how far action a is pressed, 0 to 1: a gamepad button by its travel (the triggers, even short of a press), a stick's direction (PadAxisUp ..., or up / down / left / right on the touch stick) by its push, any other code held 1\\n- `input.zoom()` †: the camera zoom the player asked for since the last step, as a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther; 1: none): the wheel, - and = and a pinch, where the scene lets them zoom. The game applies it (game.zoomBy)\\n- `input.zoomPoint()` †: [x, y] where the last zoom was asked for: the mouse pointer for the wheel, between the fingers for a pinch; null when the keys zoomed (about the screen's middle)\\n- `input.aimSource = 'move'` †: what aims right now: 'move' | 'mouse' | 'pad'\\n- `input.clock = 0`: seconds of input time (the steps' dt added up)\\n- `input.deadzone = .18`: how far a gamepad stick moves before it counts (0..0.8)\\n- `input.glyphs = 'auto'`: the gamepad's button symbols for label(): 'auto' (from the controller's name) | 'xbox' | 'playstation' | 'nintendo' | 'generic' (positions)\\n- `input.held`: the key and button codes down right now ('KeyD', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump')\\n- `input.invertMoveX`, `input.invertMoveY = false` †: the move stick turned round (the Controller settings)\\n- `input.keysTo = null` †: the other players' inputs the keyboard's codes go to as well (players sharing it, each with half its keys: game.players)\\n- `input.lastDevice = 'keyboard'`: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch', the last one used (show the right button prompts)\\n- `input.map`: each action's codes ({ jump: ['Space', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump'] }); every action also answers to 'Act:<name>' (DOM buttons with data-act, tap, hold)\\n- `input.mouse`: { cx, cy } its page position, active once it has moved over the canvas, t the input clock when it last moved\\n- `input.names = null` †: the actions' names on the Controls screen ({ attack: 'Sword' }; false hides one from rebinding: the game's menu.controls); onBindings(b): called with the player's new bindings (the game saves and applies them)\\n- `input.pad = 'any'` †: the gamepad this input reads: 'any' (the first connected one another player hasn't: player 1's), an index (a joined player's own), or null (none)\\n- `input.padAim = null` †: its right stick, or null when centered\\n- `input.padAxes = []` †: the gamepad's raw axes\\n- `input.padId = ''`, `input.padIndex = null`: the gamepad in use (null and '' without one)\\n- `input.padLost = false`: true for the step after the gamepad in use was unplugged (a pausable scene pauses)\\n- `input.padMove = [0, 0]`: the gamepad's left stick after the dead zone\\n- `input.padPrev = []`: which gamepad buttons were down at the last step\\n- `input.padValues = []` †: each gamepad button's travel, 0 to 1 (the triggers; input.value reads them)\\n- `input.preset` †: the layout last given to use() (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, SHMUP or your own)\\n- `input.rumbleScale = .6` †: vibration strength 0..1 (0 = off; game.settings.rumble sets it)\\n- `input.screen`: the game's screen (mouse positions are read through it)\\n- `input.steer = 'stick'` †: 'stick' (a stick drawn under the thumb) or 'drag' (a finger anywhere on the screen but the buttons steers by drag(), with no stick drawn: a shooter's ship that follows the thumb)\\n- `input.stick = [0, 0]` †: the touch stick's direction [x, y], length <= 1\\n- `input.stickDeadzone = .12` †: the part of that radius a thumb moves before the stick counts (0..0.5)\\n- `input.stickId` †: the pointer (finger) holding the touch stick\\n- `input.stickOn = false` †: a finger is on it\\n- `input.stickRadius = 44` †: how far (CSS pixels) a finger drags for full tilt\\n- `input.stickSide = 'left'` †: 'left' or 'right', the half of the screen the stick lives on\\n- `input.touchEnabled = true` †: false turns the touch stick off\\n- `input.zones = null` †: areas of the screen a finger holds an action down in, [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }] (fractions of the canvas, 0..1; the first that holds the finger wins), or null: a pinball's two halves, a rhythm game's lanes. A finger landing in one presses act until it lifts (several fingers, several zones), and starts no stick\\n\\n## 6. Pixel fonts\\n\\n- `E.font`: Pixel fonts. 'normal' (default) is a 5x7 proportional font with lower case; 'tiny' is the 3x5 upper-case font. text(g, str, x, y, color, opts) where opts is an outline color, false, or { font: 'normal' | 'tiny', outline: '#0b0814' | false, shadow: '#000' (1 px drop shadow), gradient: ['#fff', '#8ad'] (top to bottom), scale: 1, align: 'left' | 'center' | 'right', wrap: maxWidthPx, lineGap } '\\\\n' starts a new line. Returns { w, h } of the drawn block. title(...) draws big logo text.\\n- `E.font.default` †: the font used when none is named: 'normal' (5 x 7) or 'tiny' (3 x 5)\\n- `E.font.height(lines = 1, o, lineGap)`: the height in pixels of that many lines (with o's scale and font, and lineGap between them)\\n- `E.font.lineHeight(o)`: pixels from one line to the next\\n- `E.font.text(g, s, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o = '#0b0814')`: draw text at (x, y) (its top-left; align: 'center' or 'right' moves it) in color. o: an outline color, or { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font }; returns { w, h }\\n- `E.font.title(g, s, x, y, o = {})`: Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge. title(g, 'CASTLE OF NIGHT', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: ['#fff6c8', '#ffd36a', '#e07a2a'], outline: '#1a0c10', depth: 3, depthColor: '#6a2a1a', align: 'center' })\\n- `E.font.width(s, o)`: width in pixels of the widest line. opts: a scale number or { font, scale }\\n- `E.font.wrap(s, maxW, o)`: split text into lines no wider than maxW pixels\\n\\n## 7. Lighting\\n\\n- `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity = 1, o)` †: radius in world units; intensity ~0.5..1.5. opts: { color: '#ffaa55' (both lightings), shadow: true (GPU lighting: walls and casters block it) }\\n- `game.lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` †: something that blocks light (GPU shadows): a cylinder of radius r from the floor up to height top\\n- `game.lights.heat(x, y, z, width, strength = 1)` †: heat shimmer above a point (GPU only). width in world units, strength ~0.5..1.5\\n- `game.lights.on` †: true when the canvas lighting draws this frame: enabled, or standing in for GPU lighting the game turned on that can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view); gpu.fallback = false keeps such a frame unlit\\n- `game.lights.refresh()` †: rebuild the darkness bands after changing dark, levels or glow (lights.dark = [r, g, b]; lights.refresh())\\n- `game.lights.render(r)` †: darken the frame by the lights (the renderer calls it after the queue, when enabled)\\n- `game.lights.ambient = .15` †: how lit unlit places are, 0 (black) .. 1\\n- `game.lights.dark = [10, 6, 24]` †: the color of darkness, [r, g, b]\\n- `game.lights.darkness = .9` †: how dark the darkest places get, 0..1 (the smooth style, E.style.trans 'alpha')\\n- `game.lights.enabled = false` †: false (the default) turns the canvas lighting off\\n- `game.lights.glow = [255, 200, 140, 0]` †: the color, [r, g, b, a], where light is brightest\\n- `game.lights.glowAt = 1.7` †: the light level (1 = fully lit) above which it glows\\n- `game.lights.levels = [.9, .7, .48, .26, .08, 0]` †: the darkness of each lighting band, darkest first (dithered between them)\\n- `game.lights.list = []` †: this frame's lights from add()\\n- `game.lights.screen = false` †: true measures lights on the screen, not the ground (side views and PlatformMaps do it by themselves)\\n- `game.lights.tint = .32` †: how strongly lights add their own color, 0..1\\n\\n## 8. Particles\\n\\n\",\"total_lines\":1767}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"bd5ed958-8349-4f34-a24d-05b8c38d4452"}
