4,101,32,116,111,111,108,115,32,105,110,32,116,111,111,108,115,47,59,32,99,108,105,112,115,32,109,97,121,32,98,101,32,101,100,105,116,101,100,32,98,121,32,104,97,110,100,41,46,10,50,48,58,40,119,105,110,100,111,119,46,77,79,67,65,80,32,61,32,119,105,110,100,111,119,46,77,79,67,65,80,32,124,124,32,123,125,41,91,34,70,73,84,67,76,73,80,83,34,93,32,61,32,123,10,50,49,58,34,115,101,116,34,58,32,34,70,73,84,67,76,73,80,83,34,44,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":3,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/vendor/fitclips.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":1,\"content\":\"/* FITCLIPS: an animation set in my-3D2dge's readable format (written by the tools in tools/; clips may be edited by hand).\"},{\"line_number\":20,\"content\":\"(window.MOCAP = window.MOCAP || {})[\\\"FITCLIPS\\\"] = {\"},{\"line_number\":21,\"content\":\"\\\"set\\\": \\\"FITCLIPS\\\",\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"00382057-bc55-49e2-8860-b39f9b80e0de"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_37","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"fitclips relies on `window.MOCAP`, which is available in the Node VM context.\n\nreadable.js and mocap.js should work in the VM context. I will patch the engine first, then write game files in large batches. The test runner structure is being considered to align the game API. G.bandTrial(name, skill, seed) returns { pct, falls } using Balance. Activity scene data includes tier, seed, skill, skipIntro, classMode, contestants, attract, and limit.\n\nSkill sets the local player to a real bot. skipIntro and attract bypass card, demo, and countdown screens. The snapshot object tracks scene, activity, tier, day, session, pct, stars, minutes, spineAge, lean, phase, freePlay, unlocks, saves, attract, dev, plateau, and hash. Tests will drive menus directly via a menu API. A G.Pills class is being drafted to manage input skipping.\n\nThe G.Pills class handles directional input, pointer hover, and confirm or cancel actions. Scripted playthroughs will auto-advance through menus by pressing confirm while activities run in the background.\n\nThe test will set `G.drive = 'good'` to auto-drive activities while the player still confirms through menus. During activity play, the test advances time and then presses Done.\n\nThe title screen flow is being designed so that confirm always advances. Continue is disabled for fresh games and starts focus at New Member. The test must tap down if it wants to start a new member.\n\nThe member screen will use pills for options. \"This is me\" will be the first item so one confirm advances to arrival. The test will tap down five times to reach it, ensuring real input while still allowing customization.\n\nThe member screen will have seven customization pills: hair, skin, top, bottom, nickname, reroll, and a final \"Begin\" pill. Confirm on the last item proceeds to arrival. The test will tap down repeatedly until it reaches Begin.\n\nFocus will start on \"This is me\" at the bottom of the member screen. Confirm cycles the selected customization option, while the default focus advances the player. The arrival scene uses a dialog that consumes confirmations; after it, the instructor pills appear with Birch focused, and one confirm selects Birch to continue.\n\nThe arrival dialog will auto-advance after a short delay, so the test can tap confirm repeatedly until the scene changes. The bungalow screen focuses on the scale; confirming proceeds to bodycheck. Bodycheck runs automatically for about fifty seconds, then presents result pills; the test advances past it and confirms until reaching the island. The island screen suggests going to Stork Stand, and confirming starts the card. The card screen focuses on Begin.\n\nCard confirm proceeds through demo, countdown, and play. Play runs until time elapses, then results appear with Done focused. Confirming Done returns to the island and adds minutes. After three sessions the island reports the day is done; confirming advances to evening. Evening shows a recap, then bed leads to the next day's bungalow. On day five the bungalow confirms into gentle games instead of the normal island; four short activities run before the ending. The ending dialog and credits follow, then the test reaches free play or title with free play unlocked. Day three body check preserves spine age.\n\nDay three will store `state.spineHeld` and set spine age to yesterday's value after computing. Each day runs three guided story activities in order from the unlocked pool, with Stork forced as session one on day one. The scripted playthrough must cover all twelve story activities across five days plus day five games.\n\nThe scripted playthrough will cover all twelve activities across nine story sessions. Day one unlocks Stork, Squat, Step, and Tray; day two unlocks Sun, Push, Hoop, and Laundry; day three unlocks Candle, Lunge, Jog, and Slalom. Day four uses tier-two activities, and day five runs gentle games plus a duel. The remaining three activities will be played during free play.\n\nThe scripted playthrough will use exactly twelve sessions across days one through four. Day one forces Stork and two other unlocked activities. Days two through four fill the remaining nine slots with the remaining unlocked activities. This covers all twelve activities without needing day five or free play.\n\nThe twelve activities are numbered 1 through 12. Day one unlocks only Stork, Squat, Step, and Tray, so it can hold at most three sessions. The fourth activity must be deferred to a later day.\n\nThe twelve-session schedule is finalized. Day one plays Stork, Squat, and Step. Day two plays Tray, Sun, and Push. Day three plays Hoop, Laundry, and Candle. Day four plays Lunge, Jog, and Slalom. This covers all unlocked activities exactly once. The test will follow this guided sequence by confirming through each day. Tier-two unlock on day four is a separate verification.\n\nThe scripted playthrough will cover tier-two activities to verify the unlock rule. A separate test will check that tier-two is locked before two stars and opens on day four.\n\nDay five gentle games will run at twenty seconds each, while the duel uses thirty seconds. Free play retains full durations.\n\nStar gate tests will use full activity durations. Story gentle games can run the real activity code with a `limit` override.\n\nA passive playthrough will follow the same path but without the auto-driver, scoring low while still finishing.\n\nThe star gate test is the heaviest workload. It requires 72 runs across tier one and tier two at three skill levels each, totaling roughly 518,000 steps.\n\nActivity steps will be optimized to run at 0.02 ms or 0.2 ms by skipping rig updates during headless scoring. The star gate test will sample at full rate because physics require it. The 518,000 balance steps are only about 0.1 seconds of simulation; the real cost is engine advance overhead and rig processing.\n\nBody check will run for about fifteen seconds instead of fifty, using five seconds of stillness, four seconds of leaning, and instant question answers on confirm. This keeps the test from being tedious while still covering the required real-time activity.\n\nThe body check will use exactly five seconds of stillness, four one-second leans, and reading time, keeping the total near fifteen seconds. The exit interview will be written after testing results are known, with a placeholder in the game that the build step replaces from exit-interview.json. CSS for the shell will be added to index.html and the build output.\n\nA canonical `exit-interview.json` will hold the final about text, duplicated into the game source as a default so the build can overwrite it. The test will verify that the built JSON matches this canonical file. I will add PROGRESS updates as I go, patch the dialog now, and generate a substantial `lines.js` with over 150 lines. A `DEV` object with keys and defaults will provide runtime dev sliders.\n\nThe dev slider logic will use `adjust(key, dir)` to step values and `reset` to restore defaults. Balance reads these live values from `G.dev.live` each step. The band test will instantiate a fresh Balance with explicit table parameters rather than the dev-modified ones, ensuring it validates the shipped defaults.\n\nBalance will read dev overrides from `G.dev` during play and use explicit default parameters for band trials. A `G.dev.jump` helper will let tests skip directly to any scene, activity, tier, or day. An attract playlist scene will cycle through all activities with an expert bot and no stumbles; each item lasts fifteen seconds, so the full loop covers roughly twenty-four hundred seconds of simulation. The test will verify that every playlist item is visited. This mode must not alter saves or unlock states.\n\nThe attract playlist will run inside a copy of game state so saves, unlocks, and best stars remain untouched. During this mode, save and unlock functions no-op and activities skip awarding stars. Any input during attract is consumed by the hook before the scene sees it, returning to the title screen.\n\nThe attract playlist will use a flag set by the input hook to prevent the current frame's scene from processing input, since consumeAll may not clear it immediately. The idle timer must also avoid floating-point drift: 9.9 seconds summed over 120 steps of 1/120 does not equal exactly 10, so the threshold needs an epsilon or exact binary representation.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a7889a19-6048-4641-8254-3c3674bf201f-60","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"advance\\(seconds","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7178,"output_tokens":3720,"cache_read_input_tokens":170624,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"47732df0-128b-4b1a-8953-61235a38463c"}
