{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-6dd9ce66-b1c9-4387-8611-4fb09d29971a-61","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"2835→  }\\n  /**\\n   * run the game for `seconds` of real time in exact fixed steps, at once, without requestAnimationFrame: tests, tools and\\n   * headless runs (advance(1) is 120 updates). o.draw: then draw one frame (not in a headless game). A headless game made\\n   * with draw: true draws one frame each advance, on a stand-in renderer, so a draw error lands in game.errors (a test\\n2840→   * that catches what a player would see break). Returns the updates run\\n   */\\n  advance(seconds = this.o.step, o = {}) {\\n    if (!this.scene && !this._next) throw new Error('game.advance() needs a started game: game.start({ update, draw }) first');\\n    this._acc += Math.max(0, seconds);\\n    const n = this._steps(); this.stats.steps = n;\\n    if (this.r && (this.headless ? this.o.draw : o.draw)) try { this._frame(); } catch (e) { this.fail('draw', e); }\\n    return n;\\n  }\\n  /**\\n2850→   * the clock: an exact fixed step. Real time (at most 50 ms a frame, so a stall can't run away) fills an accumulator;\\n   * each whole o.step of it (1/120 s) runs one update of o.step * timeScale game seconds (split in two or more when\\n   * timeScale > 1, so no step is longer than o.step), and the remainder waits for the next frame (game.alpha = its\\n   * fraction of a step). 60 Hz runs two steps a frame, 120 Hz one, 144 Hz one on most frames: the same steps at any rate.\\n   * Slow motion makes steps shorter, never fewer. Paused or at timeScale 0 the steps still run (dt 0), so input ticks.\\n   * The first frame always updates (with dt 0 when its time is short of a step), so draw never comes before update.\\n   * Returns the updates run\\n   */\\n  _run(real) { this._acc += Math.min(.05, real); return this._steps(); }\\n  /** run the whole steps the accumulator holds (see _run) */\\n2860→  _steps() {\\n    const step = this.o.step; let n = 0;\\n    try {\\n      while (this._acc > step - 1e-9) {   // (the tolerance absorbs the rounding in frame times: 1/60 s is two steps)\\n        this._acc -= step; this._wall += step;   // (_wall: seconds stepped through, unscaled: slow motion or a stop doesn't hold up the player's zoom)\",\"content_concise\":\"2835→  }\\n  /**\\n   * run the game for `seconds` of real time in exact fixed steps, at once, without requestAnimationFrame: tests, tools and\\n   * headless runs (advance(1) is 120 updates). o.draw: then draw one frame (not in a headless game). A headless game made\\n   * with draw: true draws one frame each advance, on a stand-in renderer, so a draw error lands in game.errors (a test\\n2840→   * that catches what a player would see break). Returns the updates run\\n   */\\n  advance(seconds = this.o.step, o = {}) {\\n    if (!this.scene && !this._next) throw new Error('game.advance() needs a started game: game.start({ update, draw }) first');\\n    this._acc += Math.max(0, seconds);\\n    const n = this._steps(); this.stats.steps = n;\\n    if (this.r && (this.headless ? this.o.draw : o.draw)) try { this._frame(); } catch (e) { this.fail('draw', e); }\\n    return n;\\n  }\\n  /**\\n2850→   * the clock: an exact fixed step. Real time (at most 50 ms a frame, so a stall can't run away) fills an accumulator;\\n   * each whole o.step of it (1/120 s) runs one update of o.step * timeScale game seconds (split in two or more when\\n   * timeScale > 1, so no step is longer than o.step), and the remainder waits for the next frame (game.alpha = its\\n   * fraction of a step). 60 Hz runs two steps a frame, 120 Hz one, 144 Hz one on most frames: the same steps at any rate.\\n   * Slow motion makes steps shorter, never fewer. Paused or at timeScale 0 the steps still run (dt 0), so input ticks.\\n   * The first frame always updates (with dt 0 when its time is short of a step), so draw never comes before update.\\n   * Returns the updates run\\n   */\\n  _run(real) { this._acc += Math.min(.05, real); return this._steps(); }\\n  /** run the whole steps the accumulator holds (see _run) */\\n2860→  _steps() {\\n    const step = this.o.step; let n = 0;\\n    try {\\n      while (this._acc > step - 1e-9) {   // (the tolerance absorbs the rounding in frame times: 1/60 s is two steps)\\n        this._acc -= step; this._wall += step;   // (_wall: seconds stepped through, unscaled: slow motion or a stop doesn't hold up the player's zoom)\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js\",\"offset\":2835,\"limit\":30,\"raw_output\":\"  }\\n  /**\\n   * run the game for `seconds` of real time in exact fixed steps, at once, without requestAnimationFrame: tests, tools and\\n   * headless runs (advance(1) is 120 updates). o.draw: then draw one frame (not in a headless game). A headless game made\\n   * with draw: true draws one frame each advance, on a stand-in renderer, so a draw error lands in game.errors (a test\\n   * that catches what a player would see break). Returns the updates run\\n   */\\n  advance(seconds = this.o.step, o = {}) {\\n    if (!this.scene && !this._next) throw new Error('game.advance() needs a started game: game.start({ update, draw }) first');\\n    this._acc += Math.max(0, seconds);\\n    const n = this._steps(); this.stats.steps = n;\\n    if (this.r && (this.headless ? this.o.draw : o.draw)) try { this._frame(); } catch (e) { this.fail('draw', e); }\\n    return n;\\n  }\\n  /**\\n   * the clock: an exact fixed step. Real time (at most 50 ms a frame, so a stall can't run away) fills an accumulator;\\n   * each whole o.step of it (1/120 s) runs one update of o.step * timeScale game seconds (split in two or more when\\n   * timeScale > 1, so no step is longer than o.step), and the remainder waits for the next frame (game.alpha = its\\n   * fraction of a step). 60 Hz runs two steps a frame, 120 Hz one, 144 Hz one on most frames: the same steps at any rate.\\n   * Slow motion makes steps shorter, never fewer. Paused or at timeScale 0 the steps still run (dt 0), so input ticks.\\n   * The first frame always updates (with dt 0 when its time is short of a step), so draw never comes before update.\\n   * Returns the updates run\\n   */\\n  _run(real) { this._acc += Math.min(.05, real); return this._steps(); }\\n  /** run the whole steps the accumulator holds (see _run) */\\n  _steps() {\\n    const step = this.o.step; let n = 0;\\n    try {\\n      while (this._acc > step - 1e-9) {   // (the tolerance absorbs the rounding in frame times: 1/60 s is two steps)\\n        this._acc -= step; this._wall += step;   // (_wall: seconds stepped through, unscaled: slow motion or a stop doesn't hold up the player's zoom)\\n\",\"total_lines\":14974}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"752a5bc3-43ef-4660-9e51-8668a4d0a17a"}
