{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-95003a24-3de1-4c9f-a5fd-cc931eacf11b-66","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"2788→  _boot(hooks) {\\n    const o = hooks.boot || {}, h = Object.assign({}, hooks); delete h.boot;\\n2790→    this._booting = true; this._bootOpt = o;\\n    const live = !this.headless && this.o.loop !== false && hooks.boot !== false && typeof document !== 'undefined';\\n    const steps = (Array.isArray(o.steps) ? o.steps : []).filter(Boolean).map(x => Array.isArray(x) ? x : [x.label || 'Getting ready', x.run]);\\n    if (hooks.boot === false) { BOOT.played = true; const el = typeof document !== 'undefined' && document.getElementById('e3-boot'); if (el) { el.hidden = true; el.style.display = 'none'; } BOOT.el = null; return this.start(h); }\\n    let res; this.booted = new Promise(r => { res = r; });\\n    if (live) { BOOT._open(this, o); BOOT._checks(this); }\\n    const now = () => typeof performance !== 'undefined' ? performance.now() : 0, t0 = now(), n = steps.length;   // (no clock in a bare Node context)\\n    let i = 0;\\n    const fail = (label, e) => { this._booting = false; this.bootError = e; this.fail('boot step \\\"' + label + '\\\"', e); BOOT.fail('boot step \\\"' + label + '\\\"', e); res(this); };\\n    const next = () => {\\n2800→      if (this.disposed) return;\\n      while (i < n) {\\n        const [label, fn] = steps[i++], s0 = now();\\n        if (live) BOOT._step(i, n, label);\\n        let r; try { r = typeof fn === 'function' ? fn(this) : null; } catch (e) { fail(label, e); return; }\\n        const ok = () => { if (live) BOOT._line('  ' + (i + '. ').padEnd(4), label + ' · ' + Math.round(now() - s0) + ' ms'); };\\n        if (r && typeof r.then === 'function') { r.then(() => { ok(); live ? requestAnimationFrame(next) : next(); }, e => fail(label, e)); return; }\\n        ok(); if (live) { requestAnimationFrame(next); return; }   // one step a frame: the screen shows each\\n      }\\n      if (live && o.min && now() - t0 < o.min * 1000) { requestAnimationFrame(next); return; }\\n2810→      if (live) BOOT._step(n + 1, n + 1, 'Warming up');\\n      this._booting = false; const bg = this._bootGo; this._bootGo = null;\\n      if (bg && h.scenes && h.scenes[bg.name]) Object.assign(h, { scene: bg.name, data: bg.data }); else if (bg) warn('go:' + bg.name, 'game.go(\\\"' + bg.name + '\\\") in a boot step: no such scene. Scenes: ' + Object.keys(h.scenes || {}).join(', '));\\n      this.start(h); res(this);\\n    };\\n    next();\\n    return this;\\n  }\\n  /** stop the loop; the game keeps its state, and start() resumes it */\\n  stop() { this._running = false; if (this._raf != null && typeof cancelAnimationFrame === 'function') cancelAnimationFrame(this._raf); this._raf = null; return this; }\\n2820→  /**\\n   * end the game for good: stop the loop and remove everything the engine added to the page (its listeners on the window,\\n   * the document, the canvas and the buttons, the touch controls, the GPU lighting's canvas) and close the sound. Tests and\\n   * tools that make many games call it; the canvas stays\\n   */\\n  dispose() {\\n    /** disposed: true after dispose() */\\n    this.stop(); this.disposed = true;\",\"content_concise\":\"2788→  _boot(hooks) {\\n    const o = hooks.boot || {}, h = Object.assign({}, hooks); delete h.boot;\\n2790→    this._booting = true; this._bootOpt = o;\\n    const live = !this.headless && this.o.loop !== false && hooks.boot !== false && typeof document !== 'undefined';\\n    const steps = (Array.isArray(o.steps) ? o.steps : []).filter(Boolean).map(x => Array.isArray(x) ? x : [x.label || 'Getting ready', x.run]);\\n    if (hooks.boot === false) { BOOT.played = true; const el = typeof document !== 'undefined' && document.getElementById('e3-boot'); if (el) { el.hidden = true; el.style.display = 'none'; } BOOT.el = null; return this.start(h); }\\n    let res; this.booted = new Promise(r => { res = r; });\\n    if (live) { BOOT._open(this, o); BOOT._checks(this); }\\n    const now = () => typeof performance !== 'undefined' ? performance.now() : 0, t0 = now(), n = steps.length;   // (no clock in a bare Node context)\\n    let i = 0;\\n    const fail = (label, e) => { this._booting = false; this.bootError = e; this.fail('boot step \\\"' + label + '\\\"', e); BOOT.fail('boot step \\\"' + label + '\\\"', e); res(this); };\\n    const next = () => {\\n2800→      if (this.disposed) return;\\n      while (i < n) {\\n        const [label, fn] = steps[i++], s0 = now();\\n        if (live) BOOT._step(i, n, label);\\n        let r; try { r = typeof fn === 'function' ? fn(this) : null; } catch (e) { fail(label, e); return; }\\n        const ok = () => { if (live) BOOT._line('  ' + (i + '. ').padEnd(4), label + ' · ' + Math.round(now() - s0) + ' ms'); };\\n        if (r && typeof r.then === 'function') { r.then(() => { ok(); live ? requestAnimationFrame(next) : next(); }, e => fail(label, e)); return; }\\n        ok(); if (live) { requestAnimationFrame(next); return; }   // one step a frame: the screen shows each\\n      }\\n      if (live && o.min && now() - t0 < o.min * 1000) { requestAnimationFrame(next); return; }\\n2810→      if (live) BOOT._step(n + 1, n + 1, 'Warming up');\\n      this._booting = false; const bg = this._bootGo; this._bootGo = null;\\n      if (bg && h.scenes && h.scenes[bg.name]) Object.assign(h, { scene: bg.name, data: bg.data }); else if (bg) warn('go:' + bg.name, 'game.go(\\\"' + bg.name + '\\\") in a boot step: no such scene. Scenes: ' + Object.keys(h.scenes || {}).join(', '));\\n      this.start(h); res(this);\\n    };\\n    next();\\n    return this;\\n  }\\n  /** stop the loop; the game keeps its state, and start() resumes it */\\n  stop() { this._running = false; if (this._raf != null && typeof cancelAnimationFrame === 'function') cancelAnimationFrame(this._raf); this._raf = null; return this; }\\n2820→  /**\\n   * end the game for good: stop the loop and remove everything the engine added to the page (its listeners on the window,\\n   * the document, the canvas and the buttons, the touch controls, the GPU lighting's canvas) and close the sound. Tests and\\n   * tools that make many games call it; the canvas stays\\n   */\\n  dispose() {\\n    /** disposed: true after dispose() */\\n    this.stop(); this.disposed = true;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js\",\"offset\":2788,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"  _boot(hooks) {\\n    const o = hooks.boot || {}, h = Object.assign({}, hooks); delete h.boot;\\n    this._booting = true; this._bootOpt = o;\\n    const live = !this.headless && this.o.loop !== false && hooks.boot !== false && typeof document !== 'undefined';\\n    const steps = (Array.isArray(o.steps) ? o.steps : []).filter(Boolean).map(x => Array.isArray(x) ? x : [x.label || 'Getting ready', x.run]);\\n    if (hooks.boot === false) { BOOT.played = true; const el = typeof document !== 'undefined' && document.getElementById('e3-boot'); if (el) { el.hidden = true; el.style.display = 'none'; } BOOT.el = null; return this.start(h); }\\n    let res; this.booted = new Promise(r => { res = r; });\\n    if (live) { BOOT._open(this, o); BOOT._checks(this); }\\n    const now = () => typeof performance !== 'undefined' ? performance.now() : 0, t0 = now(), n = steps.length;   // (no clock in a bare Node context)\\n    let i = 0;\\n    const fail = (label, e) => { this._booting = false; this.bootError = e; this.fail('boot step \\\"' + label + '\\\"', e); BOOT.fail('boot step \\\"' + label + '\\\"', e); res(this); };\\n    const next = () => {\\n      if (this.disposed) return;\\n      while (i < n) {\\n        const [label, fn] = steps[i++], s0 = now();\\n        if (live) BOOT._step(i, n, label);\\n        let r; try { r = typeof fn === 'function' ? fn(this) : null; } catch (e) { fail(label, e); return; }\\n        const ok = () => { if (live) BOOT._line('  ' + (i + '. ').padEnd(4), label + ' · ' + Math.round(now() - s0) + ' ms'); };\\n        if (r && typeof r.then === 'function') { r.then(() => { ok(); live ? requestAnimationFrame(next) : next(); }, e => fail(label, e)); return; }\\n        ok(); if (live) { requestAnimationFrame(next); return; }   // one step a frame: the screen shows each\\n      }\\n      if (live && o.min && now() - t0 < o.min * 1000) { requestAnimationFrame(next); return; }\\n      if (live) BOOT._step(n + 1, n + 1, 'Warming up');\\n      this._booting = false; const bg = this._bootGo; this._bootGo = null;\\n      if (bg && h.scenes && h.scenes[bg.name]) Object.assign(h, { scene: bg.name, data: bg.data }); else if (bg) warn('go:' + bg.name, 'game.go(\\\"' + bg.name + '\\\") in a boot step: no such scene. Scenes: ' + Object.keys(h.scenes || {}).join(', '));\\n      this.start(h); res(this);\\n    };\\n    next();\\n    return this;\\n  }\\n  /** stop the loop; the game keeps its state, and start() resumes it */\\n  stop() { this._running = false; if (this._raf != null && typeof cancelAnimationFrame === 'function') cancelAnimationFrame(this._raf); this._raf = null; return this; }\\n  /**\\n   * end the game for good: stop the loop and remove everything the engine added to the page (its listeners on the window,\\n   * the document, the canvas and the buttons, the touch controls, the GPU lighting's canvas) and close the sound. Tests and\\n   * tools that make many games call it; the canvas stays\\n   */\\n  dispose() {\\n    /** disposed: true after dispose() */\\n    this.stop(); this.disposed = true;\\n\",\"total_lines\":14974}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"697e3ba1-40f9-4668-907c-e6098c54dc62"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_44","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Headless boot runs all steps synchronously before invoking start().\n\n`game.booted` resolves as a microtask after `start()` returns, so the game is already started when that promise settles.\n\nShell theme and hover styling will be added to core.js after game creation. check.mjs presses Enter twice on the title screen, which should proceed through New Member and arrival without errors. WASD movement and key presses in that script are safe. If game.views is null, cycling all views could be slow or error-prone.\n\n`game.views` will be set to `['brawler', 'threequarter', 'side']` after start. This limits the checker to three views instead of cycling through all twenty. Scenes can override this property on entry.\n\nEach scene will declare only its own view, leaving the short `game.views` list untouched. The checker's `setView` calls are safe because draw uses `r.actor` and `r.sky`, which render correctly in every camera mode.\n\nThe canvas CSS is set to 100% width and height. The engine should render the ps1 buffer rather than the default 300×150. The launch test requires a drawing surface that is not the default size and is substantially painted.\n\nThe launch test will load the built HTML via file://, wait for the scene title, and verify no page errors occur. It will confirm the canvas is not 300×150, read non-blank pixels, check that title text is visible, and ensure key presses move focus. The engine’s ps1 resolution should resize the canvas backing store to a non-default size. I also need to inspect screen.setOptions to understand how canvas dimensions are configured.\n\nThe engine should handle canvas sizing correctly when CSS is 100% and a viewport is present. I am now writing the code in parallel batches, with the core boot script last. I have defined the initial player state object, including slot, name, look, instructor, day, sessions, played history, minutes, unlocks, spine level, and plateau status.\n\nThe state object now includes flags for suggestion mode, free play, endings, duel wins, tips, gentle mode, breath mode, story index, and unlock progress. The unlock list is being defined with minute thresholds for each item.\n\nThe unlock list now includes a friend unlock at thirty minutes. A spineAge function is being defined to compute the character's age from four discipline scores, each derived by averaging the best tier one stars across its exercises and then averaging those disciplines.\n\nThe spineAge function now subtracts one year if the player has won a duel. A plateau mechanism is being added for day three: the spine age from the end of day two will be stored and held steady, while day four catches up by recalculating from all best scores.\n\nThe `award` function now records stars per tier, adds minutes scaled by star count and suggestion bonus, and updates spine age. On day three it uses the held age instead of recalculating. A test will verify that the spine age stays identical to the previous day.\n\nTier two unlocks require tier one completion, day four or later, or two tier one stars. The twelve guided exercises are listed in G.STORY. Sessions three through five mark day two, six through eight day three, and nine through eleven day four; the story index advances at each session boundary. After the fourth evening, day five begins and the bungalow shifts from island to games. A free play scene will be added for the island with all activities. Audio songs will be short original patterns.\n\nSound effects and a babble function are being defined. Each NPC character now has a unique voice profile with base pitch, playback speed, waveform type, and vibrato.\n\nVoice profiles for Lou and the Twin are now defined. The babble function guards against a null audio context. A plate overlay will display the spine age, and the dev panel will sit on the left third of the screen when open; pointer intent checks that the cursor is past the panel width to avoid leaning. A keyboard nudge rate of 2.2 per second is stored in `dev.live.nudge`. A new `readIntent` function in balance.js will interpret device input.\n\nThe `readIntent` function now maps gamepad input to horizontal and vertical movement, and mouse input to lean by normalizing pointer position against screen width and height using a configurable reach threshold.\n\nMouse input now clamps the lean value to a stored `_mouseU` state. Keyboard nudge drives the lean toward the movement direction at 2.2 per second while keys are held, and drifts home at 0.5 per second when released. The function also reads analog breath input.\n\n`readIntent` now accepts `dt` so keyboard nudge can interpolate by frame. The mouse lean value persists between frames, while gamepad stick input is read as absolute each frame. A new `G.device` helper detects the active input source by comparing mouse touch time against the last keyboard press time.\n\nA hash function will make runs deterministic by hashing position samples from a scripted input sequence. The class podium will rank scores from zero to one thousand and assign titles. Morning hub events are being added for the third class.\n\nHub events are grouped into three pools: stork, candle, squat, and push; step and hoop; slalom and jog. Each event lasts 150 seconds, split between a five-second intro, forty seconds of play, and a five-second scores phase. Timing is driven by AIHub.time(). Ghosts are sent via send. When time expires, the result is delivered once, then input and music stop. If the time limit is under 150 seconds, events shrink to fit. The card scene stays open while time is negative infinity, so the harness can press Enter to answer ready without waiting for a key press.\n\nThe harness will connect in ghost mode with seats 1 and 4, then call ready() after boot. During the hub event it will send ghost state roughly every ten seconds and progress roughly every second. The harness expects a single result on time-up, followed by input and audio being cleared. Outside of an active session, AIHub stays inactive and the game remains at the title screen; session.js is the first script in both the built bundle and the dev index.html.\n\nThe harness is tests/hub/harness.html. The built game.html must be self-contained with session.js inside. build.mjs writes to the root HTML path and copies to tests/hub/game.html. The dev folder is Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108. The HTML file sits in the work root parent as Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108.html. Meta tags will match the specification. No TODO, placeholder, or stub strings will appear in game sources.\n\nThe tiny font is banned from the UI. A data README will be added. The dialog patch will be written before the remaining large files.\n\nActivities using E.Rhythm, E.Mode7, E.Road, and E.Physics will be created in setup(ctx) because their update methods receive the game instance. Rhythm charts will be generated from data so bots can hit notes by checking rhythm.ahead and pressing when the time delta falls within the skill window. Headless audio position tracking remains functional for rhythm timing. For bot contestants without real input, the activity will call rhythm.hit(lane) directly rather than relying on input actions.\n\nA deterministic rhythm bot will hit every note with a fixed skill-based offset. Poor skill is 100 ms late, average 40 ms, good 20 ms, and expert 10 ms. Local players still use real input through rhythm.hit.\n\nThe bot's fixed offsets guarantee tier-two accuracy. A deterministic rhythm bot will call the real rhythm.hit method at the correct music-clock offset for each note. The step class activity may instead score hits internally while still drawing the rhythm chart.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-84a68c4f-e971-4390-bb50-97798532d030-67","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/kits/rhythm.js","limit":120}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1047,"output_tokens":4334,"cache_read_input_tokens":186240,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"0dcb4d16-b7d7-4e55-80c0-53adbb0af93e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-84a68c4f-e971-4390-bb50-97798532d030-67","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→// The RHYTHM kit's game (four lanes on the music clock: two songs, notes, jumps, holds, a rapping teacher to answer, a dancer and a crowd on the beat, grades, the audio offset), readable: the starter's shell (src/starter/00-shell.js), then\\n// the slice from line 159 (src/starter/37-rhythm.js: its header says what it plays and how), then the start (90-start.js), as the\\n// single-file kit holds them between GAME START and GAME END. rhythm.html plays it; node kits/test.mjs rhythm runs it headless.\\n/* ============================== GAME START ==============================\\n * MY-3D2DGE STARTER: a title menu and vertical slices, one per genre (a genre kit file holds one; the file with every\\n * slice adds an animation lab). Each slice is a complete small game, written to be copied and changed: its own header\\n * (below this shell) says what it plays, its keys and touch controls, its deep links and how it works.\\n * FULL SCREEN, ANY SHAPE: res: 'ps1', expand: true. 320 x 240 is the least the screen shows; a wider or taller window\\n * shows more world (r.W x r.H grows), so every HUD anchors to r.W and r.H, never to 320 and 240, and every level fills\\n10→ * what a wide screen or an upright phone shows. Prompts name the player's own keys: prompt('attack', 'Sword').\\n * To make your own game: change the slice closest to your genre into yours, with its own title, play and game-over\\n * scenes, and delete this menu and the slices you do not use; this shell (the game, its scenes, prompt, best and the\\n * proof) stays unless you replace it. Deep links: #<slice> starts a slice, #<slice>/<part> jumps into a part.\\n * ======================================================================== */\\n(() => {\\n'use strict';\\nconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp, lerp, approach, TAU } = E;\\n\\n/* ---- SHELL: one game, several scenes. Each slice switches view, keys and resolution on entry ----\\n20→ * expand: the picture fills the window, any shape, with no bars: at least 320 x 240 (a slice's res), more on the long side */\\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'side', bg: '#07060d' });\\nconst P = game.particles, L = game.lights, A = game.audio;\\nconst scenes = {};\\nlet gpu = null;                               // WebGPU lighting, used by the adventure slice only\\nconst best = (k, v) => { const old = E.store.get('best:' + k, 0); if (v > old) E.store.set('best:' + k, v); return Math.max(old, v); };\\n// a prompt in the player's own controls, after rebinding: 'J Sword' on a keyboard, 'X Sword' on a pad, 'ATTACK Sword' (the\\n// button's name) on a touch screen, where the buttons sit over the bottom corner: touch() says so, to move a prompt off it\\nconst prompt = (a, word) => game.input.label(a) + ' ' + word, touch = () => game.input.lastDevice === 'touch';\\n// each slice: its scene's id, its title entry, a line about it, and its family (the title groups them under it)\\n30→const SLICES = [\\n  { id: 'adventure', label: 'ADVENTURE', about: 'TOP-DOWN ACTION ADVENTURE', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\\n  { id: 'pointclick', label: 'POINT-AND-CLICK', about: 'RELIGHT THE LIGHTHOUSE ON GULL ROCK', family: 'ADVENTURE' },\\n  { id: 'platformer', label: 'PLATFORMER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING PLATFORMER', family: 'PLATFORM' },\\n  { id: 'metroidvania', label: 'METROIDVANIA', about: 'A WORLD OF ROOMS, ITS MAP, ITS GATES', family: 'PLATFORM' },\\n  { id: 'brawler', label: 'BRAWLER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING BEAT-EM-UP', family: 'FIGHTING' },\\n  { id: 'fighting', label: 'FIGHTING', about: 'ONE ON ONE, BEST OF THREE', family: 'FIGHTING' },\\n  { id: 'shooter', label: 'SHOOTER', about: 'VERTICAL SHOOT-EM-UP', family: 'ARCADE' },\\n  { id: 'arcade', label: 'ARCADE', about: 'A CABINET OF THREE CLASSICS', family: 'ARCADE' },\\n  { id: 'pinball', label: 'PINBALL', about: 'ONE TABLE: RAMP, MULTIBALL, JACKPOT', family: 'ARCADE' },\\n40→  { id: 'kart', label: 'KART', about: 'MODE 7 KARTS, THREE LAPS', family: 'RACING' },\\n  { id: 'road', label: 'ROAD', about: 'A COAST ROAD AGAINST THE CLOCK', family: 'RACING' },\\n  { id: 'puzzle', label: 'PUZZLE', about: 'FALLING BLOCKS AND FALLING PAIRS', family: 'PUZZLE' },\\n  { id: 'rhythm', label: 'RHYTHM', about: 'STEP UP! FOUR LANES, TWO SONGS', family: 'MUSIC' },\\n  { id: 'rpg', label: 'RPG', about: 'A JOURNEY: TOWN, WORLD, CAVE, BATTLES', family: 'RPG' },\\n  { id: 'roguelike', label: 'ROGUELIKE', about: 'A CRYPT OF TEN GENERATED FLOORS', family: 'RPG' },\\n  { id: 'tactics', label: 'TACTICS', about: 'TAKE THE HILL FORT, TURN BY TURN', family: 'STRATEGY' },\\n  { id: 'rts', label: 'RTS', about: 'GATHER GOLD, RAISE AN ARMY, BURN THEIR CAMP', family: 'STRATEGY' },\\n  { id: 'artillery', label: 'ARTILLERY', about: 'TWO TEAMS OF THREE ON AN ISLAND THAT BLOWS APART', family: 'STRATEGY' },\\n  { id: 'sim', label: 'SIM', about: 'BUILD A THEME PARK, KEEP THEM SMILING', family: 'SIMULATION' },\\n50→  { id: 'stealth', label: 'STEALTH', about: 'ROOFTOP STEALTH AND PARKOUR', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\\n  { id: 'sports', label: 'SPORTS', about: 'TWO AGAINST TWO IN THE CAGE', family: 'SPORTS' },\\n  { id: 'animlab', label: 'ANIMATION LAB', about: 'EVERY MOVE, SKIN AND VIEW', family: 'TOOLS' }\\n];\\n/* the camera: each slice's scene says how the player may zoom it, camera: { zoom: [min, max] | false, start, tall }: the\\n   engine zooms it with the mouse wheel, - and =, a pinch on a phone and Settings, Camera zoom (the player's own, kept, on\\n   top of the slice's start: tall on a phone held upright). Keys every slice shares besides: V changes the camera view,\\n   [ and ] turn it (camera.rotate), 0 resets it (the player's zoom too), G toggles GPU lighting, M mutes */\\nconst camOpt = () => (game.scene && game.scene.camera) || {};\\naddEventListener('keydown', e => {\\n60→  if (e.repeat) return;\\n  if (e.code === 'KeyV') { game.nextView(); game.note(game.view.label.toUpperCase() + ' VIEW'); }\\n  else if (e.code === 'Digit0') { game.settings.set('zoom', 1); game.resetCamera(); game.note('CAMERA RESET'); }   // (resetCamera draws it at once)\\n  else if ((e.code === 'BracketLeft' || e.code === 'BracketRight') && camOpt().rotate) { game.rotateView(e.code === 'BracketLeft' ? -45 : 45); game.note('TURN ' + game.yaw); }\\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyM') A.mute();\\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyG' && gpu) gpu.enabled = !gpu.enabled;\\n});\\n\\n/* ---- TITLE: a lineup of HD heroes on a torch-lit castle ledge under a dusk sky. The ledge is wide enough for a wide\\n * screen and sits on the screen's bottom edge whatever its height; the logo and menu hang from the top ---- */\\n70→const MID = 480, stage = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: ['S'.repeat(60), 'S'.repeat(60)], legend: { S: 1 },\\n  types: { 1: { style: 'stone', side: '#6a5a78', moss: '#4a6a3a' } }, edges: 'open' });\\nconst titleBg = new E.Backdrop('castle-night');\\nconst HEROES = [   // six heroes, each dressed with the HD rig options\\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', armor: true, hair: 'short', cape: { len: 6, width: 6, seg: 2.6 }, colors: { cloth: '#3a5ab0', cape: '#b02a3a', capeIn: '#5a1020', hair: '#e0b050', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, outfit: 'tunic', hair: 'spiky', hat: { style: 'pointed', color: '#3aa050' }, colors: { cloth: '#3aa050', pants: '#e8dcc0', hair: '#e0b040', trim: '#8a5a2a' } }) },\\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'bulky', weapon: null, hat: { style: 'band', color: '#e03a3a' }, colors: { cloth: '#f0f0f0', pants: '#2f5fd0' } }) },\\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'gun', hat: 'cap', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#d83a2a', pants: '#2f5fd0', glove: '#f0f0f0' } }) },\\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'staff', outfit: 'robe', hair: 'ponytail', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#6a3ab0', hair: '#c8402a', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', hood: true, outfit: 'coat', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#e8e4dc', coat: '#d8d2c8', trim: '#b02a2a', belt: '#b02a2a', pants: '#4a4a5a', boot: '#3a2e2a' } }) }   // the stealth slice's hooded assassin\\n80→];\\nHEROES.forEach((h, i) => { h.x = MID + (i - 2.5) * 35; });\\n// the corner's buttons (menu, full screen, the page's ? API) in game pixels: about 170 x 60 CSS pixels at the top right\\nconst corner = () => { const S = game.screen, k = S.dpr / S.S; return [170 * k, 60 * k]; };\\n// where a HUD's top-right corner ends: left of the corner's menu button while it stays there (a touch screen), else the edge\\nconst cornerX = () => { const ca = touch() && game.cornerArea(); return ca && ca.y0 < 40 ? Math.floor(ca.x0) - 4 : game.W; };\\n/* ---- THE PROOF (tools/kits-test.mjs runs each slice's twice and compares): proof(id, n, script, state, fresh) enters the\\n * slice afresh on a game clock at 0, with Math.random seeded, runs n fixed steps of 1/120 s with script(i, key) pressing\\n * keys (key.hold('right'), key.up('right'), key.tap('attack')), and gives a hash of what state() returns: the numbers that\\n * say how the run went. fresh(), when given, runs first (seeded): it puts back what the slice keeps between visits. A\\n90→ * slice's run(n) is one (__game.scenes.adventure.run(600)): the same number every time ---- */\\nconst proof = (id, n, script, state, fresh) => {\\n  const was = Math.random, I = game.input, held = new Set();\\n  const key = { hold: a => { held.add(a); I.hold(a); }, up: a => { held.delete(a); I.hold(a, false); }, tap: a => I.tap(a) };\\n  Math.random = E.rng(7);\\n  try {\\n    if (fresh) fresh();\\n    game.time = 0; game.timeScale = 1; game.go(id, null, { fade: 0 }); game.advance(1 / 120);\\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { script(i, key); game.advance(1 / 120); }\\n    const s = JSON.stringify(state()); let h = 2166136261;\\n100→    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) h = Math.imul(h ^ s.charCodeAt(i), 16777619);\\n    return h >>> 0;\\n  } finally { for (const a of held) I.hold(a, false); Math.random = was; }\\n};\\n// a proof's walk: every `every` steps it lets go of the directions and holds the next of `dirs`\\nconst walk = (i, key, dirs, every) => { if (i % every) return; for (const d of dirs) key.up(d); key.hold(dirs[(i / every) % dirs.length]); };\\n// START picks too (the touch START, a pad's Start), as Enter does: any player's\\nconst pickOnStart = m => { const P = game.players; if (P.some(I => I.pressed('start'))) { P.forEach(I => I.consumeAll()); m.onPick(m.i, m.items[m.i]); } };\\n/** the title's menu for the slices in this file: grouped by family when there are several (FamilyMenu, in 05-families.js:\\n *  the reference editions carry it, a genre kit of one slice doesn't), else a plain E.Menu */\\n110→const menuOf = (slices, o) => new Set(slices.map(s => s.family)).size > 1 ? new FamilyMenu(slices, o) : new E.Menu(game, slices.map(s => s.label), o);\\nlet titleMenu = null;   // (made at the start, once the file's slices are known)\\nscenes.title = {\\n  view: 'side', views: ['side', 'brawler'], input: 'DEFAULT', res: 'ps1',\\n  enter() { A.music('title'); game.resetCamera(); game.follow(null); game.cam.room = null; game.cam.bounds = null; if (gpu) gpu.enabled = false; L.enabled = false; },\\n  update(dt) {\\n    if (!titleMenu.update(dt)) pickOnStart(titleMenu);\\n    game.focus(MID, 0, 32 + (game.H / 2 - 42) / -game.view.bz);   // the ledge's top 42 px above the screen's bottom edge\\n    const t = game.time;\\n    HEROES.forEach((h, i) => {\\n120→      const u = (t * .45 + i * .37) % 3, swing = u < .5 ? { spec: { a0: 1.4, a1: -1.6, z0: 12, z1: 8, reach: 7.5 }, phase: u < .15 ? 'wind' : 'active', u: u < .15 ? u / .15 : (u - .15) / .35 } : null;\",\"content_concise\":\"1→// The RHYTHM kit's game (four lanes on the music clock: two songs, notes, jumps, holds, a rapping teacher to answer, a dancer and a crowd on the beat, grades, the audio offset), readable: the starter's shell (src/starter/00-shell.js), then\\n// the slice from line 159 (src/starter/37-rhythm.js: its header says what it plays and how), then the start (90-start.js), as the\\n// single-file kit holds them between GAME START and GAME END. rhythm.html plays it; node kits/test.mjs rhythm runs it headless.\\n/* ============================== GAME START ==============================\\n * MY-3D2DGE STARTER: a title menu and vertical slices, one per genre (a genre kit file holds one; the file with every\\n * slice adds an animation lab). Each slice is a complete small game, written to be copied and changed: its own header\\n * (below this shell) says what it plays, its keys and touch controls, its deep links and how it works.\\n * FULL SCREEN, ANY SHAPE: res: 'ps1', expand: true. 320 x 240 is the least the screen shows; a wider or taller window\\n * shows more world (r.W x r.H grows), so every HUD anchors to r.W and r.H, never to 320 and 240, and every level fills\\n10→ * what a wide screen or an upright phone shows. Prompts name the player's own keys: prompt('attack', 'Sword').\\n * To make your own game: change the slice closest to your genre into yours, with its own title, play and game-over\\n * scenes, and delete this menu and the slices you do not use; this shell (the game, its scenes, prompt, best and the\\n * proof) stays unless you replace it. Deep links: #<slice> starts a slice, #<slice>/<part> jumps into a part.\\n * ======================================================================== */\\n(() => {\\n'use strict';\\nconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp, lerp, approach, TAU } = E;\\n\\n/* ---- SHELL: one game, several scenes. Each slice switches view, keys and resolution on entry ----\\n20→ * expand: the picture fills the window, any shape, with no bars: at least 320 x 240 (a slice's res), more on the long side */\\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'side', bg: '#07060d' });\\nconst P = game.particles, L = game.lights, A = game.audio;\\nconst scenes = {};\\nlet gpu = null;                               // WebGPU lighting, used by the adventure slice only\\nconst best = (k, v) => { const old = E.store.get('best:' + k, 0); if (v > old) E.store.set('best:' + k, v); return Math.max(old, v); };\\n// a prompt in the player's own controls, after rebinding: 'J Sword' on a keyboard, 'X Sword' on a pad, 'ATTACK Sword' (the\\n// button's name) on a touch screen, where the buttons sit over the bottom corner: touch() says so, to move a prompt off it\\nconst prompt = (a, word) => game.input.label(a) + ' ' + word, touch = () => game.input.lastDevice === 'touch';\\n// each slice: its scene's id, its title entry, a line about it, and its family (the title groups them under it)\\n30→const SLICES = [\\n  { id: 'adventure', label: 'ADVENTURE', about: 'TOP-DOWN ACTION ADVENTURE', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\\n  { id: 'pointclick', label: 'POINT-AND-CLICK', about: 'RELIGHT THE LIGHTHOUSE ON GULL ROCK', family: 'ADVENTURE' },\\n  { id: 'platformer', label: 'PLATFORMER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING PLATFORMER', family: 'PLATFORM' },\\n  { id: 'metroidvania', label: 'METROIDVANIA', about: 'A WORLD OF ROOMS, ITS MAP, ITS GATES', family: 'PLATFORM' },\\n  { id: 'brawler', label: 'BRAWLER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING BEAT-EM-UP', family: 'FIGHTING' },\\n  { id: 'fighting', label: 'FIGHTING', about: 'ONE ON ONE, BEST OF THREE', family: 'FIGHTING' },\\n  { id: 'shooter', label: 'SHOOTER', about: 'VERTICAL SHOOT-EM-UP', family: 'ARCADE' },\\n  { id: 'arcade', label: 'ARCADE', about: 'A CABINET OF THREE CLASSICS', family: 'ARCADE' },\\n  { id: 'pinball', label: 'PINBALL', about: 'ONE TABLE: RAMP, MULTIBALL, JACKPOT', family: 'ARCADE' },\\n40→  { id: 'kart', label: 'KART', about: 'MODE 7 KARTS, THREE LAPS', family: 'RACING' },\\n  { id: 'road', label: 'ROAD', about: 'A COAST ROAD AGAINST THE CLOCK', family: 'RACING' },\\n  { id: 'puzzle', label: 'PUZZLE', about: 'FALLING BLOCKS AND FALLING PAIRS', family: 'PUZZLE' },\\n  { id: 'rhythm', label: 'RHYTHM', about: 'STEP UP! FOUR LANES, TWO SONGS', family: 'MUSIC' },\\n  { id: 'rpg', label: 'RPG', about: 'A JOURNEY: TOWN, WORLD, CAVE, BATTLES', family: 'RPG' },\\n  { id: 'roguelike', label: 'ROGUELIKE', about: 'A CRYPT OF TEN GENERATED FLOORS', family: 'RPG' },\\n  { id: 'tactics', label: 'TACTICS', about: 'TAKE THE HILL FORT, TURN BY TURN', family: 'STRATEGY' },\\n  { id: 'rts', label: 'RTS', about: 'GATHER GOLD, RAISE AN ARMY, BURN THEIR CAMP', family: 'STRATEGY' },\\n  { id: 'artillery', label: 'ARTILLERY', about: 'TWO TEAMS OF THREE ON AN ISLAND THAT BLOWS APART', family: 'STRATEGY' },\\n  { id: 'sim', label: 'SIM', about: 'BUILD A THEME PARK, KEEP THEM SMILING', family: 'SIMULATION' },\\n50→  { id: 'stealth', label: 'STEALTH', about: 'ROOFTOP STEALTH AND PARKOUR', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\\n  { id: 'sports', label: 'SPORTS', about: 'TWO AGAINST TWO IN THE CAGE', family: 'SPORTS' },\\n  { id: 'animlab', label: 'ANIMATION LAB', about: 'EVERY MOVE, SKIN AND VIEW', family: 'TOOLS' }\\n];\\n/* the camera: each slice's scene says how the player may zoom it, camera: { zoom: [min, max] | false, start, tall }: the\\n   engine zooms it with the mouse wheel, - and =, a pinch on a phone and Settings, Camera zoom (the player's own, kept, on\\n   top of the slice's start: tall on a phone held upright). Keys every slice shares besides: V changes the camera view,\\n   [ and ] turn it (camera.rotate), 0 resets it (the player's zoom too), G toggles GPU lighting, M mutes */\\nconst camOpt = () => (game.scene && game.scene.camera) || {};\\naddEventListener('keydown', e => {\\n60→  if (e.repeat) return;\\n  if (e.code === 'KeyV') { game.nextView(); game.note(game.view.label.toUpperCase() + ' VIEW'); }\\n  else if (e.code === 'Digit0') { game.settings.set('zoom', 1); game.resetCamera(); game.note('CAMERA RESET'); }   // (resetCamera draws it at once)\\n  else if ((e.code === 'BracketLeft' || e.code === 'BracketRight') && camOpt().rotate) { game.rotateView(e.code === 'BracketLeft' ? -45 : 45); game.note('TURN ' + game.yaw); }\\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyM') A.mute();\\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyG' && gpu) gpu.enabled = !gpu.enabled;\\n});\\n\\n/* ---- TITLE: a lineup of HD heroes on a torch-lit castle ledge under a dusk sky. The ledge is wide enough for a wide\\n * screen and sits on the screen's bottom edge whatever its height; the logo and menu hang from the top ---- */\\n70→const MID = 480, stage = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: ['S'.repeat(60), 'S'.repeat(60)], legend: { S: 1 },\\n  types: { 1: { style: 'stone', side: '#6a5a78', moss: '#4a6a3a' } }, edges: 'open' });\\nconst titleBg = new E.Backdrop('castle-night');\\nconst HEROES = [   // six heroes, each dressed with the HD rig options\\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', armor: true, hair: 'short', cape: { len: 6, width: 6, seg: 2.6 }, colors: { cloth: '#3a5ab0', cape: '#b02a3a', capeIn: '#5a1020', hair: '#e0b050', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, outfit: 'tunic', hair: 'spiky', hat: { style: 'pointed', color: '#3aa050' }, colors: { cloth: '#3aa050', pants: '#e8dcc0', hair: '#e0b040', trim: '#8a5a2a' } }) },\\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'bulky', weapon: null, hat: { style: 'band', color: '#e03a3a' }, colors: { cloth: '#f0f0f0', pants: '#2f5fd0' } }) },\\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'gun', hat: 'cap', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#d83a2a', pants: '#2f5fd0', glove: '#f0f0f0' } }) },\\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'staff', outfit: 'robe', hair: 'ponytail', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#6a3ab0', hair: '#c8402a', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', hood: true, outfit: 'coat', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#e8e4dc', coat: '#d8d2c8', trim: '#b02a2a', belt: '#b02a2a', pants: '#4a4a5a', boot: '#3a2e2a' } }) }   // the stealth slice's hooded assassin\\n80→];\\nHEROES.forEach((h, i) => { h.x = MID + (i - 2.5) * 35; });\\n// the corner's buttons (menu, full screen, the page's ? API) in game pixels: about 170 x 60 CSS pixels at the top right\\nconst corner = () => { const S = game.screen, k = S.dpr / S.S; return [170 * k, 60 * k]; };\\n// where a HUD's top-right corner ends: left of the corner's menu button while it stays there (a touch screen), else the edge\\nconst cornerX = () => { const ca = touch() && game.cornerArea(); return ca && ca.y0 < 40 ? Math.floor(ca.x0) - 4 : game.W; };\\n/* ---- THE PROOF (tools/kits-test.mjs runs each slice's twice and compares): proof(id, n, script, state, fresh) enters the\\n * slice afresh on a game clock at 0, with Math.random seeded, runs n fixed steps of 1/120 s with script(i, key) pressing\\n * keys (key.hold('right'), key.up('right'), key.tap('attack')), and gives a hash of what state() returns: the numbers that\\n * say how the run went. fresh(), when given, runs first (seeded): it puts back what the slice keeps between visits. A\\n90→ * slice's run(n) is one (__game.scenes.adventure.run(600)): the same number every time ---- */\\nconst proof = (id, n, script, state, fresh) => {\\n  const was = Math.random, I = game.input, held = new Set();\\n  const key = { hold: a => { held.add(a); I.hold(a); }, up: a => { held.delete(a); I.hold(a, false); }, tap: a => I.tap(a) };\\n  Math.random = E.rng(7);\\n  try {\\n    if (fresh) fresh();\\n    game.time = 0; game.timeScale = 1; game.go(id, null, { fade: 0 }); game.advance(1 / 120);\\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { script(i, key); game.advance(1 / 120); }\\n    const s = JSON.stringify(state()); let h = 2166136261;\\n100→    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) h = Math.imul(h ^ s.charCodeAt(i), 16777619);\\n    return h >>> 0;\\n  } finally { for (const a of held) I.hold(a, false); Math.random = was; }\\n};\\n// a proof's walk: every `every` steps it lets go of the directions and holds the next of `dirs`\\nconst walk = (i, key, dirs, every) => { if (i % every) return; for (const d of dirs) key.up(d); key.hold(dirs[(i / every) % dirs.length]); };\\n// START picks too (the touch START, a pad's Start), as Enter does: any player's\\nconst pickOnStart = m => { const P = game.players; if (P.some(I => I.pressed('start'))) { P.forEach(I => I.consumeAll()); m.onPick(m.i, m.items[m.i]); } };\\n/** the title's menu for the slices in this file: grouped by family when there are several (FamilyMenu, in 05-families.js:\\n *  the reference editions carry it, a genre kit of one slice doesn't), else a plain E.Menu */\\n110→const menuOf = (slices, o) => new Set(slices.map(s => s.family)).size > 1 ? new FamilyMenu(slices, o) : new E.Menu(game, slices.map(s => s.label), o);\\nlet titleMenu = null;   // (made at the start, once the file's slices are known)\\nscenes.title = {\\n  view: 'side', views: ['side', 'brawler'], input: 'DEFAULT', res: 'ps1',\\n  enter() { A.music('title'); game.resetCamera(); game.follow(null); game.cam.room = null; game.cam.bounds = null; if (gpu) gpu.enabled = false; L.enabled = false; },\\n  update(dt) {\\n    if (!titleMenu.update(dt)) pickOnStart(titleMenu);\\n    game.focus(MID, 0, 32 + (game.H / 2 - 42) / -game.view.bz);   // the ledge's top 42 px above the screen's bottom edge\\n    const t = game.time;\\n    HEROES.forEach((h, i) => {\\n120→      const u = (t * .45 + i * .37) % 3, swing = u < .5 ? { spec: { a0: 1.4, a1: -1.6, z0: 12, z1: 8, reach: 7.5 }, phase: u < .15 ? 'wind' : 'active', u: u < .15 ? u / .15 : (u - .15) / .35 } : null;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/kits/rhythm.js\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":120,\"raw_output\":\"// The RHYTHM kit's game (four lanes on the music clock: two songs, notes, jumps, holds, a rapping teacher to answer, a dancer and a crowd on the beat, grades, the audio offset), readable: the starter's shell (src/starter/00-shell.js), then\\n// the slice from line 159 (src/starter/37-rhythm.js: its header says what it plays and how), then the start (90-start.js), as the\\n// single-file kit holds them between GAME START and GAME END. rhythm.html plays it; node kits/test.mjs rhythm runs it headless.\\n/* ============================== GAME START ==============================\\n * MY-3D2DGE STARTER: a title menu and vertical slices, one per genre (a genre kit file holds one; the file with every\\n * slice adds an animation lab). Each slice is a complete small game, written to be copied and changed: its own header\\n * (below this shell) says what it plays, its keys and touch controls, its deep links and how it works.\\n * FULL SCREEN, ANY SHAPE: res: 'ps1', expand: true. 320 x 240 is the least the screen shows; a wider or taller window\\n * shows more world (r.W x r.H grows), so every HUD anchors to r.W and r.H, never to 320 and 240, and every level fills\\n * what a wide screen or an upright phone shows. Prompts name the player's own keys: prompt('attack', 'Sword').\\n * To make your own game: change the slice closest to your genre into yours, with its own title, play and game-over\\n * scenes, and delete this menu and the slices you do not use; this shell (the game, its scenes, prompt, best and the\\n * proof) stays unless you replace it. Deep links: #<slice> starts a slice, #<slice>/<part> jumps into a part.\\n * ======================================================================== */\\n(() => {\\n'use strict';\\nconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp, lerp, approach, TAU } = E;\\n\\n/* ---- SHELL: one game, several scenes. Each slice switches view, keys and resolution on entry ----\\n * expand: the picture fills the window, any shape, with no bars: at least 320 x 240 (a slice's res), more on the long side */\\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'side', bg: '#07060d' });\\nconst P = game.particles, L = game.lights, A = game.audio;\\nconst scenes = {};\\nlet gpu = null;                               // WebGPU lighting, used by the adventure slice only\\nconst best = (k, v) => { const old = E.store.get('best:' + k, 0); if (v > old) E.store.set('best:' + k, v); return Math.max(old, v); };\\n// a prompt in the player's own controls, after rebinding: 'J Sword' on a keyboard, 'X Sword' on a pad, 'ATTACK Sword' (the\\n// button's name) on a touch screen, where the buttons sit over the bottom corner: touch() says so, to move a prompt off it\\nconst prompt = (a, word) => game.input.label(a) + ' ' + word, touch = () => game.input.lastDevice === 'touch';\\n// each slice: its scene's id, its title entry, a line about it, and its family (the title groups them under it)\\nconst SLICES = [\\n  { id: 'adventure', label: 'ADVENTURE', about: 'TOP-DOWN ACTION ADVENTURE', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\\n  { id: 'pointclick', label: 'POINT-AND-CLICK', about: 'RELIGHT THE LIGHTHOUSE ON GULL ROCK', family: 'ADVENTURE' },\\n  { id: 'platformer', label: 'PLATFORMER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING PLATFORMER', family: 'PLATFORM' },\\n  { id: 'metroidvania', label: 'METROIDVANIA', about: 'A WORLD OF ROOMS, ITS MAP, ITS GATES', family: 'PLATFORM' },\\n  { id: 'brawler', label: 'BRAWLER', about: 'SIDE-SCROLLING BEAT-EM-UP', family: 'FIGHTING' },\\n  { id: 'fighting', label: 'FIGHTING', about: 'ONE ON ONE, BEST OF THREE', family: 'FIGHTING' },\\n  { id: 'shooter', label: 'SHOOTER', about: 'VERTICAL SHOOT-EM-UP', family: 'ARCADE' },\\n  { id: 'arcade', label: 'ARCADE', about: 'A CABINET OF THREE CLASSICS', family: 'ARCADE' },\\n  { id: 'pinball', label: 'PINBALL', about: 'ONE TABLE: RAMP, MULTIBALL, JACKPOT', family: 'ARCADE' },\\n  { id: 'kart', label: 'KART', about: 'MODE 7 KARTS, THREE LAPS', family: 'RACING' },\\n  { id: 'road', label: 'ROAD', about: 'A COAST ROAD AGAINST THE CLOCK', family: 'RACING' },\\n  { id: 'puzzle', label: 'PUZZLE', about: 'FALLING BLOCKS AND FALLING PAIRS', family: 'PUZZLE' },\\n  { id: 'rhythm', label: 'RHYTHM', about: 'STEP UP! FOUR LANES, TWO SONGS', family: 'MUSIC' },\\n  { id: 'rpg', label: 'RPG', about: 'A JOURNEY: TOWN, WORLD, CAVE, BATTLES', family: 'RPG' },\\n  { id: 'roguelike', label: 'ROGUELIKE', about: 'A CRYPT OF TEN GENERATED FLOORS', family: 'RPG' },\\n  { id: 'tactics', label: 'TACTICS', about: 'TAKE THE HILL FORT, TURN BY TURN', family: 'STRATEGY' },\\n  { id: 'rts', label: 'RTS', about: 'GATHER GOLD, RAISE AN ARMY, BURN THEIR CAMP', family: 'STRATEGY' },\\n  { id: 'artillery', label: 'ARTILLERY', about: 'TWO TEAMS OF THREE ON AN ISLAND THAT BLOWS APART', family: 'STRATEGY' },\\n  { id: 'sim', label: 'SIM', about: 'BUILD A THEME PARK, KEEP THEM SMILING', family: 'SIMULATION' },\\n  { id: 'stealth', label: 'STEALTH', about: 'ROOFTOP STEALTH AND PARKOUR', family: 'ACTION ADVENTURE' },\\n  { id: 'sports', label: 'SPORTS', about: 'TWO AGAINST TWO IN THE CAGE', family: 'SPORTS' },\\n  { id: 'animlab', label: 'ANIMATION LAB', about: 'EVERY MOVE, SKIN AND VIEW', family: 'TOOLS' }\\n];\\n/* the camera: each slice's scene says how the player may zoom it, camera: { zoom: [min, max] | false, start, tall }: the\\n   engine zooms it with the mouse wheel, - and =, a pinch on a phone and Settings, Camera zoom (the player's own, kept, on\\n   top of the slice's start: tall on a phone held upright). Keys every slice shares besides: V changes the camera view,\\n   [ and ] turn it (camera.rotate), 0 resets it (the player's zoom too), G toggles GPU lighting, M mutes */\\nconst camOpt = () => (game.scene && game.scene.camera) || {};\\naddEventListener('keydown', e => {\\n  if (e.repeat) return;\\n  if (e.code === 'KeyV') { game.nextView(); game.note(game.view.label.toUpperCase() + ' VIEW'); }\\n  else if (e.code === 'Digit0') { game.settings.set('zoom', 1); game.resetCamera(); game.note('CAMERA RESET'); }   // (resetCamera draws it at once)\\n  else if ((e.code === 'BracketLeft' || e.code === 'BracketRight') && camOpt().rotate) { game.rotateView(e.code === 'BracketLeft' ? -45 : 45); game.note('TURN ' + game.yaw); }\\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyM') A.mute();\\n  else if (e.code === 'KeyG' && gpu) gpu.enabled = !gpu.enabled;\\n});\\n\\n/* ---- TITLE: a lineup of HD heroes on a torch-lit castle ledge under a dusk sky. The ledge is wide enough for a wide\\n * screen and sits on the screen's bottom edge whatever its height; the logo and menu hang from the top ---- */\\nconst MID = 480, stage = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: ['S'.repeat(60), 'S'.repeat(60)], legend: { S: 1 },\\n  types: { 1: { style: 'stone', side: '#6a5a78', moss: '#4a6a3a' } }, edges: 'open' });\\nconst titleBg = new E.Backdrop('castle-night');\\nconst HEROES = [   // six heroes, each dressed with the HD rig options\\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', armor: true, hair: 'short', cape: { len: 6, width: 6, seg: 2.6 }, colors: { cloth: '#3a5ab0', cape: '#b02a3a', capeIn: '#5a1020', hair: '#e0b050', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, outfit: 'tunic', hair: 'spiky', hat: { style: 'pointed', color: '#3aa050' }, colors: { cloth: '#3aa050', pants: '#e8dcc0', hair: '#e0b040', trim: '#8a5a2a' } }) },\\n  { face: 0, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'bulky', weapon: null, hat: { style: 'band', color: '#e03a3a' }, colors: { cloth: '#f0f0f0', pants: '#2f5fd0' } }) },\\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'gun', hat: 'cap', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#d83a2a', pants: '#2f5fd0', glove: '#f0f0f0' } }) },\\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, weapon: 'staff', outfit: 'robe', hair: 'ponytail', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#6a3ab0', hair: '#c8402a', trim: '#e8c860' } }) },\\n  { face: Math.PI, rig: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', hood: true, outfit: 'coat', sleeves: 'long', colors: { cloth: '#e8e4dc', coat: '#d8d2c8', trim: '#b02a2a', belt: '#b02a2a', pants: '#4a4a5a', boot: '#3a2e2a' } }) }   // the stealth slice's hooded assassin\\n];\\nHEROES.forEach((h, i) => { h.x = MID + (i - 2.5) * 35; });\\n// the corner's buttons (menu, full screen, the page's ? API) in game pixels: about 170 x 60 CSS pixels at the top right\\nconst corner = () => { const S = game.screen, k = S.dpr / S.S; return [170 * k, 60 * k]; };\\n// where a HUD's top-right corner ends: left of the corner's menu button while it stays there (a touch screen), else the edge\\nconst cornerX = () => { const ca = touch() && game.cornerArea(); return ca && ca.y0 < 40 ? Math.floor(ca.x0) - 4 : game.W; };\\n/* ---- THE PROOF (tools/kits-test.mjs runs each slice's twice and compares): proof(id, n, script, state, fresh) enters the\\n * slice afresh on a game clock at 0, with Math.random seeded, runs n fixed steps of 1/120 s with script(i, key) pressing\\n * keys (key.hold('right'), key.up('right'), key.tap('attack')), and gives a hash of what state() returns: the numbers that\\n * say how the run went. fresh(), when given, runs first (seeded): it puts back what the slice keeps between visits. A\\n * slice's run(n) is one (__game.scenes.adventure.run(600)): the same number every time ---- */\\nconst proof = (id, n, script, state, fresh) => {\\n  const was = Math.random, I = game.input, held = new Set();\\n  const key = { hold: a => { held.add(a); I.hold(a); }, up: a => { held.delete(a); I.hold(a, false); }, tap: a => I.tap(a) };\\n  Math.random = E.rng(7);\\n  try {\\n    if (fresh) fresh();\\n    game.time = 0; game.timeScale = 1; game.go(id, null, { fade: 0 }); game.advance(1 / 120);\\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { script(i, key); game.advance(1 / 120); }\\n    const s = JSON.stringify(state()); let h = 2166136261;\\n    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) h = Math.imul(h ^ s.charCodeAt(i), 16777619);\\n    return h >>> 0;\\n  } finally { for (const a of held) I.hold(a, false); Math.random = was; }\\n};\\n// a proof's walk: every `every` steps it lets go of the directions and holds the next of `dirs`\\nconst walk = (i, key, dirs, every) => { if (i % every) return; for (const d of dirs) key.up(d); key.hold(dirs[(i / every) % dirs.length]); };\\n// START picks too (the touch START, a pad's Start), as Enter does: any player's\\nconst pickOnStart = m => { const P = game.players; if (P.some(I => I.pressed('start'))) { P.forEach(I => I.consumeAll()); m.onPick(m.i, m.items[m.i]); } };\\n/** the title's menu for the slices in this file: grouped by family when there are several (FamilyMenu, in 05-families.js:\\n *  the reference editions carry it, a genre kit of one slice doesn't), else a plain E.Menu */\\nconst menuOf = (slices, o) => new Set(slices.map(s => s.family)).size > 1 ? new FamilyMenu(slices, o) : new E.Menu(game, slices.map(s => s.label), o);\\nlet titleMenu = null;   // (made at the start, once the file's slices are known)\\nscenes.title = {\\n  view: 'side', views: ['side', 'brawler'], input: 'DEFAULT', res: 'ps1',\\n  enter() { A.music('title'); game.resetCamera(); game.follow(null); game.cam.room = null; game.cam.bounds = null; if (gpu) gpu.enabled = false; L.enabled = false; },\\n  update(dt) {\\n    if (!titleMenu.update(dt)) pickOnStart(titleMenu);\\n    game.focus(MID, 0, 32 + (game.H / 2 - 42) / -game.view.bz);   // the ledge's top 42 px above the screen's bottom edge\\n    const t = game.time;\\n    HEROES.forEach((h, i) => {\\n      const u = (t * .45 + i * .37) % 3, swing = u < .5 ? { spec: { a0: 1.4, a1: -1.6, z0: 12, z1: 8, reach: 7.5 }, phase: u < .15 ? 'wind' : 'active', u: u < .15 ? u / .15 : (u - .15) / .35 } : null;\\n\",\"total_lines\":397}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"575ddb74-33b4-4ec6-a039-5925e9a58b88"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_45","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have a clear picture of the requirements. I am starting to write the files.\n\nI have mapped out the full file structure for the game. It covers engine patches, data files, balance, rigs, audio, UI, play logic, world scenes, boot, HTML, build scripts, and tests.\n\nI will add a data README. I am also planning defensive code for headless mode, including null checks for the renderer and pointer input.\n\nMocap will be lazy-loaded on first rig creation to avoid startup delays. Clip sampling will be deferred until drawing to save time, though headless tests skip draw entirely. I am also verifying the Humanoid update signature and setBody options to prevent runtime crashes.\n\nI am replicating the tuner's exact noise and RNG logic in balance.js. Joints will only be set after drawing, using worldOffset.\n\nI am analyzing the tuner's exact physics loop. The push phase RNG is seeded after the noise phases consume six calls. Bot and player RNGs remain separate. Steadiness checks fall detection at the end of each frame, so the fall frame itself does not count.\n\nI am tracing the exact frame-by-frame logic of the fall and stumble transitions. When a fall occurs, the code sets `stumble = 1` and zeroes velocity, but the end-of-frame steadiness check sees `stumble > 0` so that frame does not count as inside. On the final frame of the stumble period, `stumble` decrements to zero before the physics branch runs, meaning the frame still eases while the next frame counts as inside if the player remains in the ring.\n\nI am replicating the bot history lag exactly. The history array is pushed at the start of each step with the current position before physics run, so the first frame contains a single zero sample. The bot reads its delayed target from that history using a lag derived from tau and dt. I will mirror this exact order in Balance.step, adding optional center and external input parameters.\n\nThe band test must use the exact bot formula without a center offset. The activity control loop can be separate.\n\nThe optional center offset defaults to zero, so the formula collapses to the exact band-test bot pattern. External forces will add extra fx and fy to the push vector. Gentle mode scales s and A when constructing the set, not inside the integrator, so band tests receive explicit sets.\n\nDev sliders will only affect the set used by activities. Band tests will keep using explicit table constants, so they remain tied to shipped defaults and pass deterministically through the same step() method. Push forces will be applied only when a set.push value is present, and activities can supply extra forces directly.\n\nThe stork activity will use the stork1 set with its push included. Gull forces may make it too hard and drop the bot below two stars, so I will test and adjust if needed. I will add a compact self-test to balance.js for quick validation. The island tile map will be a modest ASCII grid.\n\nI will implement simplified but realistic gameplay for laundry, tray, slalom, and jog using the engine modules where possible. Bot skill for these non-hold activities will be a scalar from 0 to 0.98 covering passive through expert. Scoring functions will map that skill to star percentages while still simulating the actual mechanic so real players get comparable scores. I am avoiding direct skill-to-score mapping to prevent test theater.\n\nThe plan requires tests to drive the shipped game and real lean, not reimplement. The bot must play the mechanic and score must come from it.\n\nHold will use a real lean with activity percentage derived from steadiness relative to a moving ring. The bot tracks the ring center, so I will verify star thresholds and tune ring movement or bot behavior if needed.\n\nRep will balance with s=0 on y while the bot sets target depth; tracking error becomes the score.\n\nCandle will generate flame from velocity magnitude and steadiness.\n\nStep will use rhythm.hit from the bot to score real rhythm accuracy.\n\nHoop will integrate angle from stick circling. The bot sets a circular target at 0.8 amplitude, and omega is measured for scoring.\n\nJog will use cadence from alternating breath and effort, plus zone time. Cups serve as targets that the steer collects when lean x matches. Position and obstacles will be simulated in the update, while the road draws in the draw pass.\n\nSlalom will integrate steer from lean to track x position and gate checks. Tray will use E.Physics, with the bot tilting gravity toward olives; if physics is too fiddly I will tune G and layouts. Laundry will use effort for x movement, jumps over pins, gusts via lean, and a fall penalty. The gull force for stork will be a scripted scripted fx that the bot must counter, with dev defaults to reduce its magnitude if needed. I am now starting to write files and patch the dialog.\n\nI am drafting the lines.js file with verbatim plan content plus extra entries to exceed 150 lines. I have also compiled a list of nicknames, looks categories, and silly questions for the character data.\n\nI will write the files directly instead of using a generator script. I am choosing the px drawing API for tare visuals and adding a tare draw function to rigs.js. I am also setting up an index.html dev page for pre-bundle testing, while keeping tests/run.mjs as the source-based rules loader.\n\nI am deciding whether tests/run.mjs should load source scripts directly or the built HTML. The plan requires the verifier to confirm the shipped scripts are loaded, so loading the source files that get concatenated into the ship is acceptable. I will follow the plan's instruction to load engine, vendor, and game files in a vm.\n\nTests must invoke the actual game, not merely print counts. I will load the real game scripts directly. To keep about-text validation consistent between rules and built output, I will set G.ABOUT from exit-interview.json during boot so the build inlines the same value.\n\nI will embed the exit-interview data in game/about.js as a placeholder assignment. The build step will replace that marker with the actual JSON contents, keeping the source and shipped output synchronized. Static verification will check that the built HTML contains the same strings.\n\nI will generate game/about.js directly from exit-interview.json during the build step, and commit that generated file so tests can compare it against the raw JSON. The dev index page will also embed the about object inline to avoid fetch issues. I am now starting to write the files.\n\nI will leave vendor/clipsrc in place for now since lint does not scan it. Playwright is installed in the dev folder node_modules, so check.mjs must run from that directory to resolve the import.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-45ae9b6e-89b2-4f83-a96f-80bcbdb26f88-68","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"echo \"OVERRIDE=${PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE:-unset}\" && node -e \"const {chromium}=require('playwright'); console.log('pw', require('playwright/package.json').version)\"","description":"Check Playwright env and module resolve"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8185,"output_tokens":3539,"cache_read_input_tokens":187264,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"2fd10686-e151-476d-9eae-3c09a32f32c2"}
