{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_50","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Planning to add pads 0 through 2 for players 2–4 and a keyboard input.\n\nThe test will add four players: one with the right key, and three with pads. The class scene will use game.players to assign bots to empty mats. Scores and a podium will be driven by bot performance.\n\nThe test will pass four bot skill levels to the class scene. Headless pads produce no stick input, so the test must explicitly hold actions on each player's input to drive their scores.\n\nA new `G.feedStick(input, x, y)` helper will let the test drive pad players directly. The intent reader will check a `_virtualStick` flag before reading the physical gamepad, bypassing tick overwrites.\n\nThe virtual stick approach is being replaced by a `G.leanInput(input, x, y)` hook. This lets the test and the dev bot both write directly into each contestant's intent slot. The class scene will create up to four contestants from `game.players`, matching the four inputs the test adds.\n\nThe test will tap confirm on each of the four inputs to start the activity. The verification will check that all four players are present, the podium contains four scored entries in sorted order, and higher skill levels produce higher scores.\n\nThe class scene will use `data.drive` to activate the activity's bot() when four players are present. Empty mats remain non-scoring, so only human contestants can earn scores.\n\nThe input override rule is now defined: a player's own input wins if its magnitude exceeds 0.05, otherwise the bot drives the intent. The podium will display titles like Most Still, Best Wobble, and Participated. I am beginning the full code implementation now, including the HTML shell CSS and default settings.\n\nThe settings panel now includes voice volume, gentle mode, breath mode, and Tare's tips toggles. The menu adds Restart Session and Dev Mode options, while the hub hides its items.\n\nMenu controls are being mapped to plain action names like Breathe, Catch left, and Jump. The quit option will return to the title screen.\n\nAutosave will trigger when the title scene loads, provided the session is not attract mode. It will also run at dawn and after each session. Deep links from the URL hash will boot directly into specific scenes: #act/stork/1 for the stork activity, #day/3 for a day, #ending for the ending, and #hub for the hub round.\n\nThe #hub deep link will start a solo practice morning class using local time when AIHub is inactive. The normal page without a hash will still open the title screen.\n\nAttract mode will be blocked during the hub scene. The implementation will proceed in two phases: first the core systems—patch, balance, data, audio, and rigs—then the gameplay, world, boot, and tests. A pose table will be added to the data module. The results screen will use visual stars and Gerald coins; logic only needs to award minutes. A deterministic `roll` field will be included in snapshots so the same activity run from identical seeds with identical scripted inputs produces an identical hash.\n\nA `G.trialHash` helper will run the shipped Balance with scripted inputs for five seconds and return a hash of the samples. This ensures identical seeds produce identical hashes across two test runs. An activity will also be run twice to compare percentage scores. A `game.debug.snapshot().hash` accessor is being added. The plan checklist will only be updated after all files are written.\n\nGentle mode will scale the balance parameters by 0.7 for stiffness and damping, and 1.25 for recovery. The dev panel will expose live sliders for k, c, s, A, and nf, which override the default values when G.dev.live differs from its defaults.\n\nThe dev panel will use absolute overrides rather than multipliers. A `G.dev.pick` helper will return a slider value only if it has been touched; otherwise the activity’s own default is used. Resetting the sliders clears all overrides. The plan requires that balance changes be felt live.\n\nThe dev panel will use a `G.dev.live` object to store slider values and a `G.dev.override` set tracking which keys have been touched. A `param` helper will merge these overrides into the activity’s default balance parameters, applying the ring scale multiplier only when that override is active.\n\nThe `param` helper will also apply live overrides for `pushScale` and `stumbleAt`. Band trial will bypass dev overrides entirely by reading raw table values. The dev test will verify that adjusting a slider updates `G.dev.live.k` and that a running balance step uses the overridden value when touched. A `G.dev.band` flag will suppress dev overrides during band trials.\n\nThe dev overlay will be applied at the start of each session step rather than inside the simulation function. A pure `G.simulate(set, skill, seed)` will handle band trials with explicit parameters and bypass dev overrides entirely. Resetting the dev panel will clear all touched flags and restore live defaults.\n\nA dev jump target will let the test load every scene and activity by name. Music tracks must use original note strings instead of the engine songbook. The sound effects library will define boop, creak, chime, stumble, clink, gull, flurry, flame, hoop, carve, tray, plop, rope, stamp, tick, and confirm.\n\nStork tier 2 will use a panned oscillator on the SFX bus, with pitch and pan mapped to x and y. A plate trail will record the last second of movement as 120 samples. The playthrough test will keep real time rather than fast-forwarding. Body check duration is the main bottleneck, requiring roughly 173,000 engine steps across twelve activities plus star tests. I am estimating the per-step cost to gauge feasibility.\n\nEngine step timing is now estimated at 0.05–0.2 ms per tick, so the full playthrough test will take roughly 50 seconds. Star tests will use a single seed to keep iteration fast. The band trial alone requires about 1.6 million balance steps, but that runs in under half a second because it bypasses the engine entirely. During development, only a subset of activities will be tested. I am also considering whether activities should finish early once their outcome is already determined.\n\nActivities will not finish early. The test advances the full duration plus one second and expects the activity to be done with stars set. The session will skip the intro and countdown in star tests so only the play period is timed.\n\nThe demo and countdown will be included in the full player path. The card phase will be kept short during story playthroughs. Star gate tests will skip the intro, then confirm through the card phase before advancing for the full play duration.\n\nA `pumpUntil` test helper will advance the game and tap confirm automatically. A `tap` helper will press a specific action and advance one frame. An `until` helper will loop until a predicate passes or a timeout expires, tapping every half-second. During play, taps will be restricted to card, results, or dialog phases so confirm does not accidentally press done early.\n\nThe snapshot will expose a `phase` field with values like 'card', 'demo', 'count', 'play', 'results', 'talk', and 'pills'. The playthrough test loop will advance one second during the play phase, then tap confirm and advance a quarter second in every other phase. Confirm presses will trigger Done in results, start the card in card, advance dialog, and select the focused pill in pills.\n\nThe playthrough loop is safe because confirm during play does nothing. Title and member screens use confirm to start new members. Arrival proceeds through dialog and instructor.\n\nIsland will auto-advance to evening after the third session ends, so the playthrough loop does not get stuck on the island phase.\n\nStory mode will skip the island between sessions and go straight to the next activity card after Done. The island remains reachable from the bungalow before the first session. A guided path runs bungalow → body check → island → card chain. Tests will tap through this sequence and also load the island scene directly.\n\nThe island phase will not auto-advance. The playthrough loop taps confirm once per visit to start the session. Passive play without a driver still completes activities on the timer even at zero stars. A `G.drive` flag will distinguish scripted driver mode from passive mode, set before the loop begins.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-942f9782-742b-4fd7-9d38-80dfd3c3e61b-73","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"function drive|drive\\(rig","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/vendor/mocap.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1427,"output_tokens":4403,"cache_read_input_tokens":200448,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"34a45f47-3b74-463c-8190-926bffbffb18"}
