view.\\n *    Attack specs (angles relative to facing, + = toward the right hand):\\n *      { a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, blade, spin }   e.g. horizontal slash a0: 1.7 -> a1: -1.9\\n *    Drive with rig.update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, attack:{ spec, phase, u } })\\n *    where phase is 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover' and u is 0..1 progress in that phase.\\n * ============================================================================= */\\nconst HUMAN_COLORS = {\\n\",\"total_lines\":14974}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"6726268b-56c1-4751-9081-9a11c1bbf8ef"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_63","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I understand the requirements.\n\nI'm beginning to write the game files now. The plan covers 13 files total: data arrays, balance logic, rig systems, audio, UI, gameplay, world scenes, boot sequence, HTML entry point, and a build script.\n\nA 14th file, tests/run.mjs, is added to the plan. Balance logic will mirror the tuner exactly, using a G.bandTrial helper for deterministic runs and G.SETS for rig presets. A G.Intent class is also being drafted.\n\nThe G.Intent class now tracks movement, breathing, catch buttons, jump, and input source. A readIntent helper is being written to parse keyboard, gamepad, and mouse inputs while respecting dev settings like reach and nudge.\n\nThe readIntent helper now timestamps key presses and skips device lean when a bot driver is active. Gamepad input maps directly to the intent vector, while mouse input converts pointer coordinates to normalized lean values using screen height and reach.\n\nKeyboard input is being handled by nudging the lean vector toward the movement direction at 2.2 units per second, clamped to length 1. The plan specifies additive velocity rather than interpolation.\n\nKeyboard input now adds the movement direction scaled by 2.2 per second, clamped to length one. When released, the lean vector drifts home at 0.5 per second. The breathing logic is next.\n\nBreathing logic is now drafted. It reads the highest analog value from breath and pace inputs. In toggle mode it switches on and off. In hold mode it ramps up when any button is pressed and ramps down when released. The band test will use direct bot lean values rather than keyboard input.\n\nTests will verify that WASD nudges change the lean vector. Hold activities will use Balance with section 3 sets and the specified percentage formulas. Stork percentage equals steadiness minus five times falls, so passive performance yields zero stars. A poor result of roughly thirty-four percent lands just below the one-star threshold.\n\nStork tier math is being verified. Average performance should yield two stars, while passive falls should score negative. Expert tier needs a trial check to confirm it reaches two stars. Sun activity percentage is being calculated as sixty percent steadiness plus forty percent breath match.\n\nSun activity math is being checked. With zero breathing and a linear guide, the match score is roughly 0.125. That yields about 0.47 percentage, which is below the one-star threshold.\n\nSun percentage with a breathing bot lands around 0.64, which clears the one-star threshold but falls short of two stars. The activity bot must synchronize its breath with the guide to reach higher tiers. Hold activities will use stability bots from the table.\n\nActivity-specific bot strategies are being mapped out. Sun will track the guide level with skill lag. Candle uses the existing stability bot plus gentle corrections and flame from velocity magnitude. Rep uses downward bead lag. Step uses rhythm hit with offset. Hoop uses circular lean. Jog alternates pace. Slalom steers toward gates. Tray tilts toward olives. Laundry combines effort and jump.\n\nA starsOf helper is drafted using thresholds of 0.35, 0.65, and 0.90. Passive scores below 0.35 receive zero stars.\n\nStork tier thresholds are now fixed: passive must stay below 0.35, good must reach at least 0.35, and expert must reach at least 0.65. A 53 percent good score clears the one-star bar but still needs tuning to hit two stars.\n\nThe gull effect adds ±2.5 constant force while on the arm. This extra push is excluded from band tests but included in activity scoring. It may cause passive stork falls to stay under one star and could push a good bot below two stars if the force is too strong.\n\nGull will be added as an occasional extra force and tuned only if tests show a good bot dropping below two stars. Candle thoughts and laundry gusts are also extra forces that activity scoring includes but band tests exclude; they may need tuning to keep the star bands intact.\n\nStork gull will be short and rare. If the good bot drops below two stars, the force will be reduced to 1.2.\n\nTests will run activities directly and print percentages to guide tuning.\n\nRep scoring is now defined. The bot targets downward bead depth. Expert performance scores 1.0 when lag stays under 0.08. Good reaches roughly 0.7. Average reaches roughly 0.4. Poor or passive scores 0.\n\nRep scoring now includes a form penalty for horizontal drift, capped at 0.3. Percentage equals mean rep multiplied by one minus that penalty. Passive scores zero stars. Average reaches about 0.45 for one star. Good reaches about 0.75 for two stars.\n\nLag is tuned per tier: poor uses heavy lag with noise, average uses 0.18, good uses 0.08, and expert uses 0.03. The balance system tracks the lean vector closely on the y-axis.\n\nRep lag is being tuned to hit the star bands. A 0.2 second lag would score roughly 0.93, which is too high for average tier. Average needs about 0.15 seconds of lag to reach two stars. The only hard constraints are that passive stays below one star, average reaches at least one star, and good reaches at least two stars.\n\nThe tier2 star bands are now defined as passive below one star, good at least one star, and expert at least two stars. This makes the average tier eligible for three stars.\n\nPassive performance fails most activities that require input, including step, hoop, jog, slalom, tray, and laundry.\n\nStep accuracy is zero when every note is missed because the chart must finish playing to register misses. A bot with a slight offset will hit notes.\n\nStep accuracy thresholds are now set. A 100 millisecond offset scores 0.4, landing poor below one star. A 40 millisecond offset scores 1.0 for three stars. A 20 millisecond offset scores 1.0 for two stars. Tier 2 expert needs a 10 millisecond offset to score perfectly.\n\nCharts must run at least sixty seconds at one hundred beats per minute so accuracy is meaningful. The bot will call rhythm.hit on the correct lane at each note time plus an offset, using audio.pos.step. Headless mode will use game.time when audio sync is active.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0e222a20-b69c-406c-91cf-1bc68ddfee93-43","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"const LIFE|const SCORE|function notesOf","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0e222a20-b69c-406c-91cf-1bc68ddfee93-44","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js","offset":13100,"limit":50}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3380,"output_tokens":3794,"cache_read_input_tokens":112640,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"3d878d4d-3a68-48f6-b394-d168f9fda30a"}
