"line_number\":88,\"content\":\"const warn = (key, msg) => { if (_warned.has(key)) return; _warned.add(key); console.warn('my-3D2dge: ' + msg); };\"},{\"line_number\":89,\"content\":\"E.warn = warn;\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"1a9ae295-a78f-4e48-b5cb-67f33a9014f2"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_125","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"check.mjs records console warnings starting with my-3D2dge: as engineWarnings. They are not fatal.\n\nUpdating balance.js for anisotropic axes first. Then writing the remaining files. The confirm pump bug is fixed. Focus will cycle to the next unplayed guide activity after each completion. Day 1 flow: body → session stork → island squat → step.\n\nDay 1 flow now includes title, slots, member, arrival, bungalow, body, session stork, island squat, session, island step, island bed, evening, bed, and day 2 bungalow. After three sessions the bed becomes focused. Evening triggers talk, auto sleep, then the next dawn. The evening onDone callback skips directly to the bungalow scene.\n\nDay 4 flow: bungalow → body → tare scene (one confirm) → island with lunge focused. Day 5 flow: bungalow → short body → games queue. The pump stops at the ending scene when freePlay is true, and the final confirm returns to the free play island. In passive mode, activities run without player input, accumulate minimal minutes, and may not trigger all unlocks.\n\nScripted mode unlocks at 30 minutes. Spine age falls and day 3 hits a plateau. All 12 activities play before free play opens. Tests verify completion by reading G.st.played from the VM state.\n\nSnapshot fields will include an array of unlock IDs, a saves summary, and best stars. A test play function is being written to automate scripted runs by advancing the game state until the ending or free play phase is reached.\n\nThe test pump taps confirm and advances one frame each loop. Stuck detection counts identical scene, phase, day, session, activity, and left-timer snapshots. It tolerates a few repeats from the one-frame delay before failing.\n\nDialog pages advance via page index without changing scene or phase, so the stuck-detection key must include that index. A `beat` counter may be added to the snapshot to track progress.\n\nAuto phases will transition immediately when the left timer reaches zero to prevent spinning. Session timers will advance using real game time delta. Fade duration is set to zero for headless tests and to a short default in the browser.\n\nThe browser fadeTime will be 0.15 seconds. Headless tests set it to zero at boot. Boot steps must not call go() because game.go is deferred during startup. Deep links are handled after start by reading the URL hash in the initial scene choice. Views are configured on the game object after startup.\n\ngame.views will be set once after boot to avoid scene-enter resets. Fixed scenes will disable camera zoom.\n\nThe Step class uses wheel input for stepping, so every scene needs zoom disabled to prevent the wheel from triggering camera zoom instead. The scene camera property is applied via an `ok('camera')` call during enter. Studio scenes will use the brawler view, island scenes the threequarter view, and laundry scenes the side view.\n\nSession scenes switch the camera view based on the current activity. Mode7 and Road are instantiated only when needed and drawn conditionally. The Road constructor is created during setup even in headless mode to exercise that code path, but it is only rendered if the road flag is active.\n\nA physics world will be created in setup and stepped during update so tray scoring works in headless mode. The gravity vector will be set from the plan's values. Kinematic olives will be used alongside the physics world to score from whichever is stable.\n\nThe physics world will use real scoring. Olives are circles with bounce 0.2, friction 0.2, and damping; glass is a sensor; walls are chains. Gravity updates each step. Bullet mode prevents tunneling if needed. The tray is roughly 200 by 140 pixels. A stable balance keeps passive p near 0.05. Gravity at 21 pixels per second squared may slide olives across the tray over twenty seconds.\n\nA lip wall will block passive olives from climbing, since their lean is too small. The bot will hold upward tilt to reach about 0.8 and slide olives into the glass sensor. Scoring will prefer the physics world and fall back to a kinematic slide if physics is unstable in tests. Lines, content, audio, rigs, and UI will be written in one batch before play, world, and boot.\n\nThe balance anisotropic patch introduces a helper to read axis-specific stiffness values from the set object. When a custom kx is provided, it multiplies by the developer multiplier so the dev modifier applies correctly.\n\nThe developer multiplier is now applied inside the balance step function. I will use search_replace to update it, then compose the remaining files carefully offline. I am checking the E.font.text signature and will draw UI panels with px.disc, px.rect, and px.roundRect.\n\nHeadless mode leaves the renderer null when draw is disabled, so drawing functions must guard against a missing r object. The title scene will display \"Spine Wee\" in both the snapshot and the browser document title. Browser launch tests will verify the title appears, the canvas is painted and not at its default 300 by 150 size, and keyboard focus moves correctly. The launch verification will be implemented in tests/run.mjs for the browser group and as a separate verification script per the plan, with specific log files required.\n\nActivities reference standard set names such as stork1, sun, candle, laundry, and body. Repetition sets are custom and will be attached to activity tier data rather than the global band table; Balance only needs the fields it receives.\n\nA shared `G.zeroIntent()` helper will initialize intent objects with fields for movement, breathing, catching, jumping, pacing, stepping, and source. A `G.readIntent()` function is being drafted to map raw input into these intent fields, starting with pad movement.\n\nThe `readIntent` function now computes pad magnitude and source flags. It detects keyboard lean from WASD or arrow keys, records the last key press time, and prefers mouse input only when it is active, newer than the last key press, not a gamepad, and the pad is nearly idle. Developer nudge and home multipliers are fetched from the dev state.\n\nGamepad input is mapped to x and y when pad magnitude exceeds 0.12, or 0.05 for gamepads. Mouse input is converted to normalized coordinates from the screen center using the developer reach multiplier, with a y flip to match screen down versus game up.\n\nThe lean y-axis is forward. Up on screen is positive forward, so the mouse y offset must be inverted to match the game coordinate system. The plan’s squat depth formula clamps negative py to zero, meaning downward movement triggers squatting.\n\nThe mouse y-offset is confirmed: pointer below screen centre yields negative lean. When neither gamepad nor mouse input is active, the function falls back to keyboard or gamepad and begins scanning held keys for horizontal movement.\n\nThe keyboard fallback now maps WASD and arrow keys to horizontal and vertical intent. It also checks action-down states for right, left, up, and down, because the test harness triggers these via Act: codes rather than raw KeyD.\n\nThe keyboard fallback risks double-counting input when KeyD is mapped to the right action. When developer mode is active, arrow keys also trigger the right action, so they must be excluded. A cleaner `leanKeys` helper is being drafted to avoid this overlap.\n\nThe `leanKeys` helper now checks whether an action is held via `Act:` or raw keys. When developer mode is active, it suppresses arrow-key-only input so the arrows do not trigger lean. It only allows an arrow if its mapped key or the action itself is also held.\n\nThe leanKeys helper now normalizes the final horizontal and vertical values to unit length. The nudge logic is being rewritten as a velocity-driven interpolation: while a target direction exists, the lean moves toward it at 2.2 units per second; otherwise it drifts home at 0.5 units per second.\n\nMouse input is clamped to unit length. The nudge velocity now moves lean toward the target at 2.2 units per second while held, or drifts home at 0.5 units per second when idle. Breathing logic is being drafted.\n\nBreath input is now handled separately from pace. The plan distinguishes toggle mode, which switches on press, and ramp mode, which smoothly interpolates the raw value over four seconds. Digital inputs such as Space will ramp toward ±1, while analog gamepad axes are read directly.\n\nBreath is now clamped to a step size of dt/4, so it reaches ±1 in four seconds. Gamepad analog triggers such as L2 and R2 will feed directly into the breathing value.\n\nGamepad analog axes such as L2 and R2 will feed directly into the breathing value without ramping. Keyboard and mouse inputs will still ramp smoothly over four seconds.\n\nCatch, jump, pace, and step are now handled by checking pressed or down states per frame. The Step class reads input.pressed for rhythm, so the bot uses rh.hit instead of synthesizing intent.\n\nThe engine's Rhythm.update only reads game.input, so it cannot handle multiple players. Each contestant needs its own rhythm instance. I will call hit manually from each player's pressed state before updating, avoiding double-reads.\n\nCalling `rh.update()` would read player one's input and wrongly hit other contestants' rhythm charts. I will instead call `rh.hit` manually when each player presses a lane, then run a shared miss pass that skips the engine's automatic input read.\n\nI am now drafting a `G.rhythmMiss` helper that replicates the engine's miss and end-of-note logic from `rh.update`, so I can call it manually for each contestant without touching `game.input`.\n\nFor bots, calling `rh.hit` before `update` is safe because their input is not pressed. For local human players, the engine's `update` would still read player one's input and register it on the other rhythm chart. The fix is to set a `_player` property on each rhythm instance and patch `update` so it only checks that specific player's input.\n\nA `stepRh` helper will temporarily swap `game.input.pressed` and `released` with the target player's methods before calling `rh.update()`, then restore them in a `try/finally` block. Bots will use a fake player object that always returns false for pressed and released.\n\nOnly the first rhythm instance will call `rh.start()` to begin the song. The remaining rhythms will reset their note state and set `started = true` without restarting the audio, so all charts share one clock.\n\nThe class step is 35 seconds rather than 60. The chart can remain the same; accuracy will be computed at the 35-second mark.\n\nPassive bots will miss most notes while good bots hit perfectly, so accuracy at 35 seconds will be a clean split. Perfect hits will raise life from its starting value of 0.5 or 1. The laundry average falls at 2.5 in 45 seconds is too high because the band has no walking push and extra gusts of ±9 far outweigh the normal push of 1.8. I am now ready to write the code.\n\nGusts of ±9 will likely cause stumbles. The laundry bot will pre-lean opposite the incoming gust to counter it, while passive bots do not counter and fall.\n\nGust telegraphing starts 0.8 seconds early, so the bot can react immediately or late. Pre-lean may still cause one or two falls on average. The laundry board is inherently unstable with the current stiffness and spring constants, so gusts of ±9 will likely trigger stumbles despite pre-lean. Without gusts, the good bot achieves 79% steadiness with zero falls.\n\nA 0.6-second gust of ±9 may still exceed the radius-1 limit despite pre-lean, since damping and spring forces fight the acceleration spike. The developer slider defaults to 1, so ±9 is a tuning start value rather than a fixed design number. If the good bot falls too often, the activity's gust strength can be reduced in the designer data while passive bots continue to fail.\n\nGust strength is fixed at 9 for now and will be tuned down after testing. The plan is to write the balance anisotropic update and all game files, then run the rules tests. Tuning numbers are not deviations. The core of play.js will define G.activity() and family runners for hold, candle, rep, step, hoop, jog, slalom, tray, and laundry.\n\nThe session runner in world.js will create a context object and call activity setup, update, and draw. The context will hold id, tier, act, rng, seed, time, duration, contestants, player, limit, modes, and extra data. The activity interface will define id, name, family, discipline, view, blurb, star thresholds, class and hub flags, pay, and tiers.\n\nThe activity interface now includes tier data with fields like set, dur, ringEvery, gull, and eyes. Setup, update, result, bot, draw, and hud are defined as hooks. Generic family methods in play.js will attach to registered activity data rather than living inside each entry.\n\nActivity data will be stored in `G.ACT_DATA` and registered via `play.js` by merging each entry with its family methods. Pose definitions for the stork clip will live in `content.js`. The lint test will verify that every referenced animation clip exists.\n\nThe draw routine will stay simple but recognizable. It will include a sky, floor, sea, and basic character shapes with rigs when available, plus a plate, UI text, and title logo.\n\nA `G.drawTare` helper will render Tare as a white slab with footprints and eyes. Gerald will be drawn as a small hippo, with an unlocked friend variant.\n\nWardrobe cosmetics such as towels, headbands, sunhats, and bow ties will be data-driven and visible on the player rig in the bungalow and during play.\n\nI am starting to write the files now. The hub deal function will accept a random generator and pick three activities from fixed pools, preserving deterministic output for tests.\n\nI will test `G.hubDeal` by calling it twice with the same seeded random generator and asserting identical output. I will also verify that two fresh `AIHub.rng('deal')` calls with the same `hub.seed` produce the same deal, since the seed defaults to zero before the welcome screen.\n\nThe developer will export `G.hub.report` and `G.hub.stop` so the time-up handler can call them. A test can install a fake hub by replacing those methods, though capturing the original reference first may prevent the replacement from working.\n\nThe test will temporarily replace `AIHub.result` with a counter so it can verify that `G.hub.report()` only calls the real handler once. It will also verify that `G.hub.onEnd()` sets the over flag and mutes audio.\n\nThe hub report will call `AIHub.result` with the score, stats, and awards. The test will also verify that input is cleared after the round ends. Poses will be defined in `content.js` and exposed through a thin `game/data/poses.js` wrapper.\n\nThe developer is weighing whether to split game data into separate files or keep it in one content.js with a README. The plan requires designers to edit individual files under game/data/, so the split approach better satisfies that requirement.\n\nThe developer chose separate data files over a single content file. Activities.js will hold the main activity registry, and charts.js will define step chart patterns with repeated part orders to reach roughly 400 steps.\n\nStep chart order will repeat 'A A B A' six times for roughly 384 steps at 100 BPM. Tier two charts will use 30 parts at 120 BPM. ASCII tray layouts will be parsed into physics bodies with a 16-pixel scale, containing olives, glasses, bumpers, and geckos.\n\nA parser will read ASCII tray layouts into physics bodies. Each tray will take about twenty seconds. Passive bots will tilt toward the glasses and fail to reach them because of distance and a high lip. The scoring model will require olives to enter the glass sensor.\n\nPhysics substeps will be tuned to roughly 28,800 per tray run, and constants will live at the top of each family file. The developer is starting the first batch of files: data, balance, audio, rigs, and UI. play.js, world.js, and boot.js are the next major targets. Scenes in world.js will stay thin.\n\nboot.js will handle input, state, saves, snapshot, driver, attract idle, dev mode, and hub connect. It will also create the game shell, CSS, settings, and start the engine.\n\nworld.js will own all scenes, story transitions, and the session runner that calls activities.\n\nplay.js will hold the family runners.\n\nA `G.snap()` helper will be added for debug snapshots.\n\nThe attract playlist will cycle through 15-second clips using cheat and expert drivers. A controller in the attract scene or session scene will advance the playlist by time.\n\nThe attract playlist will cycle through twelve demo scenes and activities, including the stork and sun poses. During attraction, the session auto-starts with expert and cheat drivers while skipping the card screen. A test will record every seen ID by advancing 15-second intervals and assert that all items are visited. Any input returns to the title scene. The award function will check G.attracting to avoid saving during attract mode.\n\nA `results` scene will display coins and scores. The attract playlist will use a session enter preset with `attract: true`, `id`, and `resultOnly: true`. The attract timer will be driven by a global `G.attractTick` step hook that decrements remaining time and advances the playlist when it expires.\n\nAttract mode will cut each activity at 15 seconds even if the activity itself is longer. The test will record every visited playlist item on entry and assert that advancing past all items plus one loops back to the start. A full playlist run is roughly 255 seconds, or about 30,600 physics steps, which is acceptable. To keep headless attract tests from crashing, rig updates will be skipped when no renderer exists.\n\nActivities will always update balance. The island scene will use simple stick-based movement and mouse hover highlighting for the confirm interaction, avoiding a full tilemap.\n\nIsland will be a painted three-quarter view with mat hotspots. Hotspots will be stored as screen rectangles during drawing for mouse interaction, and headless tests will cycle focus with left and right while confirming with space.\n\nThe member screen will use a pill list starting with \"This is me\" at focus index 0. Left and right will cycle through hair, skin, top, bottom, name, and reroll options while keeping the confirm target fixed on the first pill.\n\nThe slot screen will show three slots, with the first empty slot focused by default. Confirming on a filled slot loads that member; confirming on an empty slot opens the member creation flow. The title scene will disable Continue when no saves exist and default focus to New Member.\n\nDisabled pills will be skipped when navigating with the pump. The title scene will place New Member at focus index 0 so confirm selects it directly and avoids disabled buttons. Free Play will remain disabled until the session ends. The developer is ready to begin writing code.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The twelve-seed bands match the table (worst gap 7.9). Next I’ll extend the Lean for the rail exercises and start the rest of the game."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8888b13b-fb0c-4552-b991-560afc2f6d3b-51","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/game/balance.js","old_string":"        const ax = L.k * (this.ux - this.px) - L.c * this.vx + L.s * this.px + L.A * this.nx(this.t) + pfx;\n        const ay = L.k * (this.uy - this.py) - L.c * this.vy + L.s * this.py + L.A * this.ny(this.t) + pfy;","new_string":"        const dk = L.k / (this.set.k || 1), dc = L.c / (this.set.c || 1), ds = L.s / (this.set.s || 1);\n        const kx = (this.set.kx != null ? this.set.kx : this.set.k) * dk, ky = (this.set.ky != null ? this.set.ky : this.set.k) * dk;\n        const cx = (this.set.cx != null ? this.set.cx : this.set.c) * dc, cy = (this.set.cy != null ? this.set.cy : this.set.c) * dc;\n        const sx = (this.set.sx != null ? this.set.sx : this.set.s) * ds, sy = (this.set.sy != null ? this.set.sy : this.set.s) * ds;\n        const ax = kx * (this.ux - this.px) - cx * this.vx + sx * this.px + L.A * this.nx(this.t) + pfx;\n        const ay = ky * (this.uy - this.py) - cy * this.vy + sy * this.py + L.A * this.ny(this.t) + pfy;"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":352,"output_tokens":11389,"cache_read_input_tokens":175488,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"8c32fd40-7a2f-4efb-af63-83350aff9cc5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-8888b13b-fb0c-4552-b991-560afc2f6d3b-51","content":"{\"type\":\"SearchReplace\",\"EditsApplied\":{\"old_string\":\"        const ax = L.k * (this.ux - this.px) - L.c * this.vx + L.s * this.px + L.A * this.nx(this.t) + pfx;\\n        const ay = L.k * (this.uy - this.py) - L.c * this.vy + L.s * this.py + L.A * this.ny(this.t) + pfy;\",\"new_string\":\"        const dk = L.k / (this.set.k || 1), dc = L.c / (this.set.c || 1), ds = L.s / (this.set.s || 1);\\n        const kx = (this.set.kx != null ? this.set.kx : this.set.k) * dk, ky = (this.set.ky != null ? this.set.ky : this.set.k) * dk;\\n        const cx = (this.set.cx != null ? this.set.cx : this.set.c) * dc, cy = (this.set.cy != null ? this.set.cy : this.set.c) * dc;\\n        const sx = (this.set.sx != null ? this.set.sx : this.set.s) * ds, sy = (this.set.sy != null ? this.set.sy : this.set.s) * ds;\\n        const ax = kx * (this.ux - this.px) - cx * this.vx + sx * this.px + L.A * this.nx(this.t) + pfx;\\n        const ay = ky * (this.uy - this.py) - cy * this.vy + sy * this.py + L.A * this.ny(this.t) + pfy;\",\"tool_output_for_prompt\":\"The file /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/game/balance.js has been updated successfully.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/game/balance.js has been updated.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/game/balance.js\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"        const ax = L.k * (this.ux - this.px) - L.c * this.vx + L.s * this.px + L.A * this.nx(this.t) + pfx;\\n        const ay = L.k * (this.uy - this.py) - L.c * this.vy + L.s * this.py + L.A * this.ny(this.t) + pfy;\",\"old_line\":111,\"new_string\":\"        const dk = L.k / (this.set.k || 1), dc = L.c / (this.set.c || 1), ds = L.s / (this.set.s || 1);\\n        const kx = (this.set.kx != null ? this.set.kx : this.set.k) * dk, ky = (this.set.ky != null ? this.set.ky : this.set.k) * dk;\\n        const cx = (this.set.cx != null ? this.set.cx : this.set.c) * dc, cy = (this.set.cy != null ? this.set.cy : this.set.c) * dc;\\n        const sx = (this.set.sx != null ? this.set.sx : this.set.s) * ds, sy = (this.set.sy != null ? this.set.sy : this.set.s) * ds;\\n        const ax = kx * (this.ux - this.px) - cx * this.vx + sx * this.px + L.A * this.nx(this.t) + pfx;\\n        const ay = ky * (this.uy - this.py) - cy * this.vy + sy * this.py + L.A * this.ny(this.t) + pfy;\",\"new_line\":111,\"context_before\":\"          }\\n          if (use) { pfx += bx; pfy += by; }\\n        }\\n\",\"context_after\":\"        this.vx += ax * dt; this.vy += ay * dt; this.px += this.vx * dt; this.py += this.vy * dt;\\n        if (!this.cheat && Math.hypot(this.px, this.py) >= L.stumbleAt) {\\n          this.stumble = 1; this.falls++;\\n\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"2732e2ae-3f62-4fbd-8245-93a627ee3cca"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_126","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Realized that if set.c is zero, dc becomes zero and cx also becomes zero.\n\nVerified that zero values for set.s and set.k also yield zero deltas. Confirmed that dev multiplication and raw mode preserve bands correctly. Floating-point division checks out.\n\nPreparing to write the data and code files in parallel batches. Confirmed that gentle mode only adjusts s, A, and R, so set.k remains unchanged for the Balance calculation. Will be careful with scoring formulas in play.js.\n\nDefined the bead position functions for tier 1 and tier 2. Depth is clamped to the range [0, 1]. The bot will chase -bead with uy. With k=16, c=8, and s=0, the body tracks uy with some lag.\n\nInstantly chasing the bead yields a score of roughly 0.93. Passive play scores near zero. Form penalty stays small because the bot keeps px near zero. Fatigue will be simulated by raising A and lowering k through live().\n\nWill clone the balance set to apply fatigue without mutating G.SETS. If the mean score exceeds 0.8, two extra reps will be added. Rep count scoring averages |depth-bead| at each rep's end and maps that mean to a 0..1 scale. Form penalty equals min(0.3, formMean), where formMean is the average |px| across reps.\n\nDefined a `G.starCount` helper that returns star levels based on the activity threshold array, or the dev-mode slider values when dev mode is active.\n\nStar thresholds will always use the activity array, never the dev sliders, to avoid breaking star gates during tests. Sun stars are set to [0.5, 0.75, 0.92]. Started writing the sun breath match guide as an 8-second triangle wave.\n\nThe sun guide is now a full 8-second triangle wave. The bot will track the guide with a 0.15-second lag, keeping the match error under 0.25. Passive play matches only about 25% of the time. Sun poses will use 5 poses at 16 seconds each. Will implement a simple draw function, add 12 short audio loops, and define SFX.\n\nExported the babble function and built a voice table. Will write roughly 160 genuine short lines into lines.js rather than generating garbage. Files will be created in batches: data first, then systems, then play/world/boot, then tests. The activities data structure is being locked now so play.js can match it.\n\nLocked the Stork Stand activity data structure. It defines two tiers using sets 'stork1' and 'stork2', each with 45-second durations, move values of 12 and 10, and gull parameters. The entry includes a demo line, a card line, and star thresholds.\n\nSettled on `class: true` as the key name since `class` is a reserved keyword. The hold-family update will live in play.js and read tier fields directly. Will write play.js to look up G.ACTIVITIES and register them. Defined tier sets with explicit k, c, s, A, and nf values for both reps tiers.\n\nDuration will be reps times period so the activity ends exactly when reps finish. Extra reps extend duration. Push-ups will use a shorter tier of 24 seconds. Side lunge will use axis x and clamp depth to [0, 1]. The bead will swing from -1 to 1, and the bot will chase it with u = px. Scoring will compare mean |px - bead| against that range.\n\nThe lunge scoring tolerance is 0.08, which is very tight against a -1 to 1 bead swing. The bead will stay in the 0 to 1 range, with its sign indicating side.\n\nThe lunge bead will alternate between 0→1→0 on the right and left sides each rep. Depth is clamped to [0, 1] using the side sign, and the bot chases it with ux = side * bead.\n\nHoop scoring uses angular velocity from movement magnitude above 0.55. Score rate scales with hoop count up to six. Catches award +40 points and arrive every eight seconds. The bot circles at 9 rad/s with |u|=0.9 inside the band, catching all six on time for a near-perfect score.\n\nKeyboard input is multiplied by 0.6 because quadrant steps are slower than stick movement; the bot uses a continuous circle so it skips that penalty. A 1.5-second out-of-band drop removes one hoop, but passive play scores zero anyway. At the halfway point, the prompt \"Other way!\" requires the bot to reverse its angular direction; if it fails, the speed sign is wrong and nothing scores. Started drafting the Jog activity.\n\nJog scoring weights zone time at 70% and cup collection at 30%. The bot presses every 1/3 second, steers toward the next cup, and stays in the 2.6–3.4 band to collect five cups placed at ±1.2. It scores roughly 0.95. Passive play scores zero because it never moves or collects. Stitch is triggered if cadence exceeds 4.2 for two seconds. Started drafting Slalom.\n\nSlalom scoring uses a 3D course where z advances with speed and x turns with ux. Gates at z award points only if the player stays within width; missing a gate adds three seconds to total time. Obstacles cause a tumble that halts speed for 1.2 seconds. The clean run is about 52 seconds, the par is 58, and the worst is 90. Passive play scores zero because it never moves and misses every gate. A good run steers to each gate before arrival, hits all gates cleanly, and finishes near 55 seconds for a perfect score. Tier two narrows the gate width.\n\nSlalom steering is simple enough for a bot to hit gates cleanly. The x-turn rate is 80 units per second, and the gate spacing of 670 lets the bot steer within ±80 with ease.\n\nNow moving on to Tray and Laundry. For Laundry, the bot will set effort from its breath value, which scales speed by 1.6 and adds extra stiffness to the balance set. Gusts will be scheduled, and jumps will freeze position by passing zero velocity to the physics step.\n\nLaundry jumps will freeze the balance object by skipping its physics step while a freeze flag is active. This keeps the body in place without counting as a stumble, and activity frames continue to pass normally.\n\nLaundry scoring now subtracts 0.06 points per fall from the final percentage, and the activity is considered unfinished until the bot reaches the end. The course will be 55 units long at a speed of effort times 2.2, giving a par of 58 seconds and a worst of 95. Gusts will arrive every eight seconds for the bot to counter.\n\nDuel candle scoring will compare Gertrude's flame to the player's. The bot keeps its flame near 1 for the first 75 seconds, then Gertrude's weakens after that point and drains 0.02 per second. The bot wins if its flame stays higher than hers at the 90-second mark. Passive play loses because its flame dies.\n\nGentle games are capped at 20 seconds but still award real scores and pay minutes so unlocks continue. Twelve sessions already cover the full unlock schedule.\n\nDay 5 short body check is reduced to eight seconds with a `short: true` path flag.\n\nBody check will auto-complete the pump and lean phases without requiring player input. Balance physics still run during these segments, so they count as real activity. The full body check flow is roughly 11 seconds of auto-sequence followed by a question menu and two talk pages. Tare day will show one talk page with its line; confirming closes it and advances to the island. A flag will ensure tare day only appears once.\n\nThe snapshot object will carry scene, activity, tier, day, session, percentage, stars, minutes, spine age, lean position, source, phase, free play, unlocks, saves, attract mode, dev mode, plateau, title, ending, duel win state, left, beat, and focus. Save slots will be counted by filtering out empty or damaged entries. A `played` array of activity IDs will also be included for the playthrough test.\n\nNow writing the code. Adding CSS for the shell in index and build. Setting game options id to spinewee. Configuring menu.settings with custom labels for voice slider, while music and glyphs merge without labels.\n\nAdded three game settings: a Gentle Mode toggle, a Breath mode selector with Hold and Toggle options, and a Tare's Tips toggle. Defined control labels for Confirm, Back, Pause, Start, Breath or effort, Catch left, Catch right, Jump, Talk, More time, and Retry.\n\nDefined control labels for jog and step directions, plus lean directions. Added Restart Session and Dev Mode items to the menu when not in a session. Quitting returns to the title scene. Autosave will trigger on entering the title if a member exists and the current mode is not attract or hub.\n\nRestart will return to the current activity if one is active, or to the bungalow otherwise. It will not wipe the save file.\n\nA shell disposal issue was spotted: the Game constructor auto-creates a system shell, but then a second Shell instance is created and disposes it. The headless path may skip this conflict.\n\nHeadless mode avoids the shell conflict because the Game constructor only creates a system shell when not headless, and the second Shell call returns early. The pause menu still exists in headless but will not render. The dev menu item will point to the same `G.dev.open` function used by tests. A new `installShell(game)` helper will handle shell setup, including setting the document title and adding a hover listener.\n\nBoot messages are being restructured as boot steps that perform real initialization: zeroing the scale for audio, sanding the instructors for rigs, counting the ducks for data, and telling the hippo for the hub. The hub initialization must register its listeners before the connect call, and because _boot runs these steps before start() enters the scene, this ordering is safe.\n\nThe boot sequence awaits any promise returned by a step before advancing. This means the AI hub connection can be started inside a boot step without blocking the scene entry, because the engine will wait for it to resolve.\n\nThe AI hub connect promise resolves on a microtask, so the boot step returns immediately without blocking the scene entry. Headless mode will wait for this resolution before continuing.\n\nA flaw was spotted in the boot sequence. If a boot step returns a promise, `_boot` returns before `start()` is called, so `boot()` resolves while the scene is still idle. Headless tests will therefore need to await `game.booted` directly rather than assuming the scene has started.\n\nBoot steps will remain synchronous. The AI hub connect call will not return its promise, so the boot sequence does not wait for it. The ready signal and scene transition will instead be handled inside the promise callback after game.booted resolves. This prevents headless tests from blocking on a promise that never resolves.\n\nThe round scene will poll for AI hub events in its update loop while the connection info is still loading. Once the info arrives, it can immediately deal cards and process welcome events that may have queued before the scene entered.\n\nThe round scene will wait for the AI hub welcome event before dealing cards, ensuring the shared seed is set first. A flag will prevent double dealing. The card display and answer menu will appear immediately, while the ready signal stays separate and automatic. During the card phase, time is frozen at negative infinity, so ghost updates are deferred until play begins.\n\nGhost updates will wait until the playing phase begins after release, once time is no longer frozen. Progress will be sent every update while time is non-negative, so the first progress fires immediately. The card scene will draw Ready and More buttons, store their rectangles, and handle clicks for answers and more actions. Confirm input will not pause or start the activity during the card phase.\n\nThe card phase is treated as a menu, so a confirm input during the card will answer ready. The round scene runs on wall-clock time from AIHub.time() rather than game.time, which prevents desync on slow frames. The local simulation can still step with delta time, and ghosts send their positions. The harness only needs the score to be a number.\n\nThe local activity will run during the hub event using real update logic. It will step with delta time and report its accumulated score. If the event is cut short, the result will be whatever score has been reached so far. The activity RNG will be seeded once from AIHub.rng using the member index.\n\nThe activity's partial percentage will be multiplied by 1000. No native browser dialogs such as alert, confirm, or window.open will be used; only the custom E.Dialog is allowed. Boot will catch Mocap.load failures and fall back to null so it never throws. The fitclips asset is large but loads synchronously. Ready to write the files now.\n\nAdding the README, index, and build files. Writing a solid test suite in tests/run.mjs. Re-running the bands simulation to verify anisotropic division still works correctly.\n\nThe prior bands run finished quickly and safely within the tool timeout, so re-running it is unnecessary; the rules test will catch any regression. A NaN risk was checked and dismissed because set.c is never zero. A bug was found in the rep-set scaling formula: when `s` is zero and `sx` is non-zero, `ds` becomes zero and `sx` collapses to zero.\n\nThe rep-set scaling bug is fixed by computing the dev multiplier directly from `G.dev` instead of deriving it from `L.s / set.s`. This prevents `sx` from collapsing to zero when `s` is zero. The corrected formula now applies the dev values `dk`, `dc`, and `ds` to the set's `kx`, `ky`, `cx`, `cy`, and `sx` fields.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b274d3b2-0041-4102-89fb-b66c87c1d160-52","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/game/balance.js","old_string":"        const dk = L.k / (this.set.k || 1), dc = L.c / (this.set.c || 1), ds = L.s / (this.set.s || 1);\n        const kx = (this.set.kx != null ? this.set.kx : this.set.k) * dk, ky = (this.set.ky != null ? this.set.ky : this.set.k) * dk;\n        const cx = (this.set.cx != null ? this.set.cx : this.set.c) * dc, cy = (this.set.cy != null ? this.set.cy : this.set.c) * dc;\n        const sx = (this.set.sx != null ? this.set.sx : this.set.s) * ds, sy = (this.set.sy != null ? this.set.sy : this.set.s) * ds;","new_string":"        const dmul = !this.raw && G.dev && G.dev.on ? G.dev.v : null;\n        const dk = dmul ? dmul.k : 1, dc = dmul ? dmul.c : 1, ds = dmul ? dmul.s : 1;\n        const kx = (this.set.kx != null ? this.set.kx : this.set.k) * dk, ky = (this.set.ky != null ? this.set.ky : this.set.k) * dk;\n        const cx = (this.set.cx != null ? this.set.cx : this.set.c) * dc, cy = (this.set.cy != null ? this.set.cy : this.set.c) * dc;\n        const sx = (this.set.sx != null ? this.set.sx : this.set.s) * ds, sy = (this.set.sy != null ? this.set.sy : this.set.s) * ds;"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":11571,"output_tokens":7966,"cache_read_input_tokens":175744,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"a1457bbd-ecba-487f-8ce8-b0aeb2e0f972"}
