08/game/balance.js has been updated.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/game/balance.js\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"      if (this.holdP) {\\n        this.ux = 0; this.uy = 0;\\n      }\\n      const dx = this.px - cx, dy = this.py - cy;\",\"old_line\":127,\"new_string\":\"      const dx = this.px - cx, dy = this.py - cy;\",\"new_line\":127,\"context_before\":\"          const h = Math.hypot(this.px, this.py) || 1; this.px /= h; this.py /= h;\\n        }\\n      }\\n\",\"context_after\":\"      if (this.stumble <= 0 && Math.hypot(dx, dy) < L.R) this.inside++;\\n      this.frames++;\\n      this.t += dt;\\n\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"63fb0631-041f-4fec-b352-9d0703342ff6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_130","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"holdP is true. Physics is skipped. The count still proceeds.\n\nThe holdP freeze logic is confirmed correct. Now writing the game source files, starting with large data modules.\n\nThe plan calls for separate data files. I'll create them now.\n\nI'll consolidate all designer data into a single `game/data/content.js` file to stay within token limits. I'm now writing `lines.js`, `audio.js`, `rigs.js`, and `ui.js`.\n\nNow writing `play.js`, the core game logic file. I'm sketching shared helpers for clamping values, resolving actor skills, and calculating input lag.\n\nA `zero()` helper is defined to reset actor states. The stork family update is being sketched: the center moves every tier.move seconds to a precomputed nearby point, gulls trigger at fixed times, and balance uses skill-based construction. Sun will also track breath. Special-case handling for specific activity IDs is being considered.\n\nThe `hold` family is being implemented in `play.js`. Setup precomputes Balance objects for each actor using a seeded random generator. Bot skill is applied to all actors except the sun. The result function computes a score from steadiness minus fall penalties.\n\nThe sun hold result blends steadiness and breath match into a single score. The class activity will evaluate each contestant individually. For sun, the balance skill is being reconsidered so the bot can control both movement and breathing directly.\n\nSun bot breathing is now computed from the skill lagged guide instead of direct intent. Human players still use intent for breath. Match requires the absolute difference between breath and guide to be within 0.25. Candle is kept as a separate family because it uses flame mechanics.\n\nGull and thought animations are precomputed from the seeded RNG at setup so all hub copies match exactly.\n\nThe stork center moves in a small circle every 12 seconds. The bot tracks this center to keep the stork high.\n\nCandle thought forces are applied as extra pushes on top of table physics. They last 2.5 seconds every 7 seconds with magnitude 3.5. Passive bots get pushed out by these forces.\n\nCandle thought force is set to 3.0, the lower bound of the design range. A passive actor who remains untouched for 20 seconds will trigger a non-blocking story line.\n\nThe 20-second passive line will be a non-blocking subtitle. It sets a context note so the session can display it without pausing the activity or triggering modal dialogs.\n\nCandle flame drains when velocity exceeds 0.55 or the actor leaves the ring, and refills while inside the ring at low speed. The flame can drop to zero, forcing a relight; a second drain ends the activity early with current scores. Passive candle bots typically score only 15% steadiness, often sit outside the ring, and burn through flame quickly.\n\nCandle tier 2 will add goose pushes and thoughts every four seconds. A mean flame tracker will be added. The world.js story is being restructured with a scene helper.\n\nA scene helper is being defined in world.js. It sets input to null initially because boot.js defines G.INPUT after world.js loads. Boot.js will assign the real input object to scenes after INPUT exists. A cleaner approach is to have world.js call G.makeScenes() from boot.js once INPUT is ready.\n\nG.makeScenes will be defined in world.js and invoked from boot.js after INPUT is ready. The class scene will use its own input object, G.CLASS_INPUT. Story state machine helpers like G.storyGo, G.beginDay, and G.finishSession will live in world.js. World.js will handle menus and flow, while play.js handles activities. The session scene will call G.actUpdate.\n\nUI pills and drawing studio helpers will be added to ui.js. Total test duration across all activities, tiers, and skills is roughly 500,000 steps.\n\nTotal test duration is roughly 700,000 steps. At 0.02 ms per step that is about 14 seconds; at 0.1 ms it is about 70 seconds. Tray physics adds roughly 172,000 substeps, which is acceptable. Sun is the longest activity at 80 seconds. Music clock and audio tick overhead should be negligible in headless mode. If tests are too slow, tier durations can be shortened.\n\nSun must keep its full 80-second duration so the no-input score of about 0.4 depends on complete breath cycles. The star gate test requires this length.\n\nPassive step activities will run until the chart is finished or the time limit expires. This preserves honesty while letting passive bots end early when they fail.\n\nThe star gate test will call `G.trial(id, tier, skill)` to run the activity inside the real game. It will drive the shipped session from its true start rather than skipping ahead to the activity. The activity itself is the unit under test.\n\nThe star gate test will drive the real session path, including card presentation and confirmations, then hand control to the activity. A `runAct` helper is being drafted to set the driver skill before starting.\n\nThe trial helper will enter the session scene and advance one frame. It will then loop until the phase leaves the card or demo menus. A simpler approach is to start trials directly in the play phase, skipping the demo entirely, since scoring does not depend on it. The card presentation path will be tested separately in the playthrough test.\n\nThe star gate test will pump through the card, demo, and countdown menus before entering play. This adds roughly 432 seconds across all runs but guarantees the real path is exercised.\n\nThe star gate test will advance the session in 1-second chunks by reading `snapshot.left`, since the demo and countdown phases are auto-driven. This avoids a special skip path and keeps the trial on the real card flow.\n\nSession phases are being written carefully so the test pump advances cleanly. After a trial finishes, the test reads the percentage and stars from the result screen, then calls go('session') to start the next activity without pressing Done. Trial mode should not award minutes, saves, or unlocks, because that would dirty the playthrough state.\n\nStar gate tests will run in trial mode so they do not mutate the member's minutes, stars, or saves. The playthrough test will stay on a fresh story. I am now writing the large play.js and world.js files. Big numbers will use a font scale of 2.\n\nThe title will be drawn with E.font.title at scale 3 and centered near y=36 to avoid the corner area. The logo will sit in the attract corner. I am now writing the files.\n\nLines.js, content.js, audio.js, rigs.js, and ui.js will be written in one batch. The lines file will contain verbatim dialogue arrays totaling over 150 strings, with extra entries added for safety. Content.js will include nicknames and a G.LOOK skin palette with hair styles and skin tones.\n\nContent.js will hold the full designer database, including top and bottom colors, a 20-entry nicknames list, days guide, unlocks, activities, charts, courses, trays, and poses. A `G.allLines` helper is being added to flatten every string in the line bank for linting and counting.\n\nA recursive `G.allLines` helper will flatten every string in the line bank for linting and counting. The lint checks will enforce string lengths, reject empty entries, require at least 150 lines, verify clip names exist, ensure activity IDs are unique, confirm unlock IDs are known, and scan for banned words after writing. Audio.js songs will use only short note sequences like C4 D4 E4 F4 G4 A4 B4 with rests to keep the parser happy, and Sting songs will remain brief. Voice synthesis settings for Tare are being defined in a new `G.VOICE` object.\n\nBirch voice settings are being added to the voice bank. A `G.babble` function is being written to synthesize speech using Web Audio API oscillators, filters, and gain nodes. It selects a waveform based on the character's assigned voice, applies pitch and speed modifiers, and adjusts duration for vowels versus consonants.\n\nThe babble function now derives pitch from the character's assigned voice and the ASCII value of the spoken letter. It applies a bandpass filter tuned to 1.4× or 2.2× the pitch for vowels and consonants, with Q values of 0.7 and 2.5 respectively. Gain is modulated with exponential ramps to create natural speech amplitude. The oscillator connects to the audio destination after starting and stopping at the computed duration plus a small buffer.\n\nThe babble helper now accepts an optional OfflineAudioContext for test rendering. It falls back to the game audio bus when no context is provided. Dialog voice callbacks will route through G.babble. Audio initialization will define sound effects and defer music until the title screen. A note-sequence helper is being added for song generation. The rig loader will attempt to load mocap clips from window.MOCAP if available.\n\nThe rig loader now catches errors and falls back to null if mocap clips are missing. `G.makeRig` creates a Humanoid with size 2.1 and an optional seed, then drives it with loaded clips when available. It overrides `solvePose` to apply body animations and wobble offsets.\n\n`G.makeRig` now returns the rig or null on failure. `G.poseRig` samples a pose clip at a time offset driven by a sine wave, then assigns the result to `rig.mocap` and initializes `rig.wob`. The rig update is guarded so it only runs when `G.game.r` exists.\n\nRig wobble is being wired into the actor update loop. The actor will draw the rig via `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view)` and fall back to a coral capsule if the rig is missing. Ball joints are being added as specified. `setBody` will be copied exactly from the plan. `E.V3` is being verified against the engine.\n\nUI pills will be laid out in the update phase using game.W and game.H so headless tests can run without a renderer. They will sit at the bottom center, and the update loop will check pointer position and clicks to handle store boxes.\n\nFocus will repeat up and down, and confirm will pick only enabled options. Horizontal mode is being added for some screens. Select and confirm sound effects are defined before any scene starts, since boot steps run first. A headless audio initialization failure might cause sfx to return early before the unknown check.\n\nThe tick sound effect is being added for menu use. A dev panel will be wired into ui.js through G.devDraw and G.devUpdate, called from the boot hook so it works in every scene. The dev update reads arrow keys and runs before the scene update.\n\nThe dev panel will use an input hook so arrow keys register immediately. A `G.DEV_ROWS` slider list is being defined with key labels, names, min and max values, and step sizes. `adjust(key, delta)` clamps changes to bounds, and `reset()` restores defaults. `open()` enables the panel, resumes the game if paused, and hides the shell.\n\nA `G.dev.jump` helper is being added to the dev panel. It can advance to a specific day, launch an activity in trial mode, or enter any named scene directly. During tests, jumps use zero fade time so the next step advances immediately. The jump targets include title, about, member, arrival, bungalow, body, island, evening, ending, class, round, and every activity ID. The round scene will run offline for practice without AIHub.active by using a local clock.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-f88ea873-3b57-48e7-97c0-2c16c3b547f9-58","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"r\\.ctx","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/kits","head_limit":8}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1672,"output_tokens":6868,"cache_read_input_tokens":197760,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"f31822ec-6295-4361-9825-b2a49570174c"}
