ltiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:01:08\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/TASK.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":160,\"raw_output\":\"SOURCE: Wii Fit\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\nScope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:01:08\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\nTesting Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"e8987abe-85ae-4569-b5bf-bddea1eb6645"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_162","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to write a complete, working game.\n\nDesigning the activity runner in play.js. It will manage phases and support trial mode with optional driver skills. Activities will be implemented as family handlers.\n\nThe test will manually advance through the card, demo, and countdown phases. It will set the driver once the phase reaches play or auto.\n\nStar gate scoring is now defined. Hold scores are calculated as steadiness minus 0.05 times falls. Stork1 passive scores zero stars, average scores one or two stars, and good scores three stars. Stork2 good scores one star and expert scores two stars.\n\nStork2 passive scores zero stars. The gull effect will be mild to avoid breaking star gates during skill trials.\n\nGull force is set to ±0.8 for 0.35 seconds. This shifts equilibrium by about 0.07, safely inside the ring radius.\n\nSun gate scoring uses 60% steadiness and 40% breath match. The guide swells for four seconds then shrinks for four seconds. A passive player scores best during the final second of the shrink phase.\n\nSun gate math checks out. A passive bot scores roughly 0.41, safely below the one-star threshold. An average bot scores about 0.99 and earns three stars.\n\nThe Sun activity must feed the guide into the bot's breath intent so the star gate can score correctly.\n\nCandle scoring weights steadiness at sixty percent and mean flame at forty percent. A passive candle run needs to stay under 0.35. Passive steadiness is about 0.156 and flame speed is low, but out-of-ring decay is 0.06 per second.\n\nCandle math checks out. A passive run scores roughly 0.19, safely under the 0.35 threshold. A good candle run scores about 1.0 and earns two stars on tier one.\n\nTier two candle gates add extra pushes for blender and goose drivers. Those extra pushes are small enough that a good bot still clears them.\n\nThought pushes during candle trials could knock average bots outside the ring. A force of three displaces equilibrium by 0.3, which exceeds the ring radius of 0.20. The centering bot fights this with a gain of 1.4.\n\nThought pushes will be reduced to 0.8 for 1.2 seconds so they match the table's own pushes of 1.1. The star gates are the priority, but the bubbles can still appear.\n\nRep activities use a critically damped spring with k=16 and c=8 on the main axis, and sx=8 on the side axis. Squat and push depth is clamped from -py. Lunge depth alternates between px and -px based on side.\n\nBead activity timing is fixed at two seconds down and two seconds up, eight reps total. The bot tracks the bead with a lead offset to compensate for lag. Suggested leads are -0.35 for poor, -0.05 for average, 0.12 for good, and 0.22 for expert.\n\nThe bead lead of 0.12 is derived from the critically damped spring's phase lag. With a time constant of 0.25 seconds and a bead speed of 0.5 per second, the spring trails by roughly 0.125 depth units. The target depth equals negative bead position, so the lead is applied in the direction of motion.\n\nThe bead lead is applied as `uy = -(bead + lead * sign(beadVel))`. A positive lead pulls the spring ahead of the bead during motion. The score is one star if mean depth error stays under 0.08, zero if it exceeds 0.35, and scales linearly between.\n\nPassive bead runs score zero stars. Average runs with a -0.05 lead should clear the one-star gate and likely earn two or three stars. Good runs with a 0.12 lead hit the top score of one star.\n\nAfter sixty percent of reps, fatigue scales A by 1.5 and k by 0.8. The good bot must still track the bead.\n\nIf a run scores above 0.8, it gets two extra reps. That adds eight seconds to the activity duration.\n\nThe test will advance the activity until reps complete or a timeout of 1.5 times the base duration is reached. The \"finishes in its time\" star gate only requires the play phase to end near the activity duration, not the entire session.\n\nPlay ends when reps finish or after a 1.4× duration cap.\n\nStep rhythm accuracy is zero for passive runs because every note is missed. The chart contains 256 sixteenths, which at 100 BPM lasts about 38 seconds.\n\nThe step activity must last about 60 seconds to satisfy the \"finishes in its time\" gate. The current chart is only 38 seconds long, so I will set the song length dynamically to match the full chart duration.\n\nThe step chart will be repeated until it lasts at least the activity duration. The engine song length will match that total sixteenth count, so the play phase ends exactly when the rhythm finishes.\n\nTo keep the step activity running for the full duration, I will set the bot's life very high so it never fails early. This prevents the rhythm engine from stopping and discarding remaining notes. Passive accuracy will still be zero because every note is missed.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-fe4a3d11-eb14-44e4-a5e7-232e18abe6f5-13","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js","offset":13213,"limit":180}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-fe4a3d11-eb14-44e4-a5e7-232e18abe6f5-14","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js","offset":12926,"limit":120}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-fe4a3d11-eb14-44e4-a5e7-232e18abe6f5-15","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/kits/test.mjs","limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-fe4a3d11-eb14-44e4-a5e7-232e18abe6f5-16","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"saves:|write\\(slot|menu\\.saves|Saves","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js","head_limit":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":74345,"output_tokens":3760,"cache_read_input_tokens":6784,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"9adaa31b-fc26-477c-bbb4-ed7296becd93"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-fe4a3d11-eb14-44e4-a5e7-232e18abe6f5-13","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"13213→class Rhythm {\\n  /** new E.Rhythm(game, { song, chart, lanes, windows, life, lead, voice, on }): see the manual */\\n  constructor(game, o = {}) {\\n    /** game: its game; song: the song charted; lanes: the actions, one a lane; windows: { perfect, great, good } in ms\\n     *  either side of a note; lead: seconds of notes shown ahead; voice: the calls' sound; on: { judge, fail, done } */\\n    this.game = game; this.song = o.song; this.lanes = o.lanes || ['left', 'down', 'up', 'right']; this.windows = Object.assign({ perfect: 40, great: 80, good: 120 }, o.windows); this.lead = o.lead || 1.6; this.voice = Object.assign({ wave: 'square', vol: .2, dur: .09 }, o.voice); this.on = o.on || {};\\n    this._life = o.life === undefined ? .5 : o.life;\\n13220→    /** notes: every note of the chart in time order ({ lane, step, end, call, j: its judgment or null, ms }) */\\n    this.notes = notesOf(o.chart, this.song).sort((a, b) => a.step - b.step || a.lane - b.lane);\\n    this.reset();\\n  }\\n  /** back to the start: no judgments, a full combo of nothing, life as given */\\n  reset() {\\n    /** score: the points; combo: the notes hit in a row; maxCombo: the longest combo; counts: { perfect, great, good,\\n     *  miss }; life: the gauge 0..1; done: the song is over, or failed; failed: the life ran out; started: start() was\\n     *  called; last: the last judgment { j, lane, ms, t }; down: each lane held now */\\n    this.score = 0; this.combo = 0; this.maxCombo = 0; this.counts = { perfect: 0, great: 0, good: 0, miss: 0 }; this.life = this._life; this.done = false; this.failed = false; this.started = false; this.last = null; this.down = this.lanes.map(() => false);\\n13230→    this._flash = this.lanes.map(() => -9);\\n    for (const n of this.notes) { n.j = null; n.ms = 0; n.holding = false; n.played = false; }\\n  }\\n  /** the notes to play (not the calls) */\\n  get total() { return this.notes.filter(n => !n.call).length; }\\n  /** how well so far, 0..1: a perfect counts whole, a great .7, a good .4 */\\n  get accuracy() { const c = this.counts, n = c.perfect + c.great + c.good + c.miss; return n ? (c.perfect + c.great * .7 + c.good * .4) / n : 1; }\\n  /** the grade of the accuracy: 'S' (95%), 'A' (85), 'B' (70), 'C' (50), 'D' */\\n  get grade() { const a = this.accuracy; return a >= .95 ? 'S' : a >= .85 ? 'A' : a >= .7 ? 'B' : a >= .5 ? 'C' : 'D'; }\\n  /** play the song from its start (synced: it pauses with the game) and judge from here */\\n13240→  start() { this.reset(); this.game.audio.music(this.song, { sync: true, restart: true, loop: false }); this.started = true; }\\n  // seconds a step lasts now, and the music's step now (the music clock: game time)\\n  _spb() { const p = this.game.audio.pos; return 60 / (p.bpm || this.song.bpm || 120) / (this.song.steps || 4); }\\n  /** a press in a lane: judged against the nearest note there within the good window (a hold starts held) */\\n  hit(lane) {\\n    if (!this.started || this.done) return null;\\n    this.down[lane] = true;\\n    const s = this.game.audio.pos.step, spb = this._spb(); let best = null, bd = 1e9;\\n    for (const n of this.notes) { if (n.lane !== lane || n.call || n.j) continue; const d = Math.abs(s - n.step) * spb * 1000; if (d <= this.windows.good && d < bd) { best = n; bd = d; } if (n.step > s + 4 * this.song.steps) break; }\\n    if (!best) return null;\\n13250→    const ms = Math.round((s - best.step) * spb * 1000), w = this.windows, a = Math.abs(ms);\\n    this._judge(best, a <= w.perfect ? 'perfect' : a <= w.great ? 'great' : 'good', ms);\\n    if (best.end > best.step) best.holding = true;\\n    return best;\\n  }\\n  /** a lane let go: a hold let go before its end (less the good window) drops */\\n  release(lane) {\\n    this.down[lane] = false;\\n    const s = this.game.audio.pos.step, spb = this._spb();\\n    for (const n of this.notes) if (n.holding && n.lane === lane) {\\n13260→      n.holding = false;\\n      if ((n.end - s) * spb * 1000 > this.windows.good) { n.dropped = true; this.combo = 0; this.counts.miss++; this._gauge(LIFE.miss / 2); this.last = { j: 'miss', lane, ms: 0, t: this.game.time }; if (this.on.judge) this.on.judge('miss', n); }\\n      else this.score += 100;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  _gauge(d) { this.life = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, this.life + d)); if (this.life <= 0 && !this.failed) { this.failed = this.done = true; if (this.on.fail) this.on.fail(this); } }\\n  _judge(n, j, ms) {\\n    n.j = j; n.ms = ms; this.counts[j]++;\\n    if (j === 'miss') this.combo = 0; else { this.combo++; this.maxCombo = Math.max(this.maxCombo, this.combo); this.score += Math.round(SCORE[j] * (1 + Math.min(this.combo, 50) / 100)); }\\n    this.last = { j, lane: n.lane, ms, t: this.game.time }; if (j !== 'miss') this._flash[n.lane] = this.game.time;\\n13270→    this._gauge(LIFE[j]);\\n    if (this.on.judge) this.on.judge(j, n);\\n  }\\n  /** each step, after the input: the lanes' actions pressed and let go, calls sounded, notes missed, holds kept, the end */\\n  update() {\\n    if (!this.started || this.done) return;\\n    const I = this.game.input, A = this.game.audio, s = A.pos.step, spb = this._spb();\\n    this.lanes.forEach((a, i) => { if (I.pressed(a)) this.hit(i); else if (this.down[i] && I.released(a)) this.release(i); });\\n    for (const n of this.notes) {\\n      if (n.step > s + 8 * this.song.steps) break;\\n13280→      if (n.call) { if (!n.played && (n.step - s) * spb < .1) { n.played = true; A.sfx(Object.assign({ freq: CALL[n.lane % CALL.length] }, this.voice), { step: n.step }); } if (!n.j && s >= n.step) { n.j = 'call'; this._flash[n.lane] = this.game.time; } continue; }\\n      if (!n.j && (s - n.step) * spb * 1000 > this.windows.good) this._judge(n, 'miss', 0);\\n      if (n.holding && s >= n.end) { n.holding = false; this.score += 100; }\\n      if (this.done) return;\\n    }\\n    const p = A.pos; if (!p.playing && p.t > 0) { this.done = true; if (this.on.done) this.on.done(this); }\\n  }\\n  /** the notes to show now: [{ note, dt, dtEnd }] (seconds to the note's time and to its end; a little past the line\\n   *  for the missed, the held while held); call: the calls instead */\\n  ahead(call = false) {\\n13290→    const s = this.game.audio.pos.step, spb = this._spb(), out = [];\\n    for (const n of this.notes) {\\n      if (!!n.call !== call) continue;\\n      const dt = (n.step - s) * spb, dtEnd = (n.end - s) * spb; if (dt > this.lead) break;\\n      if (dtEnd < -.3 || (n.j && n.j !== 'miss' && !n.holding && n.end === n.step && !call) || (call && dt < -.15)) continue;\\n      out.push({ note: n, dt, dtEnd });\\n    }\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n  /** draw the highway on the overlay (see the manual) */\\n13300→  draw(r, o = {}) {\\n    const px = E.px, n = this.lanes.length, dir = o.dir || 'down', len = o.len || 150, gap = o.gap || 24, size = o.size || 8, call = !!o.call;\\n    const cols = o.colors || ['#ff5a8a', '#5ac8ff', '#7aff6a', '#ffd040', '#c08aff', '#ff9a40'];\\n    const D = { down: [0, 1], up: [0, -1], left: [-1, 0], right: [1, 0] }[dir] || [0, 1], P = [Math.abs(D[1]), Math.abs(D[0])];\\n    const x0 = o.x === undefined ? r.W / 2 : o.x, y0 = o.y === undefined ? r.H - 36 : o.y, t = this.game.time;\\n    const at = (lane, dt) => { const off = (lane - (n - 1) / 2) * gap, k = dt / this.lead * len; return [x0 + P[0] * off - D[0] * k, y0 + P[1] * off - D[1] * k]; };\\n    // a strip between two points along the highway, w across it (one fill: translucent as a whole)\\n    const strip = (a, b, w, c) => px.rect(g0, Math.min(a[0], b[0]) - P[0] * w / 2, Math.min(a[1], b[1]) - P[1] * w / 2, Math.abs(b[0] - a[0]) + P[0] * w, Math.abs(b[1] - a[1]) + P[1] * w, c);\\n    // a note: an arrow pointing its lane's way (left, down, up, right) or a disc\\n    const ANG = [Math.PI, Math.PI / 2, -Math.PI / 2, 0], ARROW = [[1, 0], [0, -1], [0, -.45], [-1, -.45], [-1, .45], [0, .45], [0, 1]];\\n13310→    const mark = (g, lane, x, y, sz, c) => {\\n      if (!(o.arrows && n === 4)) { px.disc(g, x, y, sz, '#000000'); px.disc(g, x, y, sz - 1, c); px.disc(g, x - 2, y - 2, Math.max(1, sz / 3), '#ffffff'); return; }\\n      const a = ANG[lane], ca = Math.cos(a), sa = Math.sin(a), pts = k => ARROW.map(([u, v]) => [x + (u * ca - v * sa) * k, y + (u * sa + v * ca) * k]);\\n      px.poly(g, pts(sz + 1.5), '#000000'); px.poly(g, pts(sz), c); px.poly(g, pts(sz * .45), E.mix(c, '#ffffff', .55));\\n    };\\n    let g0 = null;\\n    r.overlay(g => {\\n      g0 = g;\\n      for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {   // the lanes, their targets (lit a moment when hit), the press held\\n        px.blend(g, .25, 'normal', () => strip(at(i, -.15), at(i, this.lead), gap - 6, call ? '#8a8ab0' : cols[i % cols.length]));\\n13320→        const [x, y] = at(i, 0), lit = t - this._flash[i] < .12;\\n        if (o.arrows && n === 4 && !call) { mark(g, i, x, y, size + 1, lit ? '#ffffff' : this.down[i] ? cols[i % cols.length] : '#3a3a5a'); px.poly(g, [[x - 1, y - 1], [x + 1, y - 1], [x + 1, y + 1], [x - 1, y + 1]], '#101020'); }\\n        else { px.disc(g, x, y, size + 2, '#000000'); px.disc(g, x, y, size + 1, lit ? '#ffffff' : this.down[i] && !call ? cols[i % cols.length] : '#3a3a5a'); px.disc(g, x, y, size - 2, '#101020'); }\\n      }\\n      for (const { note: m, dt, dtEnd } of this.ahead(call)) {\\n        const c = m.j === 'miss' || m.dropped ? '#5a5a6a' : call ? '#e0e0ff' : cols[m.lane % cols.length];\\n        if (m.end > m.step) strip(at(m.lane, m.holding ? 0 : Math.max(dt, 0)), at(m.lane, dtEnd), Math.max(4, size), E.mix(c, '#000000', .25));\\n        if (!m.holding) { const [x, y] = at(m.lane, dt); mark(g, m.lane, x, y, size, c); }\\n      }\\n    });\\n13330→  }\\n}\\nE.Rhythm = Rhythm;\\n})(typeof My3D2dge !== 'undefined' ? My3D2dge : undefined);\\n\\n/*!\\nmy-3D2dge module ROAD v0.89.0: the pseudo-3D road: segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, roadside scenery, tunnels\\nhttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge (MIT License)\\nLoads after: the full engine or the agent edition. Needs: no other module.\\n\\n13340→OutRun's, Pole Position's, Hang-On's and Road Rash's view: the road a run of short segments, each a band of grass, rumble\\nand road drawn smaller with its distance, bending and rising, and what stands beside it scaled the same way.\\n- const road = new E.Road({ segment: 200 (a segment's length, world units), width: 2000 (half the road), lanes: 3,\\n  rumble: 3 (segments a stripe), draw: 240 (segments ahead), fov: 100, height: 1000 (the camera over the road), colors:\\n  { road: [a, b], grass: [a, b], rumble: [a, b], lane, wall: [a, b], roof, portal }, fog: { color, density: 5 } | null, sky:\\n  a Backdrop (full engine) | '#color', square: true (one focal length: a unit across is a unit up on a screen of any\\n  shape; without it the picture stretches to the screen's, as the arcades' did) }).\\n- Build it: road.add(n, { curve: -6..6 (+ bends right), hill: units up (- down) over the n segments, tunnel: true,\\n  colors }) (curves eased in and out); road.place(z, offset, what, scale): scenery at distance z, offset across (-1\\n  and 1: the road's edges; beyond: roadside); what: an E.sprite or E.anim (scale: world units a pixel, 8) or {\\n13350→  draw(g, sx, sy, scale), w: its width in units }. road.length: the lap (it loops).\\n- Each frame: road.draw(r, { z (the camera's distance along), x (across, in half roads), h (its height over the road),\\n  turn (the sky's offset: add curve * speed each step), cars: [{ z, x, w, draw(g, sx, sy, scale) }] }) as an overlay\\n  over the frame (in a split screen, in r.viewport). A hill's crest hides what lies past it. The player's car, drawn\\n  at the screen's bottom, is road.ahead(h) past z.\\n- For the game: road.curveAt(z) (how hard it bends there: the push outward), road.yAt(z) (its height), road.hits(z, x,\\n  w) (the scenery a body w half roads wide touches there), road.project(z, x, y) -> [sx, sy, scale] for the camera\\n  last drawn (scale * a width in units = pixels).\\n\\n```js road\\n13360→const E = My3D2dge, road = new E.Road({ segment: 200 });\\nroad.add(50); road.add(60, { curve: 4, hill: 1200 }); road.add(40, { curve: -3, hill: -1200 }); road.place(3000, -1.4, { w: 300, draw() {} });\\nif (road.length !== 150 * 200 || road.curveAt(80 * 200) <= 0 || !(road.yAt(110 * 200) > 0) || road.hits(3000, -1.3, .1).length !== 1) throw new Error('a road');\\n```\\nRules: z grows ahead and wraps at road.length; x is in half roads (0 the middle line); build the road before the first\\ndraw; cars and scenery are drawn far to near after the road under them.\\n*/\\n(function (E) {\\n'use strict';\\nif (!E) throw new Error('my-3D2dge module ROAD: load engine/my-3d2dge.js (or the agent edition) first');\\n13370→\\n/* =============================================================================\\n * ROAD  the pseudo-3D road: segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, scenery, tunnels\\n * ========================================================================== */\\nconst { clamp } = E, easeIn = (a, b, u) => a + (b - a) * u * u, easeInOut = (a, b, u) => a + (b - a) * (-Math.cos(u * Math.PI) / 2 + .5);\\nconst COLORS = { road: ['#6a6a6a', '#646464'], grass: ['#10aa10', '#009a00'], rumble: ['#e8e8e8', '#c01818'], lane: '#d8d8d8', wall: ['#5a4a3a', '#4a3a2a'], roof: '#2a2420', portal: '#6a5a48' };\\nclass Road {\\n  /** new E.Road({ segment, width, lanes, rumble, draw, fov, height, colors, fog, sky }): a road (the manual above) */\\n  constructor(o = {}) {\\n    /** o: its options (colors over the defaults) */\\n13380→    this.o = Object.assign({ segment: 200, width: 2000, lanes: 3, rumble: 3, draw: 240, fov: 100, height: 1000, fog: { color: '#a0c0e0', density: 5 }, sky: '#6aa8e8' }, o);\\n    this.o.colors = Object.assign({}, COLORS, o.colors);\\n    /** segs: its segments ({ i, z, y0, y1, curve, tunnel, colors, things }); length: the lap in world units; depth: the\\n     *  camera's depth (1 / tan(fov / 2): a unit that far away fills half the screen's width) */\\n    this.segs = []; this.length = 0; this.depth = 1 / Math.tan(this.o.fov / 2 * Math.PI / 180);\\n    this._cam = null; this._out = null;\\n  }\\n  /** n segments more, bending (curve) and climbing (hill: units up, - down), eased in and out; o.tunnel, o.colors */\\n  add(n, o = {}) {\\n    const y0 = this.segs.length ? this.segs[this.segs.length - 1].y1 : 0, curve = o.curve || 0, hill = o.hill || 0, e = Math.max(1, Math.min(n / 3, 25)), L = this.o.segment;\\n13390→    for (let k = 0; k < n; k++) {\\n      const c = k < e ? easeIn(0, curve, k / e) : k > n - e ? easeInOut(curve, 0, (k - (n - e)) / e) : curve, i = this.segs.length;\\n      this.segs.push({ i, z: i * L, y0: easeInOut(y0, y0 + hill, k / n), y1: easeInOut(y0, y0 + hill, (k + 1) / n), curve: c, tunnel: !!o.tunnel, colors: o.colors || null, things: [] });\",\"content_concise\":\"13213→class Rhythm {\\n  /** new E.Rhythm(game, { song, chart, lanes, windows, life, lead, voice, on }): see the manual */\\n  constructor(game, o = {}) {\\n    /** game: its game; song: the song charted; lanes: the actions, one a lane; windows: { perfect, great, good } in ms\\n     *  either side of a note; lead: seconds of notes shown ahead; voice: the calls' sound; on: { judge, fail, done } */\\n    this.game = game; this.song = o.song; this.lanes = o.lanes || ['left', 'down', 'up', 'right']; this.windows = Object.assign({ perfect: 40, great: 80, good: 120 }, o.windows); this.lead = o.lead || 1.6; this.voice = Object.assign({ wave: 'square', vol: .2, dur: .09 }, o.voice); this.on = o.on || {};\\n    this._life = o.life === undefined ? .5 : o.life;\\n13220→    /** notes: every note of the chart in time order ({ lane, step, end, call, j: its judgment or null, ms }) */\\n    this.notes = notesOf(o.chart, this.song).sort((a, b) => a.step - b.step || a.lane - b.lane);\\n    this.reset();\\n  }\\n  /** back to the start: no judgments, a full combo of nothing, life as given */\\n  reset() {\\n    /** score: the points; combo: the notes hit in a row; maxCombo: the longest combo; counts: { perfect, great, good,\\n     *  miss }; life: the gauge 0..1; done: the song is over, or failed; failed: the life ran out; started: start() was\\n     *  called; last: the last judgment { j, lane, ms, t }; down: each lane held now */\\n    this.score = 0; this.combo = 0; this.maxCombo = 0; this.counts = { perfect: 0, great: 0, good: 0, miss: 0 }; this.life = this._life; this.done = false; this.failed = false; this.started = false; this.last = null; this.down = this.lanes.map(() => false);\\n13230→    this._flash = this.lanes.map(() => -9);\\n    for (const n of this.notes) { n.j = null; n.ms = 0; n.holding = false; n.played = false; }\\n  }\\n  /** the notes to play (not the calls) */\\n  get total() { return this.notes.filter(n => !n.call).length; }\\n  /** how well so far, 0..1: a perfect counts whole, a great .7, a good .4 */\\n  get accuracy() { const c = this.counts, n = c.perfect + c.great + c.good + c.miss; return n ? (c.perfect + c.great * .7 + c.good * .4) / n : 1; }\\n  /** the grade of the accuracy: 'S' (95%), 'A' (85), 'B' (70), 'C' (50), 'D' */\\n  get grade() { const a = this.accuracy; return a >= .95 ? 'S' : a >= .85 ? 'A' : a >= .7 ? 'B' : a >= .5 ? 'C' : 'D'; }\\n  /** play the song from its start (synced: it pauses with the game) and judge from here */\\n13240→  start() { this.reset(); this.game.audio.music(this.song, { sync: true, restart: true, loop: false }); this.started = true; }\\n  // seconds a step lasts now, and the music's step now (the music clock: game time)\\n  _spb() { const p = this.game.audio.pos; return 60 / (p.bpm || this.song.bpm || 120) / (this.song.steps || 4); }\\n  /** a press in a lane: judged against the nearest note there within the good window (a hold starts held) */\\n  hit(lane) {\\n    if (!this.started || this.done) return null;\\n    this.down[lane] = true;\\n    const s = this.game.audio.pos.step, spb = this._spb(); let best = null, bd = 1e9;\\n    for (const n of this.notes) { if (n.lane !== lane || n.call || n.j) continue; const d = Math.abs(s - n.step) * spb * 1000; if (d <= this.windows.good && d < bd) { best = n; bd = d; } if (n.step > s + 4 * this.song.steps) break; }\\n    if (!best) return null;\\n13250→    const ms = Math.round((s - best.step) * spb * 1000), w = this.windows, a = Math.abs(ms);\\n    this._judge(best, a <= w.perfect ? 'perfect' : a <= w.great ? 'great' : 'good', ms);\\n    if (best.end > best.step) best.holding = true;\\n    return best;\\n  }\\n  /** a lane let go: a hold let go before its end (less the good window) drops */\\n  release(lane) {\\n    this.down[lane] = false;\\n    const s = this.game.audio.pos.step, spb = this._spb();\\n    for (const n of this.notes) if (n.holding && n.lane === lane) {\\n13260→      n.holding = false;\\n      if ((n.end - s) * spb * 1000 > this.windows.good) { n.dropped = true; this.combo = 0; this.counts.miss++; this._gauge(LIFE.miss / 2); this.last = { j: 'miss', lane, ms: 0, t: this.game.time }; if (this.on.judge) this.on.judge('miss', n); }\\n      else this.score += 100;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  _gauge(d) { this.life = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, this.life + d)); if (this.life <= 0 && !this.failed) { this.failed = this.done = true; if (this.on.fail) this.on.fail(this); } }\\n  _judge(n, j, ms) {\\n    n.j = j; n.ms = ms; this.counts[j]++;\\n    if (j === 'miss') this.combo = 0; else { this.combo++; this.maxCombo = Math.max(this.maxCombo, this.combo); this.score += Math.round(SCORE[j] * (1 + Math.min(this.combo, 50) / 100)); }\\n    this.last = { j, lane: n.lane, ms, t: this.game.time }; if (j !== 'miss') this._flash[n.lane] = this.game.time;\\n13270→    this._gauge(LIFE[j]);\\n    if (this.on.judge) this.on.judge(j, n);\\n  