{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_462","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The visible canvas shows zero ink and zero white pixels on the title, despite the internal buffer having significant variation.\n\nThe canvas is 1280x720. The game draws to an offscreen buffer and blits to the canvas. The beach colors in screenshots confirm the canvas is painted. Zero ink and zero white pixels suggest the canvas is transparent or a solid color that fails the g>40 ink check.\n\nThe beach colors are present on the canvas, but the title text is missing. The title PNG at 1920 showed ink pixels, confirming the text was rendered internally. The zero-ink reading at 1280 is therefore puzzling and needs explanation.\n\nThe offscreen buffer is not the issue. The text was drawn to it and blitted to the visible canvas. The canvas context might be WebGL rather than 2D. That would explain why getImageData returns no ink.\n\nThe 1920 title PNG showed ink because the boot overlay text is DOM-based, not canvas-rendered. This explains why the canvas itself contains no ink pixels.\n\nThe boot overlay text is dark, so it cannot explain the 38025 ink pixels. The center pixel in the title PNG matches the ink condition, but that color could be either darkened text or a specific beach pixel. The sample density of one pixel every three pixels may also be distorting the count.\n\nThe darkened sea band likely accounts for the ink pixels, not text. The title \"ink\" was probably just the darkened ocean during boot fade. No text appears on any screenshot. The diagnostic confirms the canvas contains no ink or white pixels even after boot completes.\n\nThe overlay canvas might not be the #screen element. It could be absent from the DOM, have zero size, or sit behind other layers. The engine's overlay implementation needs inspection.\n\nThe overlay might be drawn mid-frame, but the diagnostic waited two frames and 800 milliseconds, so the frame should already be complete. The real issue may be that `getContext('2d')` on the canvas does not return the engine's actual drawing surface. If the engine uses WebGL or a bitmap renderer instead of 2D canvas, the context call would fail or return a different surface entirely.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-71aa86d7-6078-4283-a409-c4cf0fec1182-103","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"overlay\\(fn|_overlay|this\\.buf","glob":"**/engine/my-3d2dge.js","head_limit":25}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":576,"output_tokens":1278,"cache_read_input_tokens":183680,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"773a00b4-4d57-4647-a2ea-c5e477b60356"}
