ow the last used device; glyphs default to PlayStation and a setting switches to Xbox; hover moves focus and click activates in both the game pills and the HTML menus. Touch-only devices get no touch controls, the text “Controller or keyboard required”, and a Settings button, and attract still runs. After 10 seconds without input on the title or top-level menus, a looping AI/replay demo plays the activities and does not write saves or unlocks; any input returns to the title and swallows that press; it does not start at 9.9 seconds. Dev mode opens from the pause menu in every scene, keeps the game running, exposes the important Lean, scoring, cardio, game, and time variables as live sliders with a reset to defaults, and can jump scene, activity, tier, and day. All music, effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are Animalese-style gibberish; music defaults to 10% volume and is not made louder to compensate. Art is the engine’s bright pixel-puppet look plus new source-specific pieces (scale, plate, mats, and the activity props). Text stays large, one idea per screen, no dense data grids.\\n10→4. On its own, local Class joins 2–4 players on one screen (pads or split keyboard) for Stork Stand, Quiet Candle, Squat Rail, Step Class, and Hoop Hips, scores them, and shows a podium; empty mats are non-scoring bots. In an AI Hub session the game skips title, attract, dev, quit, restart, and saves, connects ghost mode for 1–4 seats, shows the ready card, and plays Morning Class: three shared events dealt from the plan’s pools, timed from hub time, with translucent ghosts, progress, and a single result. The unmodified hub session script is the first script in the built page, and the hub meta tags identify a Game, source Wii Fit, 1–4 players, ghost session, about 3 minutes. The five harness cases pass (1, 2, 3 and 4 with bad network, 4 with host-leave). Outside a session, connect does not take over, and the page reaches its own title.\\n5. `node tests