{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_550","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A Node process has been running for 9:46. Its only child is a Python server.\n\nChrome is not a direct child of the Node process. The browser may be orphaned or reparented. It might also be controlled by an unseen Playwright driver node.\n\nThe test may be stuck waiting for a dead Playwright process. The browser could be a grandchild through an intermediate process that already exited. A hung waitForFunction promise is also possible if the page froze without rejecting. The 90-second timeout should have fired after nine minutes, so the clock may have paused.\n\nThe test is inside the cases loop, not stuck in buildHtml. Node is alive and RSS is low, so a blocked event loop is unlikely. The total runtime suggests the cases may be running sequentially and each taking the full 90-second timeout.\n\nThe 535-second runtime is 85 seconds past the maximum if all five cases hit the 90-second timeout. The last case may be stuck on a standalone page with a 15-second timeout, or its actual timeout is longer than assumed. The hub start time estimate might also be off.\n\nThe hub has been running for 535 seconds, exceeding the expected 450-second ceiling by about 85 seconds. The standalone wait is only 15 seconds, so something is hanging beyond the timeouts.\n\nPossible causes include page.goto hanging without its default 30-second navigation timeout, page.evaluate blocking, browser.close() stalling in a finally block, or the first navigation waiting indefinitely for network. Another possibility is that Playwright failed to launch Chrome and is retrying.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-f8751413-cb3e-438a-904f-099d09b71911-124","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"echo \"wchan $(cat /proc/142768/wchan 2>/dev/null) state $(awk '/^State/ {print}' /proc/142768/status)\"\necho '--- fds ---'\nls -l /proc/142768/fd 2>/d