his._players.length > 1 && this.pausedBy ? 'P' + this.pausedBy + ' PAUSED' : 'PAUSED', r.W / 2, r.H / 2 - 8, '#ffffff', { align: 'center', scale: 2 });   // (several players: who)\\n      E.font.text(g, 'PRESS ESC OR START', r.W / 2, r.H / 2 + 8, '#c9c0e0', { align: 'center' });\\n    });\\n    const fx = this._flash;\\n    if (fx) { const a = (1 - fx.t / fx.dur) * (fx.k === undefined ? 1 : fx.k); if (a <= 0) this._flash = null; else this.r.overlay(g => { const r = this.r; g.save(); if (E.style.trans === 'dither') { const pat = r._fadePattern(a * .85), c = mkCanvas(4, 4), cg = c.getContext('2d'); cg.drawImage(pat, 0, 0); cg.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-in'; cg.fillStyle = fx.color; cg.fillRect(0, 0, 4, 4); g.fillStyle = g.createPattern(c, 'repeat'); } else { g.fillStyle = fx.color; g.globalAlpha = qa(a * .75); g.globalCompositeOperation = 'lighter'; } g.fillRect(0, 0, r.bw, r.bh); g.restore(); g._c = null; }); }\\n\",\"total_lines\":14974}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"baadf321-6044-49d2-b58e-af0119ce6039"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_606","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll boot with drawing enabled, inject a ghost message, advance the frame, and inspect the stand-in canvas for non-empty pixels.\n\nThe canvas inspection won't prove alpha 0.5 specifically. I'll wrap game.r.actor to spy on the real draw path instead.\n\nThe wrapped actor call will record the alpha value from each draw. After advancing one frame with drawing enabled, I'll assert that a call had alpha 0.5. I'll also verify the pose used l/w by storing the ghost rig in a map keyed by sender and checking its wobble after drawing.\n\nI'll attach the ghost rig to the ghost object itself so the test can read its wobble after drawing. I need both the actor alpha and the rig wobble to prove the draw happened at alpha 0.5. I also need to assert that ghosts with a mismatched angle are not drawn, while a matching angle is drawn.\n\nI'll extend the fake hub test to boot with draw:true. The existing hub assertions should remain valid since drawing each frame won't throw errors.\n\nI'll add a still-player versus good-bot assertion to the fake hub test. After the session starts playing, I'll record the human player's progress percentage and compare it against a good-bot run of the same activity. The goal is to prove that a still player does not post the good-bot percentage.\n\nThe good-bot percentage must come from the shipped session itself, not a hardcoded constant. I'll construct a second Session with driver 'good' and the same activity ID to serve as the oracle. Stepping it alongside the live session risks sharing G.driver state.\n\nSetting G.driver to null for the live session is safe. The reference session's seat.skill will then be used as the oracle skill, and the good bot does not read G.lean. Both sessions must use the same seed and identical time steps to ensure a fair comparison.\n\nA still player and a good bot will likely score differently on stork, candle, squat, and push. The first activity is deterministic because the deal RNG is fixed.\n\nThe comparison will use a 0.02 percentage-point threshold, or require different pixel movement if the scores are close. The held-lean test will record the initial balance pixel, press a key to hold a lean, advance the frame, and assert that the pixel position changed.\n\nKeyD corresponds to the right lean in the hub input map. Holding it should increase the balance pixel over time.\n\nThe held-lean test must not set G.driver to 'good' during the round, because Session.update copies that global driver onto the local seat. The bot tool can set G.driver directly instead; the running session will pick it up automatically.\n\nThe Bot: on action will set both this.bot and G.driver to 'good'. The Bot: off action will clear both this.bot and G.driver. This prevents the running session from picking up a stale driver when the dev panel is toggled off.\n\nSession.update only sets the local seat skill when G.driver is a bot, but never clears it when G.driver becomes null. This means Bot: off must explicitly null out the seat skill as well. The developer panel logic is getting messy, so I'll move the driver and skill clearing into toolAct instead.\n\nI'll consolidate the bot tool logic into an `applyDevBot` helper. The Bot: on action will set both `this.bot` and `G.driver` to `'good'`, then call `applyBot()`. The Bot: off action will clear both fields and invoke `clearBot()`. This helper will also be wired into `Session.update` so the running session automatically picks up the developer panel's active bot.\n\nSession.update will now set the local seat skill from G.dev.bot and clear it when the dev bot is disabled. The test sequence will open the panel, start an activity, cycle to Bot: good with the more tool, press confirm via keyboard, and advance.\n\nThe test will assert that the active session seat skill is 'good' and that G.driver is 'good'. The balance's bot field should also be armed on the next step. This proves the good bot is driving the balance without player input.\n\nThe skeptic wants to see something actually reading `G.dev.bot`, not just the tool writing it. I'll modify `skillName` so it checks `G.dev.bot` when the developer panel is active and the seat is local, ensuring the bot driver is wired into the session's skill resolution.\n\nThe developer panel will only set `this.bot`, leaving `G.driver` untouched. `skillName` will read `G.dev.bot` when the panel is active and the seat is local, falling back to `seat.skill` or `G.driver` otherwise. This fixes the bug where the tool wrote to both fields but nothing consumed the developer bot state. The Bot: off action will also clear `G.driver` if it holds a valid bot skill.\n\nThe developer panel will only write `this.bot` and never touch `G.driver`. `skillName` will read `G.dev.bot` directly when the panel is active, falling through to existing logic when it is not. This keeps the Bot: off action minimal. Star-gate tests remain unaffected because the panel stays closed.\n\nThe developer panel logic is sound. A local seat will use the panel's bot skill even when G.driver is null, and both local seats in a class will become bots when the panel is active with Bot: good.\n\nI am adding jump tools to the dev panel. The boot cycle must match the new TOOLS length. Each jump tool needs a scene, activity, tier, and day.\n\nA single \"Jump\" tool will enter a submode where up and down select between Day, Scene, Activity, and Tier. Left and right change the current value. Confirming jumps to that destination. This satisfies the skeptic by letting the panel set scene, activity, tier, and day directly.\n\nThe developer panel currently ignores keyboard confirm presses. I will change `update()` to call `toolAct` on every confirm press, except in pose mode and jump mode where the action is handled separately. For pad testing, I will set `lastDevice` to `'gamepad'` and press Pad0.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-07d49a9a-4d14-4b4f-b412-dd7125079917-39","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"lastDevice","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js","head_limit":25}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-07d49a9a-4d14-4b4f-b412-dd7125079917-40","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"setDriver|G\\.driver","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/game","glob":"*.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2492,"output_tokens":3572,"cache_read_input_tokens":103168,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"dbe6220b-abf5-40d2-979c-00e39984231a"}
