;font:600 11px system-ui,sans-serif;touch-action:none';\\n      st.appendChild(b); document.body.appendChild(st); this.bindButtons(st);\\n    }\\n    return box;\\n  }\\n  /** press a key or button code ('KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:attack') as if the person did: scripted input,\\n   *  tests, bots */\\n  press(code) {\\n    if (this.held.has(code) || (code === 'Pad9' && this.held.has('Pad8'))) return;   // (Create + Options is the host's: the AI Hub opens its bar on it, so Options while Create is held is no press)\\n    this.held.add(code); this._freshCodes.add(code);\\n    if (code.startsWith('Pad')) { this.lastDevice = 'gamepad'; this.aimSource = 'pad'; }\\n    else if (code.startsWith('Act:')) this.lastDevice = 'touch';\\n    else this.lastDevice = code.startsWith('Mouse') ? 'mouse' : 'keyboard';\\n    for (const a of this._codes[code] || []) { this._pt[a] = this.clock; this._used[a] = false; this._rk[a] = -1; this._fresh.add(a); }\\n  }\\n  /** let go of a code pressed with press() */\\n  release(code) {\\n    if (!this.held.delete(code)) return;\\n    for (const a of this._codes[code] || []) if (!this.down(a)) this._freshUp.add(a);\\n  }\\n  /** press an action for one update step, as a quick tap of its key (scripted input, tests, an AI): pressed() and down()\\n   *  are true in the next step, released() in the one after */\\n  tap(a) { if (!this.map[a]) { this._check(a); return; } const d = this.lastDevice; this._tapCode('Act:' + a); this.lastDevice = d; }   // (a script's tap is no device: prompts keep the player's)\\n  _tapCode(c) { this.press(c); this._taps.add(c); }\\n  /** hold an action down (on) or let it go (false), as its key would */\\n  hold(a, on = true) {\\n    if (!this.map[a]) { this._check(a); return; }\\n    const c = 'Act:' + a, d = this.lastDevice; if (on) { this._holds.add(c); this.press(c); } else { this._holds.delete(c); this.release(c); }\\n    this.lastDevice = d;   // (no device either; and a tap of a held action leaves it held)\\n\",\"total_lines\":14974}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"bebe0f4e-47de-4a08-b49c-26601e598509"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_610","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The pressed() function returns false when the corresponding entry in _used is already true.\n\nconsume() marks keys as used. The dev panel will consume confirm and directional presses before the scene update so menus don't move. wireScenes.update will run dev.input first, then check talk state.\n\nThe dev.update call will be removed from the scene update loop to prevent double execution. The panel will consume confirm before returning so the scene never sees it. Running the panel before the scene preserves existing test behavior.\n\nThe \"more\" key will cycle tools in the step hook before the scene update. A new Jump tool is being designed with fields for row, day, scene, act, and tier, plus lookup arrays for scenes and acts. It will be placed at the end of the tool list so Pose remains index 11.\n\nThe tool indices are now fixed: Pose viewer stays at 11, and Jump is appended at 13. boot.js will use `% G.dev.toolCount()` instead of a hardcoded 13 for the tool cycle, and `toolAct(13)` will toggle jumpMode.\n\nJump mode will use four rows: day, scene, activity, and tier. Up and down switch between rows; left and right change the current value. Confirm triggers the jump and exits mode; cancel exits without jumping. Default values will be day 3 and scene bungalow so a confirm without edits lands somewhere visible. The test will edit a value via input then confirm to prove the panel drives the jump. Existing slider and pose tests remain unchanged.\n\nThe test will cycle through the tools with `more` to reach Jump, avoiding any direct `toolAct()` calls for new assertions. In jump mode, pressing right increases the day value, and down moves between scene, activity, and tier rows. Confirming will trigger the jump and assert that the resulting scene, activity, tier, and day match the edited values.\n\nA pad-based test path will be added alongside the keyboard one. After jumping, cycling `more` from index 13 reaches Bot: good at index 6. Pressing Pad0 confirms this tool. The test advances a few frames and asserts that the balance skill is armed and the seat UX is nonzero. If the jump lands on stork, beginFamily triggers after the 0.4-second count.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-586bb0e2-8692-419f-b0a0-8bff17c31285-45","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js","offset":12790,"limit":50}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3110,"output_tokens":1438,"cache_read_input_tokens":113280,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"5fbf05ae-4dd0-4640-9274-ebb519fc17c0"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-586bb0e2-8692-419f-b0a0-8bff17c31285-45","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"12790→  world.remove(b); world.wake(); world.bodies (in the order added); world.contacts (the last substep's: { a, b, nx, ny,\\n  pts }); world.hash() (a proof's number); world.draw(g, to,\\n  color) outlines every body (to(x, y) -> [sx, sy]: your plane on the screen).\\n\\n```js physics\\nconst E = My3D2dge, world = new E.Physics({ gravity: [0, 600] });\\nworld.chain([[0, 0], [0, 200], [160, 200], [160, 0]]);                    // a box open at the top\\nconst L = world.flipper(40, 170, { side: 'left' }), ball = world.circle(64, 150, 5, { bullet: true, bounce: .4 });\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 300 && !world.contacts.some(c => c.a === L || c.b === L); i++) world.step(1 / 120);   // it lands on it\\nL.flip(true); for (let i = 0; i < 12; i++) world.step(1 / 120);\\n12800→if (!(ball.vy < -100)) throw new Error('the flipper fires it up');\\nconst shot = world.circle(80, 100, 4, { bullet: true, vx: 9000 }); for (let i = 0; i < 30; i++) world.step(1 / 120);\\nif (shot.x > 160) throw new Error('never through the wall');\\n```\\nRules: step it from the game's fixed step only (the same steps give the same world); a fast ball is a bullet; keep\\npolygons convex (a concave shape is several bodies or a chain); give a table's walls a little thickness (r: 1) where\\nballs press hard; draw your own art at the bodies' places (draw() is for checking).\\n*/\\n(function (E) {\\n'use strict';\\n12810→if (!E) throw new Error('my-3D2dge module PHYSICS: load engine/my-3d2dge.js (or the agent edition) first');\\n\\n/* =============================================================================\\n * PHYSICS  2D rigid bodies: a core (a point, a segment or a convex polygon) with a radius; sequential impulses in a\\n * fixed order; swept circles for fast balls; sensors, layers, sleep, kinematic bodies, a pinball's parts\\n * ========================================================================== */\\nconst hyp = Math.hypot, clamp = (v, a, b) => v < a ? a : v > b ? b : v, MARGIN = 1, SLOP = .3;\\n// the points wound one way (a positive area), so each edge's outward normal is (dy, -dx)\\nconst wind = P => { let a = 0; for (let i = 0; i < P.length; i++) { const p = P[i], q = P[(i + 1) % P.length]; a += p[0] * q[1] - q[0] * p[1]; } return (a < 0 ? P.slice().reverse() : P.slice()).map(p => [p[0], p[1]]); };\\n// a polygon's area, centroid and second moment of area about its centroid\\n12820→function polyMass(P) {\\n  let A = 0, cx = 0, cy = 0, I = 0;\\n  for (let i = 0; i < P.length; i++) { const [x1, y1] = P[i], [x2, y2] = P[(i + 1) % P.length], c = x1 * y2 - x2 * y1; A += c / 2; cx += (x1 + x2) * c / 6; cy += (y1 + y2) * c / 6; I += c / 12 * (x1 * x1 + x1 * x2 + x2 * x2 + y1 * y1 + y1 * y2 + y2 * y2); }\\n  if (Math.abs(A) < 1e-9) return null;\\n  cx /= A; cy /= A; return { A, cx, cy, I: I - A * (cx * cx + cy * cy) };\\n}\\n\\nclass PBody {\\n  /** made by world.add (and its shorthands): see the manual */\\n  constructor(world, d) {\\n12830→    Object.assign(this, { type: 'dynamic', shape: 'circle', x: 0, y: 0, angle: 0, vx: 0, vy: 0, spin: 0, r: 0, bounce: .3, friction: .4, damping: 0, layer: 1, mask: 0xffff, sensor: false, bullet: false, off: false, kick: 0, gravity: 1, maxSpeed: 0, data: null }, d);\\n    /** world: its world; id: its number (the order added); sleeping: at rest, skipped until something wakes it; held: the\\n     *  kicker holding it ({ k, t }), or null; to: a kinematic body's goal ({ x, y, angle, speed, turn }), or null */\\n    this.world = world; this.id = world._id++; this.sleeping = false; this.held = null; this.to = this.to || null;\\n    const s = this.shape;\\n    /** local: the core's points round x, y (a box's corners, a polygon's, a segment's two ends), or null (a circle) */\\n    this.local = s === 'box' ? [[-d.w / 2, -d.h / 2], [d.w / 2, -d.h / 2], [d.w / 2, d.h / 2], [-d.w / 2, d.h / 2]] : s === 'poly' ? wind(d.points) : s === 'segment' ? [d.a.slice(), d.b.slice()] : null;\\n    if (s === 'segment' && !d.x && !d.y) { this.x = (d.a[0] + d.b[0]) / 2; this.y = (d.a[1] + d.b[1]) / 2; this.local = [[d.a[0] - this.x, d.a[1] - this.y], [d.b[0] - this.x, d.b[1] - this.y]]; }\\n    // mass and the turning inertia (dynamic bodies only; the others never yield)\\n    if (this.type === 'dynamic') {\",\"content_concise\":\"12790→  world.remove(b); world.wake(); world.bodies (in the order added); world.contacts (the last substep's: { a, b, nx, ny,\\n  pts }); world.hash() (a proof's number); world.draw(g, to,\\n  color) outlines every body (to(x, y) -> [sx, sy]: your plane on the screen).\\n\\n```js physics\\nconst E = My3D2dge, world = new E.Physics({ gravity: [0, 600] });\\nworld.chain([[0, 0], [0, 200], [160, 200], [160, 0]]);                    // a box open at the top\\nconst L = world.flipper(40, 170, { side: 'left' }), ball = world.circle(64, 150, 5, { bullet: true, bounce: .4 });\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 300 && !world.contacts.some(c => c.a === L || c.b === L); i++) world.step(1 / 120);   // it lands on it\\nL.flip(true); for (let i = 0; i < 12; i++) world.step(1 / 120);\\n12800→if (!(ball.vy < -100)) throw new Error('the flipper fires it up');\\nconst shot = world.circle(80, 100, 4, { bullet: true, vx: 9000 }); for (let i = 0; i < 30; i++) world.step(1 / 120);\\nif (shot.x > 160) throw new Error('never through the wall');\\n```\\nRules: step it from the game's fixed step only (the same steps give the same world); a fast ball is a bullet; keep\\npolygons convex (a concave shape is several bodies or a chain); give a table's walls a little thickness (r: 1) where\\nballs press hard; draw your own art at the bodies' places (draw() is for checking).\\n*/\\n(function (E) {\\n'use strict';\\n12810→if (!E) throw new Error('my-3D2dge module PHYSICS: load engine/my-3d2dge.js (or the agent edition) first');\\n\\n/* =============================================================================\\n * PHYSICS  2D rigid bodies: a core (a point, a segment or a convex polygon) with a radius; sequential impulses in a\\n * fixed order; swept circles for fast balls; sensors, layers, sleep, kinematic bodies, a pinball's parts\\n * ========================================================================== */\\nconst hyp = Math.hypot, clamp = (v, a, b) => v < a ? a : v > b ? b : v, MARGIN = 1, SLOP = .3;\\n// the points wound one way (a positive area), so each edge's outward normal is (dy, -dx)\\nconst wind = P => { let a = 0; for (let i = 0; i < P.length; i++) { const p = P[i], q = P[(i + 1) % P.length]; a += p[0] * q[1] - q[0] * p[1]; } return (a < 0 ? P.slice().reverse() : P.slice()).map(p => [p[0], p[1]]); };\\n// a polygon's area, centroid and second moment of area about its centroid\\n12820→function polyMass(P) {\\n  let A = 0, cx = 0, cy = 0, I = 0;\\n  for (let i = 0; i < P.length; i++) { const [x1, y1] = P[i], [x2, y2] = P[(i + 1) % P.length], c = x1 * y2 - x2 * y1; A += c / 2; cx += (x1 + x2) * c / 6; cy += (y1 + y2) * c / 6; I += c / 12 * (x1 * x1 + x1 * x2 + x2 * x2 + y1 * y1 + y1 * y2 + y2 * y2); }\\n  if (Math.abs(A) < 1e-9) return null;\\n  cx /= A; cy /= A; return { A, cx, cy, I: I - A * (cx * cx + cy * cy) };\\n}\\n\\nclass PBody {\\n  /** made by world.add (and its shorthands): see the manual */\\n  constructor(world, d) {\\n12830→    Object.assign(this, { type: 'dynamic', shape: 'circle', x: 0, y: 0, angle: 0, vx: 0, vy: 0, spin: 0, r: 0, bounce: .3, friction: .4, damping: 0, layer: 1, mask: 0xffff, sensor: false, bullet: false, off: false, kick: 0, gravity: 1, maxSpeed: 0, data: null }, d);\\n    /** world: its world; id: its number (the order added); sleeping: at rest, skipped until something wakes it; held: the\\n     *  kicker holding it ({ k, t }), or null; to: a kinematic body's goal ({ x, y, angle, speed, turn }), or null */\\n    this.world = world; this.id = world._id++; this.sleeping = false; this.held = null; this.to = this.to || null;\\n    const s = this.shape;\\n    /** local: the core's points round x, y (a box's corners, a polygon's, a segment's two ends), or null (a circle) */\\n    this.local = s === 'box' ? [[-d.w / 2, -d.h / 2], [d.w / 2, -d.h / 2], [d.w / 2, d.h / 2], [-d.w / 2, d.h / 2]] : s === 'poly' ? wind(d.points) : s === 'segment' ? [d.a.slice(), d.b.slice()] : null;\\n    if (s === 'segment' && !d.x && !d.y) { this.x = (d.a[0] + d.b[0]) / 2; this.y = (d.a[1] + d.b[1]) / 2; this.local = [[d.a[0] - this.x, d.a[1] - this.y], [d.b[0] - this.x, d.b[1] - this.y]]; }\\n    // mass and the turning inertia (dynamic bodies only; the others never yield)\\n    if (this.type === 'dynamic') {\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js\",\"offset\":12790,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"  world.remove(b); world.wake(); world.bodies (in the order added); world.contacts (the last substep's: { a, b, nx, ny,\\n  pts }); world.hash() (a proof's number); world.draw(g, to,\\n  color) outlines every body (to(x, y) -> [sx, sy]: your plane on the screen).\\n\\n```js physics\\nconst E = My3D2dge, world = new E.Physics({ gravity: [0, 600] });\\nworld.chain([[0, 0], [0, 200], [160, 200], [160, 0]]);                    // a box open at the top\\nconst L = world.flipper(40, 170, { side: 'left' }), ball = world.circle(64, 150, 5, { bullet: true, bounce: .4 });\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 300 && !world.contacts.some(c => c.a === L || c.b === L); i++) world.step(1 / 120);   // it lands on it\\nL.flip(true); for (let i = 0; i < 12; i++) world.step(1 / 120);\\nif (!(ball.vy < -100)) throw new Error('the flipper fires it up');\\nconst shot = world.circle(80, 100, 4, { bullet: true, vx: 9000 }); for (let i = 0; i < 30; i++) world.step(1 / 120);\\nif (shot.x > 160) throw new Error('never through the wall');\\n```\\nRules: step it from the game's fixed step only (the same steps give the same world); a fast ball is a bullet; keep\\npolygons convex (a concave shape is several bodies or a chain); give a table's walls a little thickness (r: 1) where\\nballs press hard; draw your own art at the bodies' places (draw() is for checking).\\n*/\\n(function (E) {\\n'use strict';\\nif (!E) throw new Error('my-3D2dge module PHYSICS: load engine/my-3d2dge.js (or the agent edition) first');\\n\\n/* =============================================================================\\n * PHYSICS  2D rigid bodies: a core (a point, a segment or a convex polygon) with a radius; sequential impulses in a\\n * fixed order; swept circles for fast balls; sensors, layers, sleep, kinematic bodies, a pinball's parts\\n * ========================================================================== */\\nconst hyp = Math.hypot, clamp = (v, a, b) => v < a ? a : v > b ? b : v, MARGIN = 1, SLOP = .3;\\n// the points wound one way (a positive area), so each edge's outward normal is (dy, -dx)\\nconst wind = P => { let a = 0; for (let i = 0; i < P.length; i++) { const p = P[i], q = P[(i + 1) % P.length]; a += p[0] * q[1] - q[0] * p[1]; } return (a < 0 ? P.slice().reverse() : P.slice()).map(p => [p[0], p[1]]); };\\n// a polygon's area, centroid and second moment of area about its centroid\\nfunction polyMass(P) {\\n  let A = 0, cx = 0, cy = 0, I = 0;\\n  for (let i = 0; i < P.length; i++) { const [x1, y1] = P[i], [x2, y2] = P[(i + 1) % P.length], c = x1 * y2 - x2 * y1; A += c / 2; cx += (x1 + x2) * c / 6; cy += (y1 + y2) * c / 6; I += c / 12 * (x1 * x1 + x1 * x2 + x2 * x2 + y1 * y1 + y1 * y2 + y2 * y2); }\\n  if (Math.abs(A) < 1e-9) return null;\\n  cx /= A; cy /= A; return { A, cx, cy, I: I - A * (cx * cx + cy * cy) };\\n}\\n\\nclass PBody {\\n  /** made by world.add (and its shorthands): see the manual */\\n  constructor(world, d) {\\n    Object.assign(this, { type: 'dynamic', shape: 'circle', x: 0, y: 0, angle: 0, vx: 0, vy: 0, spin: 0, r: 0, bounce: .3, friction: .4, damping: 0, layer: 1, mask: 0xffff, sensor: false, bullet: false, off: false, kick: 0, gravity: 1, maxSpeed: 0, data: null }, d);\\n    /** world: its world; id: its number (the order added); sleeping: at rest, skipped until something wakes it; held: the\\n     *  kicker holding it ({ k, t }), or null; to: a kinematic body's goal ({ x, y, angle, speed, turn }), or null */\\n    this.world = world; this.id = world._id++; this.sleeping = false; this.held = null; this.to = this.to || null;\\n    const s = this.shape;\\n    /** local: the core's points round x, y (a box's corners, a polygon's, a segment's two ends), or null (a circle) */\\n    this.local = s === 'box' ? [[-d.w / 2, -d.h / 2], [d.w / 2, -d.h / 2], [d.w / 2, d.h / 2], [-d.w / 2, d.h / 2]] : s === 'poly' ? wind(d.points) : s === 'segment' ? [d.a.slice(), d.b.slice()] : null;\\n    if (s === 'segment' && !d.x && !d.y) { this.x = (d.a[0] + d.b[0]) / 2; this.y = (d.a[1] + d.b[1]) / 2; this.local = [[d.a[0] - this.x, d.a[1] - this.y], [d.b[0] - this.x, d.b[1] - this.y]]; }\\n    // mass and the turning inertia (dynamic bodies only; the others never yield)\\n    if (this.type === 'dynamic') {\\n\",\"total_lines\":14974}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"f0137f91-0089-46ee-ad7a-a13298f25bbf"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_611","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"World bodies exist. Olives have zero friction.\n\nFriction will be set to 0.04 times trayFric during development mode. It stays at 0.12 otherwise. The rope strength will also switch between dev and default values when dev mode is active.\n\nBalloon rope strength equals the base value plus four times the effort. The default panel setting matches the laundry band test exactly. A test will verify that adjusting ropeS changes the balloon strength when the dev panel is open.\n\nThe dev panel key list is indexed. trayFric sits at index 24 and ropeS at 25.\n\nThe dev panel lists 29 keys total. The test will navigate to trayFric and ropeS separately, then advance into play to verify the adjusted values.\n\nA per-seat class scoring design is being drafted. Each seat will score independently while keeping the single-seat path byte-identical.\n\nA helper `eachSeat` will run a function for every seat. Single-player and class sessions with one local seat will behave exactly as before. A regular seat is a bot with scoring disabled, so stepping its balance does not alter the local seat's family state if accumulators are isolated. `stepHold` writes session percentage from seat 0 only, so other seats can be stepped safely without side effects.\n\n`stepHold` now steps every seat's balance independently. Each seat stores its own percentage in `q.pct`. The class finish logic will read those values directly.\n\nEach seat's percentage is computed as steadiness minus five percent of falls, clamped between zero and one. The session percentage is copied from seat zero's result, preserving the original single-seat path exactly. The eyes check remains in the same order because seat zero steps first with identical inputs. Refactoring `stepRep` is harder because it depends on shared family error and score accumulators.\n\nShared family bead state will be updated once from seat zero. Fatigue currently mutates only the local balance set, so the plan is to apply the same mutation to every seat once its doneReps reaches sixty percent. The thought ends with a question about whether to drive all seats in stepRep.\n\nA bug was found in the current class scoring path. `stepFamily` drives all seats before the bead updates, so extra seats are driven with the old bead. Only seat zero is driven again afterward with the fresh bead. The plan is to re-drive every seat inside `stepRep` so each one uses the updated bead.\n\nRe-driving every seat in `stepRep` is safe. Seat zero already receives two drives, so the second call overwrites the first with the same inputs. Other seats will also be driven once more there, correcting the old-bead bug without changing the single-seat path.\n\nSeat zero keeps the exact existing scoring logic and copies its percentage into the session field. Other seats in class mode will store their own replica of the scoring state. Fatigue will only mutate the local balance set when that seat reaches sixty percent done reps, leaving the single-player path untouched. The extra-rep bonus that increases the shared planned count and session limit is also noted.\n\n`stepHoop` is being refactored for class mode. Shared family state will remain on seat zero. Each other seat will store its own hoop state. The function will branch to a per-seat helper when multiple seats are present.\n\nA critical flaw was found in the per-seat hoop plan. `sess.rng()` is shared across seats, so if other seats call it during `stepHoop`, seat zero's random sequence shifts and the toss timing becomes non-deterministic. This breaks the single-seat path byte-identical requirement.\n\nSeat zero will keep the exact same random sequence by calling the shared `sess.rng`. Other seats will use their own seeded RNG derived from `sess.seed + seat index`. The loop will step seat zero first, then each remaining seat with its own sequence. This keeps the single-seat path deterministic while letting class seats run independently.\n\nFor `stepCandle`, flame state will remain on the family object for seat zero, and each extra seat will store its own per-seat flame state.\n\nThe thought timer will follow the same seat-zero-first pattern, using shared RNG for seat zero and independent state and RNG for the rest.\n\n`stepSun` will keep match counters on the family object for seat zero, with each extra seat holding its own copy.\n\nFor `stepStep`, calling `rh.update()` consumes P1's input presses. If P2's hits are then scored against P1's chart, both seats would share a single score. This needs to be fixed so each seat scores independently.\n\nA `startStep` helper will assign seat zero's rhythm to `sess.fam.rh` and clone it for every other seat in class mode. This gives each seat its own Rhythm object so scores stay independent, while the shared audio clock keeps the song synchronized.\n\nCloning the rhythm object for extra seats is problematic because `update()` still reads `game.input`, so every seat would consume the same keyboard presses. A custom update path for class seats is needed that does not touch the global input state.\n\nThe `stepStep` loop will run seat zero's rhythm normally and call a new `stepSeatRhythm` helper for every extra seat. That helper will judge hits by calling `q.rh.hit(lane)` on P2 presses, and it will check misses manually using the shared audio clock position.\n\nA `freshRhythm` helper will build each extra seat's rhythm from the tier chart and song, setting `started = true` without calling `start()` so the shared audio clock stays synchronized. The miss loop will be extracted into a shared utility so both seat zero and extra seats can reuse it.\n\nNon-local seats in class mode will be skipped during step because they are bots with scoring disabled. Empty mats score zero without stepping.\n\nTwo human players can now play step together. P1 uses half-keyboard controls like KeyA for left, while P2 uses ArrowLeft.\n\nP2's arrow presses now hit P2's rhythm object directly, so both seats score independently. On a stork hold, P2's balance and steadiness diverge from P1's centered state, producing different scores. The class surprise test is being adjusted to leave one local human and one Regular bot; the bot scores zero while the human still plays.\n\nThe finish and podium loop advances by large time steps, but it should still complete within the iteration limit. A two-human class test is being drafted: boot into class mode, confirm Stork Stand without removing extra seats, and verify both players are present.\n\nThe two-human test holds P2 right for three seconds while P1 stays still. It asserts both seats are human and scoring, then checks that seat percentages copy into final scores.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-55630f94-5756-40dc-8513-7991784e42d8-46","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"keysTo","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-220108__x61bd7e/work/Spine Wee-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-220108/engine/my-3d2dge.js","head_limit":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2999,"output_tokens":4230,"cache_read_input_tokens":116352,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa15-0d43-7de2-87b2-754e4acb611c","uuid":"5a34fede-973e-47a8-b9aa-6ab4c40ba306"}
